      "outputText": "Recommendation: a stateless ingest tier writing directly into one ScyllaDB cluster, with the database as the sole authority and no log on the acknowledgement path. Ingest gateways terminate device connections over HTTP/2 or MQTT, validate a batch of 1 to 1,000 readings, and write it into two tables in a Cassandra-class wide-column store replicated three ways across three availability zones. The history table is keyed by device ID plus sequence number, carries a 24-hour per-row TTL, and uses time-window compaction so expired data drops as whole SSTables without tombstone cost. The latest table is keyed by device ID alone, has no TTL, holds a full copy of the payload, and uses leveled compaction so it stays small and cache-resident. Latest monotonicity is enforced by writing that row with the sequence number as the cell timestamp, so the store's last-writer-wins merge discards any lower-sequence write without a read-before-write or a Paxos round. Idempotency comes from the primary key: a retried reading is an identical upsert onto the same row.\n\nEvery request follows one fixed order: history rows at local quorum, then a single latest-row write for the highest accepted sequence, then the acknowledgement. Reconnect storms are handled by admission control rather than a queue, because the devices' own 1,000-reading buffers already hold the backlog and a 429 with jittered Retry-After costs nothing to store. If analytics or alerting consumers appear later, they read a change-data-capture stream into Kafka that is strictly derived from the database and never authoritative. The design scales linearly by adding nodes, since every write and every read touches exactly one partition.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** the ScyllaDB cluster. A reading is accepted only when its history row is quorum-committed, and latest is defined as the highest-sequence row ever merged into the latest table. No gateway memory, cache, or stream is authoritative.\n- **Stable ownership key:** device ID is the partition key for both tables. Sequence number is the clustering key of the history table and serves as idempotency key, ordering, and cursor at once, immune to clock skew. Per-device rate is uniform, so hashed device IDs produce no hot partitions, and a partition holds roughly 2,880 steady rows plus backfill.\n- **Bounded ingest path:** body capped near 1 MB. The service rejects non-increasing sequences within a request, applies a per-device token bucket and a global admission gate keyed on database latency, splits the batch into single-partition unlogged batches of 100 rows with up to 10 in flight, then issues one latest write stamped with the maximum accepted sequence. Steady state costs two single-row quorum writes per reading.\n- **Bounded query path:** latest is one point read of one row. The 24-hour cursor is one single-partition range scan ordered by sequence, paged by the last sequence returned, at most 200 rows per page and about 60 pages for a full day. The window is enforced by accept-time TTL, with an optional accept-time filter for exactness at the boundary.\n- **Bounded retry path:** a retried sequence upserts onto its existing row and is reported as accepted, indistinguishable from first acceptance. Late or out-of-order batches cannot move latest backward because the lower sequence loses the timestamp merge. The response lists accepted ranges, permanently rejected sequences with a reason, and sequences to retry after backoff. A 429 or 503 means nothing was accepted.\n- **Durability boundary:** the ack is sent only after both the history rows and the latest row are confirmed by two of three replicas in distinct zones with a synced commit log. Quorum reads make an acked reading immediately visible on both paths. A crash before the ack means the device retries and both writes reapply idempotently, so no reconciliation job exists.\n- **Permanent components:** the latest table with no TTL and its own payload copy, so a device silent for days still shows its last reading. The device registry holding credentials, provisioning state, and a sequence epoch guarding against any future ID reuse. The ingest and query services, the cluster schema, and backups of the two permanent tables. History rows are the only expiring data.\n\n| Quantity at 10M devices | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Steady ingest | ~333K readings/s, ~333 MB/s |\n| 24h history, raw | ~28.8 TB, ~86 TB at three replicas before compression |\n| Latest table | ~12 GB |\n| Reconnect worst case | 1 MB per request, ~1 TB backlog if 1M devices reconnect at once |\n\nOpen decisions and risks to settle before launch:\n\n- **Never write the latest table with a default timestamp.** One such write or delete would shadow every future sequence-stamped update. Confine writes to a single code path and add a schema test.\n- **Accept time defines the 24-hour window.** Device timestamps are stored for display but not used for retention, so device clock skew cannot expire or extend data.\n- **A retry refreshes that row's TTL.** Treat 24 hours as a minimum retention, not an exact cutoff.\n- **Admission gate tuning is the main scaling unknown.** If field data shows device buffers overflow during regional outages, a non-authoritative log can be inserted under the gateway later without changing the authority or the key design.",
