      "outputText": "**Recommendation.** Run one stateless HTTP deployable for both ingest and query, backed by a single disk-backed, quorum-replicated, owner-partitioned store. The store partitions every row by device ID, so each device's 24-hour history and its \"latest\" head row live on one owner shard. History rows are keyed by device ID plus sequence number, carry a native 24-hour row TTL measured from server acceptance, and are written as deterministic same-key upserts, so retries converge without a dedupe table. The head is a separate row in the same partition with no TTL, updated by one conditional write per accepted batch that applies only when the incoming sequence exceeds the stored one. Late, out-of-order, and retried readings therefore land in history only and can never regress latest. Retention expires in the store itself, and the device's own buffer is the retry queue. There is no broker, worker, scheduler, cache, or second store.\n\nThe given envelope selects a disk-backed store over the memory-resident default. At the 1 KB cap, 24 hours of readings is tens of terabytes per copy, and an ack that must survive for 24 hours needs quorum-durable commit rather than asynchronous replication. Day 1 and the 10-million-device end state use identical component types and differ only in store node count and HTTP replica count. The choice reverses only if measured mean payloads are roughly a hundred bytes or less and the product accepts a sub-second loss window on ack; then hash-tagged Redis or Valkey with the identical key contract is smaller.\n\n| Measure | Value at contract cap |\n|---|---|\n| Aggregate writes | about 333,000 readings per second |\n| Ingest bytes | about 341 MB per second |\n| Retained 24h history, one copy | about 29.5 TB |\n| Per-device partition | 2,880 rows, about 2.9 MB |\n| Max batch body | about 1 MB |\n\n- **Canonical authority:** one wide-column or key-value store of the Cassandra, ScyllaDB, or DynamoDB class with replication factor 3, quorum writes and reads, row TTL, per-partition range scans by sequence, and per-partition conditional writes.\n- **Stable ownership key:** device ID, used as the partition key for history rows and the head row. Never partition by time or sequence.\n- **Bounded ingest path:** one request carries one device and at most 1,000 readings. The deployable validates size and ordering, upserts history rows at quorum, then issues one conditional head write using the batch's maximum sequence. It touches exactly one partition. Store backpressure returns a retry-after response.\n- **Bounded query path:** latest is a single-row quorum read of the head. History is a range scan within one partition ordered by sequence descending with a page limit, the cursor being the smallest sequence returned. Readers filter to acceptance time within the last 24 hours in case TTL purge lags. At most 2,880 rows exist per device.\n- **Bounded retry path:** the device retries the whole batch on any non-success or timeout and trims its buffer to sequences above the returned head. Rewrites are identical rows and the head write is a no-op when not newer, so any interleaving converges.\n- **Durability boundary:** the success response is sent only after every reading in the batch is committed to a quorum of replicas with a durable commit log and the head write has been evaluated. Anything unacknowledged carries no promise. Partially written rows are still valid readings, and the head may lag until the device retries or sends a newer reading. Loss requires two replica failures before repair.\n- **Permanent components:** the stateless HTTP deployable, forced by authentication, validation, batch semantics, and history-before-head ordering that devices cannot be trusted to enforce, and the owner-partitioned store, forced by retained volume, write rate, quorum-durable acks, native TTL, and same-shard head co-location. A queue collapsed because the store absorbs writes directly and the device buffer already holds unacknowledged work. A sweeper collapsed because TTL is native. A latest cache collapsed because the head read is one key.\n- **Preserved unknowns:** whether the 24-hour window is measured by device capture time or server acceptance time, whether readings carry a device timestamp, and the real reconnect burst profile. The design assumes acceptance time and a store with time-window compaction so expiry drops whole segments. A large simultaneous reconnect wave is absorbed by write headroom and retry-after backpressure, not by a broker.",
