      "outputText": "**Recommendation:** a stateless ingest and query gateway in front of one replicated wide-column store, Cassandra-compatible such as ScyllaDB, hash-partitioned on device ID, with two tables and nothing else in the correctness path. The readings table is partitioned by device ID and clustered by sequence number descending, every row carries a 24-hour TTL counted from acceptance, and writes are plain upserts keyed on device ID plus sequence number, so a retried reading rewrites its own row and can never become a second logical reading. The latest table holds one row per device with no TTL, and every write to it uses the device's sequence number as the cell write-timestamp. The store's last-write-wins rule then keeps the highest sequence and silently drops any lower or repeated one, with no read-before-write and no lightweight transaction, and the row survives indefinitely after its history copy expires. The one rule that must hold is that nothing writes or deletes that table with a default timestamp; deprovisioning deletes with a reserved maximum. One device's 24 hours is roughly 2,880 rows and 3 MB, so a single partition per device is safe, and time-window compaction lets expired data drop wholesale.\n\nAn upload request carries readings from exactly one device, at most 1,000. The gateway authenticates the device ID against the registry, writes every reading to both tables at local quorum in chunks of about 50 rows, and acknowledges only when every row in the request is durable; a partial failure returns an error and the device resends. The latest query is one point read on the latest table. The history query is one range read on one partition, ordered by sequence descending, with a fixed page size and the last sequence returned as the cursor; reads use local quorum so an acknowledged reading is immediately visible. Launch on three nodes with this exact schema; reaching 10 million devices, about 330,000 writes per second and about 30 TB of raw history per day before replication, is a matter of adding store nodes and gateway replicas with no structural change. An optional change-data-capture stream feeds analytics and alerting off the acknowledgement path.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** the store's per-device rows. The latest row is the only authority for the current reading, the readings partition is the only authority for history, and the device sequence number is the only ordering authority. No gateway memory, cache, or stream is consulted for correctness.\n- **Stable ownership key:** device ID. Every write, retry, latest read, and history page for a device lands on exactly one partition, and the hot path has no cross-device operation.\n- **Bounded ingest path:** one request, one device, at most 1,000 readings and about 1 MB, one partition, chunked quorum writes, one acknowledgement.\n- **Bounded query path:** latest is one point read. History is one partition, one fixed-size page, one opaque cursor equal to the last sequence seen. Pages are stable because rows are immutable and the TTL enforces the 24-hour window.\n- **Bounded retry path:** no acknowledgement means resend. The server keeps no dedupe state because the readings upsert is idempotent on device ID plus sequence and the latest write is monotone by timestamp. A retry is at worst a rewrite of identical bytes.\n- **Durability boundary:** the acknowledgement. Device buffers, TLS buffers, and gateway memory before it are disposable. CDC, analytics, and any cache after it are optional.\n- **Permanent components:** the device registry and credentials, the latest table for each device's lifetime, the store cluster and its schema, and the gateway as a role. Disposable: readings rows after 24 hours, individual gateway instances, caches, downstream streams.",
