      "outputText": "**Recommended shape.** Run one stateless HTTP deployable that serves both device ingest and user queries, in front of one canonical authority: a replicated, disk-backed, transactional relational store sharded by device ID from day one. Route every device through a fixed table of virtual buckets hashed from the device ID and mapped to physical shards, so launch with a handful of shards and a mature deployment with over a hundred share the same routing and ownership contract. Each shard holds two tables owned by the same device key. The head table has one unexpiring row per device holding the latest accepted sequence and payload. The history table holds one row per reading keyed by device ID and sequence number, with an accepted-at timestamp. An ingest request carries one device and up to a thousand readings and runs as one transaction on that device's shard. It inserts each reading with insert-if-absent on the device and sequence key, upserts the head row only when the batch's highest sequence exceeds the stored one, deletes that device's own history rows accepted more than a day ago, commits with synchronous replication, and only then acknowledges. A retried reading collides on its key and becomes a no-op. A late reading lands in history but cannot lower the head. The head survives history expiry because it lives in a separate unexpiring row written in the same transaction.\n\n**Why nothing else is needed.** Reads are single-shard lookups on the same key. The latest reading is one head-row read, and the day cursor is a keyset scan on device ID and descending sequence with a page limit and an accepted-at floor. No queue or broker is retained because the device's own buffer is the retry buffer, acknowledgement follows durable commit, and dedupe makes retries converge, so an unavailable shard simply returns a retryable error. No cache or projection is retained because every read touches one device's rows on one shard, which is bounded compute-on-read. No expiry sweeper is retained because each device trims its own history on write, so a silent device holds at most its last day of readings, which stays inside the storage ceiling. A memory-resident Redis or Valkey authority was rejected on the derived envelope, since worst-case history is near 29 TB per copy and acknowledged-write durability across failover needs the synchronous replication a disk-backed store provides natively.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** a replicated disk-backed relational store, one primary plus one synchronous replica per shard, holding both head and history tables. A Postgres-class store is the architecture-bearing pick because insert-if-absent and a conditional single-statement head upsert run inside one transaction without explicit locks.\n- **Stable ownership key:** device ID, hashed into a fixed bucket count. The bucket-to-shard map is versioned configuration read by every HTTP replica, not a service. User reads follow the same key.\n- **Bounded ingest path:** one request equals one device, at most a thousand readings and roughly one megabyte, one transaction, one shard. Work per request is at most a thousand inserts, one conditional upsert, and one owner-local delete.\n- **Bounded query path:** latest reads one row. The cursor reads at most one page from a per-device history capped near 2,880 rows.\n- **Retry path:** the device resends the same batch. Duplicate keys are ignored, the head only rises, and the whole batch either commits or rolls back, so a partial failure is retried whole and converges.\n- **Durability boundary:** a reading is accepted only after the commit is fsynced on the primary and confirmed by the synchronous replica. Anything unacknowledged is not owed to the user and must be retried by the device. Failover promotes the replica with no acknowledged loss.\n- **Permanent components:** stateless HTTP replicas scaled by count, sharded replicated relational authority scaled by bucket reassignment, versioned bucket map. Day one and final differ only in replica and shard counts.\n- **Capacity envelope from stated facts:** about 333K writes per second, about 10 GB of head state, at most about 28.8 TB of history per copy if every reading is a full kilobyte. At 64 shards that is roughly 5K writes per second and 450 GB per shard.\n- **Top failures and corrections:** a fleet-wide reconnect storm can offer up to 10 TB in one wave, so each shard enforces an admission limit and returns retry-after while devices keep buffering. Moving a bucket to a new shard briefly rejects that bucket's writes with retry-after until the map version flips, and dedupe absorbs the retries. Delete-on-write churn can bloat history tables, and if measured vacuum lag exceeds tolerance the history table moves to hourly partitions dropped after a day with dedupe done by a per-device existence check under a head-row lock.\n- **Assumptions preserved as unknowns:** the day window is measured from accepted-at, late readings below the head are accepted into history, device and user authentication are unspecified, and actual payload sizes are unknown so the one-kilobyte ceiling sized everything.",
