      "outputText": "## Recommended architecture\n\nAt steady state this is ~333K readings/sec (10M devices ÷ 30s) at ≤1 KB each — roughly 330 MB/s — which fits a partitioned log in front of a wide-column store. Stateless HTTPS gateways accept single readings or reconnect batches (≤1,000 readings ≤1 MB per request), validate shape, and append each reading to a Kafka topic partitioned by hash(device_id) with acks=all; the client is acknowledged only after that replicated append succeeds. One consumer group applies the log to ScyllaDB/Cassandra: a **history** table keyed `(device_id, seq DESC)` with a 24-hour TTL, and a **latest** table with one non-expiring row per device holding `(seq, payload, high_watermark)`. Because sequence numbers are strictly increasing and never reused, every write is a natural idempotency key: retried or duplicated readings upsert the same `(device_id, seq)` history row (a no-op), and the latest row is updated only when the incoming `seq` exceeds the stored one — since each device's readings all flow through one log partition and thus one consumer, this compare-and-set needs no distributed coordination. Late arrivals land in history at their sequence position but never regress latest.\n\nReads never touch the log. \"Get latest\" is a single-row point read from the latest table, which survives history TTL expiry because it is only ever *replaced* by a higher-seq reading, never deleted. Cursoring 24 hours of one device is a clustering-range scan on `(device_id)` paginated by `seq` as the cursor token — bounded at 2,880 rows per device, so pages are cheap and predictable. Scaling from launch to 10M devices is done by adding Kafka partitions and Scylla nodes; the device-hash partitioning key never changes, so no data model migration is required. Optionally, batches carrying seqs at or below the device's stored high watermark are dropped at apply time, cheaply absorbing full 1,000-reading replay storms.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** the latest table's per-device row — its `seq` is the accepted high watermark; a reading is \"latest\" iff its seq is the maximum ever applied for that device.\n- **Stable ownership key:** `device_id` (hashed to a log partition), guaranteeing all of a device's readings serialize through exactly one consumer, making monotonic latest updates and dedup race-free.\n- **Bounded ingest path:** ≤1 MB per request (1,000 × 1 KB), fixed per-partition throughput, idempotent upserts keyed by `(device_id, seq)`.\n- **Bounded query path:** latest = one point read; history = seq-cursored range scan capped at 2,880 rows/device/24h.\n- **Bounded retry path:** retries and buffered replays re-upsert identical keys (no-ops) and are gated by the high watermark, so worst-case retry cost is one batch apply with zero logical duplicates and no latest regression.\n- **Durability boundary:** the replicated Kafka append (acks=all). Acknowledgment is issued only past this line; everything downstream is deterministic, idempotent replay, so acked readings always become and stay queryable for their 24 hours.\n- **Permanent components:** the latest table (one small row per device, no TTL), the partition/ownership mapping, and device identity itself. History rows (24h TTL) and log segments (short retention) are transient by design.",
