      "outputText": "**Recommendation.** One stateless HTTP deployable in front of one replicated, disk-backed row store sharded by `device_id` — nothing else. Each accepted reading is an idempotent upsert keyed by `(device_id, seq)` with a 24-hour TTL, and each device additionally owns one un-expiring \"latest\" row in the same shard, advanced by a conditional write only when the incoming sequence number is greater than the stored one. A batch upload of up to 1,000 readings is one single-shard atomic write: upsert all rows, then one monotonic compare-and-set of the latest head; the acknowledgment is returned only after the write is quorum-replicated to disk. Retrying the whole batch replays the same keyed upserts and the same guarded CAS, so it converges without a dedupe ledger, and late or out-of-order readings land in history but can never move latest backward. Reads are equally bounded: latest is a single-row get, and the 24-hour cursor is a paginated range scan over `(device_id, seq)` within one partition (at most 2,880 rows per device). The storage class is selected by the envelope, not fashion: 10M devices × 1 reading/30 s ≈ 333 K writes/s and 10M × 2,880 × 1 KB ≈ 29 TB of live data (≈87 TB at replication 3), which rules out a memory-resident authority and calls for an LSM/wide-row store with native row TTL and per-partition ordered scans (Cassandra/Scylla-class); sharded relational storage would need hand-rolled TTL sweeping at this write rate.\n\nDay 1 and vFinal are the same graph — stateless replicas plus owner-sharded storage — differing only in counts: launch runs a handful of shards, 10M devices runs ~100–200, each seeing a few thousand writes/s since per-device load is fixed at one write per 30 seconds with no hot-entity skew. No queue, worker, cache, or broker earns rent: ingest is synchronous and bounded (≤1 MB per batch, one shard touched), the cursor is bounded compute-on-read, and expiry is the store's native TTL, so every collapse test deletes those planes. The one non-obvious invariant is that latest must not be TTL'd with history — it is a separate monotonic head row in the same shard, kept until replaced.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** one replicated, disk-backed wide-row store; history rows `(device_id, seq) → reading` with 24 h TTL, plus one persistent `latest(device_id) → {seq, reading}` row per device. No derived stores or projections.\n- **Stable ownership key:** `device_id`, from day 1 — every ingest, retry, and query touches exactly one shard; scaling is shard count only.\n- **Bounded ingest/retry path:** synchronous HTTP; batch ≤ 1,000 readings ≤ ~1 MB; single-shard atomic upsert-all + seq-guarded CAS of latest; retries replay the same keys and converge (idempotent by construction, no request-ID ledger needed because `seq` is never reused).\n- **Bounded query path:** latest = one-row get; history = paginated range scan on `(device_id, seq)` capped at 2,880 rows/device; both single-shard.\n- **Durability boundary:** the acknowledgment. Ack is sent only after quorum-replicated disk persistence of the batch and latest update; anything unacked may be retried safely.\n- **Permanent components:** exactly two — the stateless HTTP tier and the sharded storage authority. vFinal changes replica and shard counts, never the topology.\n- **Revisit triggers:** retention growing well beyond 24 h or readings growing well beyond 1 KB (storage class re-check), or a future requirement for cross-device fleet queries, which would force a derived index this per-device shape deliberately omits.",
