      "outputText": "**Recommendation.** Use a partitioned log + keyed store design. Devices upload over HTTPS to stateless ingest nodes, which write each batch to Kafka partitioned by `device_id` (hash), so all readings for a device land on one partition in arrival order. A consumer group applies each reading idempotently to two Cassandra/Scylla tables: a **history table** keyed by `(device_id, seq)` with a 24-hour TTL, and a **latest table** keyed by `device_id` with **no TTL**, updated only when the incoming `seq` is greater than the stored one (conditional/LWT or single-writer-per-partition compare-and-set in the consumer). Because sequence numbers are strictly increasing and never reused, `(device_id, seq)` is the natural idempotency key: retried readings overwrite themselves harmlessly in history and are ignored by the monotonic latest update, so duplicates and out-of-order arrivals can never move latest backward. Batch uploads of up to 1,000 readings are one Kafka produce (~1 MB, within default limits); the device is acked only after the broker confirms the write, making the log the durability boundary — anything acked is guaranteed to become queryable. Reads never touch Kafka: latest is a single-key point read, and cursoring is a range scan on `(device_id, seq)` descending with `seq` as the opaque cursor.\n\nScale math: 10M devices × 1 reading/30s ≈ 333K writes/s and ~333 MB/s — comfortably handled by a few hundred Kafka partitions and a modest Cassandra cluster; history storage is bounded at ~2,880 readings × 1 KB × 10M ≈ 29 TB rolling, pruned automatically by TTL.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** the Kafka log (acked-produce is the source of truth); the databases are derived, idempotently rebuildable views.\n- **Stable ownership key:** `device_id` — hash-partitions the log and both tables, giving per-device ordering and single-owner conditional updates.\n- **Bounded ingest path:** ≤1,000 readings × 1 KB = ≤1 MB per request → one produce → ack; per-device rate is fixed, so partition load is predictable.\n- **Bounded query path:** latest = one point read on the latest table; history = seq-range pagination limited to a 24-hour window (~2,880 rows max per device).\n- **Bounded retry path:** device retries the same batch; `(device_id, seq)` idempotency plus monotonic latest CAS makes replays no-ops — no dedup state beyond the rows themselves.\n- **Durability boundary:** client ack is issued only after replicated Kafka commit (acks=all); consumer offsets advance only after DB write, so nothing acked can be lost.\n- **Permanent components:** the latest table (per-device, never expires until superseded); everything else — history rows (24 h TTL) and log segments (short retention) — is ephemeral.",
