      "outputText": "**Recommendation.** One stateless HTTP deployable in front of a Redis/Valkey cluster (disk-backed: AOF everysec + replication, no eviction), sharded by `device_id` hash tag from day 1. Ingest is a single device-local atomic operation (Redis function/Lua on the device's shard): for each reading in a batch (≤1,000 × ≤1 KB ≈ 1 MB, well within bounds), insert into a per-device sorted set `hist:{device_id}` keyed by sequence number — `ZADD NX` makes retries converge without duplicates since sequence numbers are never reused — and update `latest:{device_id}` only if the incoming sequence exceeds the stored one, so late/out-of-order arrivals never move latest backward. The latest key is a separate hash with no TTL, so it survives its history copy's expiry until a strictly newer reading replaces it. History trimming is a bounded per-device `ZREMRANGEBYSCORE`/timestamp cleanup run opportunistically on write (each device writes every 30 s, so trimming piggybacks on ingest; no scheduler or worker). Queries are device-local: latest is one GET; cursoring 24 h of one device is `ZRANGEBYSCORE` with limit over at most 2,880 entries (~3 MB per device), intrinsically bounded.\n\nAt 10 M devices this is ~333 K writes/s and ~10 GB × replication of hot state, distributed evenly by device hash across shards; vFinal is the same graph with more shards and more stateless replicas — no new component types. There is no queue, broker, worker fleet, projection, second store, or coordination service: the batch fits one request, dedup and monotonicity live in one atomic owner-shard operation, and reads hit the same authority that acknowledged the write. The acknowledgment boundary is the durability boundary: the API returns success only after the shard write (fsync-bounded by AOF everysec + a synchronous replica ack if the contract demands zero acknowledged loss on single-node failure).\n\n- **Canonical authority:** the sharded, replicated, disk-backed Redis/Valkey cluster — it is the system of record for both the 24 h history and latest; no derived stores.\n- **Stable ownership key:** `device_id` hash tag; every key for a device (`hist:{id}`, `latest:{id}`, `maxseq:{id}`) lives on one shard, same routing day 1 and at 10 M.\n- **Bounded ingest path:** one HTTP request → one atomic shard-local batch apply, ≤1,000 readings/≤1 MB per call; idempotent via seq-keyed `ZADD NX` + monotonic latest compare-and-set.\n- **Bounded query paths:** latest = one key read; 24 h cursor = range scan over ≤2,880 device-local entries with a seq-number cursor.\n- **Retry contract:** device retries the whole batch with the same sequence numbers; the operation is a deterministic same-key upsert, so replays converge and return the same ack.\n- **Durability boundary:** ack after owner-shard persistence (AOF) and replica acknowledgment; on shard failover the replica has every acked reading, and latest never regresses because updates are seq-guarded.\n- **Permanent components (all of them):** stateless HTTP tier + sharded Redis/Valkey authority. Capacity scales only by adding shards/replicas. Revisit trigger: if the 24 h retention contract grows to long-term analytical history, add cold archival storage then — not now.",
