      "outputText": "**Recommendation.** One horizontally scalable stateless HTTP tier in front of one replicated, disk-backed key-ordered store (an LSM/row store) sharded by `hash(device_id)` from day 1. Each shard holds two things per device: a **history keyspace** keyed `(device_id, seq)` holding the raw reading with its ingest timestamp, and a single **latest row** keyed `device_id` holding `(seq, payload)` with no expiry. Ingest — single reading or a reconnect batch of up to 1,000 — is one atomic transaction on the device's owner shard: upsert each reading at `(device_id, seq)` (a retry writes the same key with the same bytes, so duplicates converge rather than needing an arbitration ledger), and update the latest row only when the incoming `seq` exceeds the stored one, which makes latest monotonic under any out-of-order arrival. The device's strictly increasing, never-reused sequence number is the idempotency key for free; no separate dedupe table exists. History older than 24 hours is dropped by TTL/compaction on the history keyspace only; because latest lives in its own non-expiring row, it survives its history copy's expiry until a newer reading replaces it. Reads are trivial: latest is a point read; the 24-hour cursor is a paginated range scan over `(device_id, seq)` bounded to the retention window — both single-shard, single-owner operations.\n\nThe envelope proves this needs no second plane. 10M devices at one 1 KB reading per 30 s is ~333K writes/s aggregate — across, say, 128 shards that is ~2.6K writes/s per shard, comfortable for a disk-backed store; the working set is bounded at 2,880 readings ≈ 3 MB per device (~29 TB total), which forces disk over RAM but nothing else. Every path is intrinsically bounded (≤1,000 readings per request, ≤2,880 rows per cursor, one owner shard per action), so there is no queue, broker, worker fleet, cache, projection, or stream processor: ingest is synchronous (device gets its ack only after the transaction commits, so \"acknowledged\" and \"durable/queryable\" are the same event), and growth from launch to 10M is shard count and replica count only — the day-1 and vFinal graphs are identical component types.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** the sharded, replicated disk-backed store; history rows `(device_id, seq)` plus one non-expiring latest row per device. Nothing is derived from it.\n- **Ownership key:** `device_id` — routing, transactions, dedupe, latest, and reads all resolve to that device's shard; no cross-shard operation exists.\n- **Bounded ingest/retry path:** synchronous HTTP → one owner-shard transaction; ≤1,000 × 1 KB per request (~1 MB); retries are same-key upserts and converge; latest updates only on `seq > stored_seq`, so late arrivals never move it backward.\n- **Bounded query path:** latest = one point read; history = range scan on `(device_id, seq)` capped at 2,880 rows, cursor = last seq returned.\n- **Durability boundary:** the ack is sent after the replicated commit; anything unacked is still in the device's local buffer and will be re-uploaded, so no reading exists only in flight.\n- **Permanent components:** exactly two — the stateless HTTP deployable and the sharded store. Scaling is adding shards/replicas within the same routing contract. Revisit trigger: a single device's measured load exceeding one shard (contract says it cannot — 1 reading/30 s), or a retention requirement beyond 24 h changing the storage envelope.",
