      "outputText": "**Recommended architecture.** One stateless HTTP deployable (ingest + query in the same replicas) in front of one canonical, disk-backed, synchronously replicated row store, hash-sharded by `device_id` from day 1. Each device owns two things on its shard: a time-partitioned **history table** keyed `(device_id, seq)` holding 24 hours of readings, and a permanent **head row** holding the latest accepted reading and its sequence number, with no expiry. An upload (1 reading or a reconnect batch of up to 1,000) is a single transaction on the owner shard: `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT (device_id, seq) DO NOTHING` into history, plus a conditional head update that applies only when the batch's max seq exceeds the stored head seq. Because sequence numbers are strictly increasing and never reused, a duplicate key is by definition the same logical reading, so retries converge with no compare-and-set ledger, and the conditional head update makes late or out-of-order arrivals unable to move latest backward. The ack is returned only after the transaction commits and is replicated, which is the entire durability guarantee. Expiry is an hourly `DROP PARTITION` on history per shard (executed by any app replica leasing a per-shard maintenance row in the store itself); it never touches head rows, so the latest reading survives its history copy's expiry until a newer reading replaces it.\n\n**Scale function.** The envelope is fully derivable: 10M devices × 1 reading/30 s ≈ 333K rows/s and ~333 MB/s inbound; 24 h retention ≈ 2,880 rows (~2.9 MB) per device, ~29 TB total. That working set and write-once/read-rare access pattern is why the authority is disk-backed rather than a memory-resident Redis/Valkey plane — the 29 TB RAM cost is the measured requirement that breaks the in-memory default; the shape is otherwise identical. Pre-split the hash space into ~1,024 logical partitions day 1 and map them to physical shards: launch might run 4 shards, vFinal runs ~64–128 (each carrying ~3–5K rows/s and 250–500 GB — comfortably one commodity node plus replica). Growth is only partition reassignment; routing, ownership, transaction boundary, and failure contract never change. No queue, broker, worker tier, cache, or stream is needed: ingest is synchronous and single-shard, latest is one point read, and a 24 h cursor is a keyset-paginated range scan bounded at 2,880 rows.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** one replicated disk-backed OLTP row store (e.g., partitioned Postgres per shard), holding both history and the head row; nothing else is authoritative or derived.\n- **Stable ownership key:** `hash(device_id)` → logical partition → shard; every operation for a device touches exactly one shard, day 1 through vFinal.\n- **Bounded ingest path:** ≤1,000 readings (~1 MB) per request, one owner-shard transaction: idempotent history insert + monotonic head update; reconnect storms are absorbed by device-side buffering plus jittered backoff, since unacked data is safe on the device.\n- **Bounded query paths:** latest = one point read of the head row; 24 h cursor = keyset pagination on `(device_id, seq)`, ≤2,880 rows total per device, page size capped (~500).\n- **Bounded retry path:** re-sending any batch re-executes the same-key insert-ignore and the same conditional head update; the result converges with no duplicates and no arbitration state.\n- **Durability boundary:** ack ⇔ committed and synchronously replicated to at least one replica on the owner shard; on primary loss, promote the replica — anything unacked is still buffered on the device and retried idempotently, so no reconciliation machinery exists.\n- **Permanent components:** stateless HTTP replicas and the sharded store — that is the whole graph at launch and at 10M devices; the hourly partition drop is a leased task inside existing replicas, not a scheduler or worker deployment.\n\n**Top failure to watch:** a fleet-wide reconnect after a regional outage turns steady 333K rows/s into a burst of 1,000-row batches. The correction is admission control per shard (reject with retry-after; devices hold data), not a queue — buffering already lives at the edge, and adding a broker would only duplicate it. Revisit the shard count, not the topology, if sustained per-shard ingest approaches the measured single-node commit ceiling.",
