      "outputText": "## Recommended architecture\n\nRun a stateless ingest tier in front of a single partitioned LSM store: anycast L4 → stateless gRPC/HTTP2 gateways (autoscaled, no per-device affinity, no local state) → ScyllaDB (Cassandra-compatible) with RF=3 and LOCAL_QUORUM writes. The gateway authenticates the device, validates the batch (≤1,000 readings, ≤1 KB each, ≤2 MB body), collapses duplicate sequence numbers inside the batch, and issues two writes per reading, both to the same `device_id` partition: an idempotent upsert into `readings(device_id PK, seq CK DESC, payload)` with `TTL 24h` under TWCS with 1-hour windows, and an upsert into `latest(device_id PK, seq, payload)` with **no TTL**. At 10M devices the steady state is ~333k readings/s, ~28.8B live rows, ~29 TB logical / ~90 TB replicated, which is roughly 60–80 storage-optimized Scylla nodes; at launch the identical schema runs on three nodes and scales by adding nodes, since `device_id` hashing spreads load uniformly and the largest partition is bounded at 2,880 rows (~2.8 MB).\n\nMonotonicity and dedupe need no coordination, no locks, and no Paxos/LWT — which is what makes this shape hold at 333k writes/s. Because `(device_id, seq)` is the full primary key of `readings`, a retry rewrites the identical cell and is a no-op, so duplicate logical readings are structurally impossible. For `latest`, the write is issued as `USING TIMESTAMP <seq>` (offset into the int64 microsecond domain), turning the store's own last-write-wins cell resolution into a per-device monotonic guard: a late or out-of-order reading with a lower `seq` carries a lower cell timestamp and is discarded by the database itself, on every replica, in any order, forever. That table is never deleted from and never TTL'd, so the last accepted reading outlives the expiry of its history copy and is replaced only by a strictly higher `seq`. Reads are single-partition: `GET /devices/{id}/latest` is one point read of `latest`; `GET /devices/{id}/readings` is a bounded descending slice of `readings` with an opaque cursor encoding the last-returned `seq`, page size capped at 500.\n\n- **Canonical authority** — the ScyllaDB cluster; specifically `readings` for the 24-hour window and `latest` for the current value. No cache, queue, or gateway holds authoritative state; caches (if added) are read-through with ≤5s TTL and never serve a value newer than the store.\n- **Stable ownership key** — `device_id` alone is the partition key for both tables; `seq` is the clustering key and the sole idempotency and ordering token. Ownership is by consistent hash of `device_id`, so it survives gateway restarts, rebalances, and node additions without remapping any device's data.\n- **Bounded ingest path** — batch ≤1,000 readings / ≤2 MB, per-device token bucket (sustained 2 readings/min, burst 1,000/reconnect), global admission control shedding with `429` + jittered `Retry-After`; all writes in a request target one partition, so a batch is one coordinator round trip.\n- **Bounded query path** — every read is single-partition and cursor-paginated (≤500 rows, ≤1 MB per page); no cross-device scans, no aggregations, no unbounded time ranges — the 24-hour bound is enforced by TTL *and* by an explicit lower bound in the query.\n- **Bounded retry path** — retries are safe by construction (same `(device_id, seq)` → same cell), so clients retry the whole batch with exponential backoff plus full jitter, capped at 5 attempts and a 60s ceiling; the server keeps no retry ledger and needs no dedupe window.\n- **Durability boundary** — the `200 OK` is returned only after both writes reach LOCAL_QUORUM (2 of 3 replicas, commitlog-synced). Nothing is acknowledged from memory or from a gateway buffer; anything acknowledged is queryable immediately and for the full 24 hours. Partial batch failure returns `207` with the per-`seq` outcome so the device retries only the unacknowledged tail.\n- **Permanent components** — the `latest` table (unbounded lifetime, ~10M rows, a few GB), the device registry and credential store, the schema/config, and operational metrics. Everything else — `readings` rows, cursors, gateway memory — is bounded and self-expiring, so total footprint converges rather than growing with time.",
