      "outputText": "## Recommendation\n\nRun one stateless HTTP deployable in front of one owner-sharded, replicated, disk-backed keyed store as the single canonical authority, partitioned by `device_id` on day 1 and at 10M devices with identical routing. Each device owns exactly one partition holding two kinds of rows: TTL'd history rows clustered by `seq`, and one non-expiring head row at a fixed clustering slot. Ingest is one bounded write set per request: for each reading in the batch, an insert of `(device_id, seq) -> ts, payload` with `TTL = 24h`, plus a single write of the head row **using `seq` as the write timestamp**. Primary-key identity makes retries converge on the same logical row rather than a duplicate (a retry only re-extends that row's TTL, never shortens it), and the storage engine's last-write-wins on client-supplied timestamps makes the head monotonic with no compare-and-set, no lock, and no read-before-write: a late reading with a lower `seq` is discarded by the engine itself and can never move latest backward. History expiry is engine-native TTL plus compaction, so no sweeper, worker, or retention job exists; the head has no TTL, so latest survives its history copy by construction. Reads are single-partition: latest is a one-row lookup of the head slot, and the 24h cursor is a clustered range scan `seq > cursor LIMIT 500`.\n\nThis needs properties, not a stack: partition-by-owner, clustered range reads inside a partition, per-row TTL, and client-supplied write timestamps for conflict resolution — the Cassandra/Scylla family supplies all four natively, which is why I name it here rather than the Redis/Valkey default. The forcing measure is size, not shape: 10M devices × 2,880 readings × 1 KB ≈ **28.8 TB** of hot window (~86 TB at RF3), which is a disk working set, not a memory one; every other property Redis/Valkey would have given us is preserved. Everything else collapses. There is no queue — nothing is deferred past the ack, since a 1,000-reading upload is ~1 MB into one partition and completes inside the request. There is no cache or projection — latest is one row, not a computed view. There is no dedupe ledger or idempotency table — the primary key is the ledger. There is no separate \"latest\" store or service — the head lives in the device's own partition, under the same key, in the same write. Day 1 is 3 nodes and a few app replicas; vFinal is the same two component types with more of each (roughly 30–40 storage nodes at RF3 for 86 TB and ~667K row-writes/s including head writes), scaling by node count and token reassignment, never by new topology.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** one owner-sharded, replicated, disk-backed keyed store (Scylla/Cassandra family). It is authoritative for both history and latest — no derived copies anywhere.\n- **Stable ownership key:** `device_id`, day 1 and vFinal. Every write and read for a device touches exactly one partition on one shard; aggregate scale spreads bounded devices across shards and never splits a device.\n- **Bounded ingest path:** ≤1,001 rows, ≤~1 MB, one partition, one authority per request. History rows `(device_id, seq) → ts, payload` at `TTL 24h`; head row written `USING TIMESTAMP = seq`, no TTL. Large uploads are chunked into same-partition batches; ack only after all chunks succeed.\n- **Bounded query paths:** latest = one head-row read; history cursor = one clustered range scan, `seq > cursor`, page ≤500 rows (~512 KB), opaque cursor = last `seq` returned. Both are single-partition, O(page), independent of fleet size.\n- **Bounded retry path:** retries are the same primary-key writes. Duplicate `seq` → same logical row (idempotent); lower `seq` at the head → discarded by timestamp resolution (monotonic). A client may safely re-send its entire buffer after a lost ack; a partial-batch failure is repaired by re-sending the batch, not by reconciliation machinery.\n- **Durability boundary:** the ack is returned only after quorum (`LOCAL_QUORUM`, RF3) commit of every row in the request, including the head write. Before that point the client owns the reading and must retry; after it, the reading is queryable for 24h and latest reflects it. Nothing is acknowledged from memory or from an unwritten buffer.\n- **Permanent components:** exactly two — stateless HTTP app replicas, and the sharded replicated store. No broker, queue, worker, scheduler, cache, projection, retention job, or coordination service.\n- **Top failures and corrections:** (1) *Mass reconnect surge* — 10M devices returning at once has no sourced envelope; capacity is proven only for the 333K readings/s steady target. Correction: bounded server-side concurrency with 429 + `Retry-After` and jittered client backoff, which is safe precisely because retries are idempotent — but treat surge capacity as **unproven** until a target burst multiple is supplied. (2) *Seq reset or device re-provisioning* — timestamp-based monotonicity means a device whose `seq` restarts low would freeze its own latest forever. This is an **unknown**: if IDs can be re-provisioned or firmware can reset the counter, the ownership key must become `device_id:epoch` before launch. (3) *Per-device skew* — a device emitting far faster than 1/30s inflates one partition and one shard's write path; bound accepted readings per device per window at the ingest edge.\n- **Revisit triggers:** history retention grows beyond ~24h, or payloads exceed ~1 KB mean (both move the storage/cost curve, not the shape); a required cross-device query or fanout appears (that, and only that, would break single-partition ownership and force a real redesign).",
