      "outputText": "## Recommendation\n\nRun one stateless HTTP deployable (ingest + query in the same replicas, no worker tier) in front of a Redis/Valkey cluster sharded by `{device_id}` hash tag, which is the canonical authority for both history and latest. Every device's data lives in exactly three keys in one slot: `{dev:ID}:h` (sorted set scored by local sequence number, member packs accept-time + ≤1 KB payload), `{dev:ID}:idx` (sorted set scored by server accept time, member = sequence, used only for retention pruning and as the 24 h window index), and `{dev:ID}:latest` (string holding the highest accepted sequence and its payload, **no TTL**). An upload of up to 1,000 readings is one call to a single device-local Redis function: for each reading it does a `ZADD` on `:h` keyed by sequence — a retry of the same sequence is a deterministic same-member upsert, so duplicates are impossible without any locking or first-write-wins ledger — then advances `:latest` only when the incoming sequence exceeds the stored one, so late or out-of-order arrivals can never move latest backward. The same function prunes `:idx`/`:h` entries older than 24 h and refreshes a 24 h TTL on the two history keys. The whole request is atomic and retry-safe on one slot; the HTTP 200 is the durability boundary and is returned only after the function commits.\n\nReads are the same shape: latest is a `GET` on `:latest`; the 24 h cursor is a bounded `ZRANGEBYSCORE` over `:idx` (page cap ~500 readings, opaque cursor = last sequence), fetching payloads from the same slot. Capacity: 10 M devices × 1 reading/30 s ≈ 333 k readings/s and ≈ 29 TB of live history at 1 KB × 2,880 readings/device — the bind is memory, not ops, so scaling is purely shard count (≈150 primaries of 256 GB with replicas), never a topology change. Day 1 is the identical graph with 3 shards. Because `:latest` carries no TTL, it survives history expiry for silent devices at ~1 KB × device count (~10 GB), which is negligible.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** replicated, persistence-enabled (AOF everysec + replica) Redis/Valkey cluster; no second store, no projection, no cache.\n- **Stable ownership key:** `device_id` hash tag; identical routing contract day 1 and vFinal.\n- **Bounded ingest:** ≤1,000 readings × 1 KB, one slot, one atomic device-local function call, O(n log n) on that device only.\n- **Bounded query:** latest = one `GET`; history = one paged `ZRANGEBYSCORE` capped at 500 readings, single slot, no cross-shard fanout.\n- **Retry path:** dedupe by device-local sequence as the sorted-set member/score — same-key upsert, converges under unlimited retries; no idempotency ledger, no queue.\n- **Durability boundary:** acknowledgment is sent after the function returns from the primary; unreplicated-primary-loss window is the AOF/replication lag, and acknowledged-but-lost readings are re-uploaded from the device buffer on reconnect.\n- **Permanent components:** stateless HTTP app replicas; sharded replicated Redis/Valkey. Nothing else — no broker, worker, scheduler, TSDB, blob tier, or object cache survives collapse, since retention is per-key TTL, dedupe is the sequence key, and fanout is one device per request.\n- **Revisit triggers:** if a single device's rate or a single tenant's cross-device query exceeds one shard (not in the stated contract), or if ~29 TB of RAM is cost-disqualifying, keep this exact routing/ownership/atomicity contract and move `:h`/`:idx` to a disk-backed store sharded by the same `device_id`; `:latest` stays in Redis/Valkey.",
