      "outputText": "## Recommendation\n\nBuild a **log-fronted, single-owner-per-device pipeline** with two separate serving stores. Devices speak mTLS HTTP/2 to a stateless gateway fleet that validates, caps, and compresses batches, then produces each reading to a partitioned durable log (Kafka/Redpanda, **8,192 partitions fixed from day one**, RF=3, `acks=all`, `min.insync.replicas=2`, 6h retention) with `partition = hash(device_id) mod 8192`. Steady state is 10M ÷ 30s ≈ **333K readings/s ≈ 333 MB/s ≈ 28.8 TB/day**, so each partition carries only ~41 readings/s — the partition count is over-provisioned at launch (6 brokers, 16 owner pods at ~512 partitions each) and you scale by *moving* partitions to more brokers and pods, never by resharding, so the key→partition mapping is stable forever. Each partition is consumed by exactly one **shard owner** process (consumer-group ownership + lease epoch), which gives per-device serialization for free and lets it hold an in-memory `max_seq` map (10M devices × ~24B ≈ 240 MB fleet-wide). The owner materializes into ScyllaDB: a **history** table keyed `((device_id, ingest_hour), seq DESC)` with TWCS on 1-hour windows and `default_time_to_live = 93600` (26h = 24h contract + 2h slack for materialization lag, clock skew, and window granularity) so expiry is a whole-SSTable file drop rather than a tombstone storm (~28.8 TB logical, ~110 TB provisioned at RF=3); and a **latest** table of one row per device, no TTL, never deleted.\n\nCorrectness rests on two mechanisms that need no coordination. **Dedup is by exact primary key, not by high-water mark**: a retried reading rewrites the identical `(device_id, ingest_hour, seq)` row, so it is idempotent, while a legitimately old buffered reading (lower `seq`, never previously accepted) still lands — you must not reject on `seq ≤ max_seq`, because the strictly-increasing/never-reused guarantee permits gaps from partially-acked batches. **Monotonic latest** comes from upserting the latest row with the Scylla cell timestamp set to `seq`: last-write-wins resolution then makes a higher `seq` always win and a late or out-of-order `seq` always lose, with no LWT (Paxos at 333K/s is not viable), no tombstones, and no risk from a zombie owner during failover — the only constraint is that this table is delete-free. Because all queries are single-device, the query router hashes `device_id` to the same shard owner, which serves `latest` from memory and merges its unmaterialized tail into history reads, so an ack implies immediate queryability despite async materialization (target p99 < 1s, page at 5s). On owner failure the successor replays from the last committed offset and rebuilds `max_seq` from the latest table in seconds; affected devices get 503 with `Retry-After` during that window. Device buffers hold 1,000 readings ≈ **8.3 hours**, so set a hard recovery objective of ≤2h to avoid data loss at the edge.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** the partitioned durable log. It is the ordering and truth source; both Scylla tables are deterministic, rebuildable projections of it. Within a device, `seq` — never wall-clock time — is the authority for recency, since device clocks skew.\n- **Stable ownership key:** `device_id`. It fixes log partition (`hash(device_id) mod 8192`, count immutable), shard owner, history partition prefix, latest row, and query route. Nothing in the system re-keys on growth.\n- **Bounded ingest path:** ≤1,000 readings and ≤1 MB per request; ≤1 KB per reading; one in-flight request per device; per-device token bucket (sustained 2/min, burst 1,000); global catch-up admission capped at 2× steady state (~667 MB/s) to shape reconnect storms; 5s gateway timeout; overflow sheds with 429 + jittered `Retry-After` while devices keep buffering.\n- **Bounded query path:** two shapes only, both single-device. `GET latest` is an O(1) point read. History is cursor-paginated, ≤1,000 readings or 1 MB per page, opaque cursor encoding `(ingest_hour, seq)` — no offsets, no scans, server-enforced 24h window, no cross-device queries on this path. History orders by *acceptance* (a buffered dump lands in the hour it was ingested); each reading carries its device timestamp for client-side reordering.\n- **Bounded retry path:** idempotent on `(device_id, seq)` at the storage primary key, so retries are safe at any time and in any order. Every batch response returns a per-reading status vector plus `highest_accepted_seq`, letting the device truncate its buffer deterministically rather than re-uploading blindly.\n- **Durability boundary:** the log commit to ≥2 in-sync replicas. The device is the owner of a reading and must keep it buffered until it sees that ack; after the ack the platform is the owner and the 24h retention clock starts. Materialization lag lives entirely inside the platform's guarantee, which is why TTL carries the 2h slack.\n- **Permanent components (no TTL, must survive everything):** the `device_latest` table — one row per device holding `{seq, payload, device_ts, ingest_ts}`, ~12 GB logical / ~36 GB at RF=3 for 10M devices — which doubles as the durable high-water mark used to rebuild owner state and to reject backward `latest` moves long after the history copy expires; plus the device registry (ID, credentials, tenant) and the partition/ownership map. Everything else — log segments (6h), history rows (26h), owner memory, caches — is deliberately ephemeral.",
