      "outputText": "## Recommendation\n\nRun one stateless HTTP deployable (ingest and query in the same service) in front of one Valkey/Redis Cluster that is sharded by device from day 1. Every key a device owns carries the hash tag `{device_id}`, so all of its state lives in one slot on one shard and every request touches exactly one shard. A device owns three key types: **history blocks** `tel:{dev}:b:<blk>` — a hash whose fields are sequence numbers and whose values are the ≤1 KB payloads, where `blk = floor(seq / 128)` is a fixed, permanent constant of the key contract; a small **block index** `tel:{dev}:blk` — a sorted set of live block numbers (~23 members for a 24 h window); and the **latest head** `tel:{dev}:lat` — a hash holding `{seq, payload, ts}` with **no TTL**. Ingest is one Redis Function per request, applied atomically on the owning shard: for each reading it `HSET`s field `seq` into its seq-derived block (a deterministic same-key upsert, so a retry of the same `(device, seq)` overwrites itself and can never create a second logical reading), adds the block to the index and refreshes that block key's 25 h TTL, then updates the latest head **only if `seq > lat.seq`**. Because block identity is derived from `seq` and not from arrival time, a late or duplicate reading always lands in the same field it would have landed in originally; because the head is guarded by a strict comparison and never expires, latest is monotonic and survives the expiry of its own history copy until a higher `seq` replaces it. Queries are the mirror image: latest is one `HGETALL` on `tel:{dev}:lat`; the cursor is one function call that reads `ZRANGEBYSCORE blk (after_seq/128, +inf) LIMIT 0 32`, `HGETALL`s those blocks, sorts ≤128 numeric fields per block, returns ≤500 readings plus a continuation cursor, and lazily `ZREM`s index members whose block key has expired.\n\nAt vFinal this is 10M/30 s ≈ **333 k readings/s and ≤333 MB/s**, with 2,880 readings × ≤1 KB ≈ **≤2.9 MB resident per device → ≤29 TB** of history plus ~11 GB of latest heads. Sized at ~400 GB usable per primary that is roughly **100 primaries × 1 replica**, ~3.3 k function calls/s and ~3.3 MB/s per shard — far inside one node. Day 1 is the *same* graph with 3 primaries × 1 replica; growth is adding shards and moving whole slots, never a new component type, protocol, or authority. No device exceeds one shard's capacity: a single owner is 1 reading/30 s plus a ≤1,000-reading (~1 MB, ~8 blocks) reconnect burst. Nothing here is forced beyond the request — no external dependency, no fanout, no schedule, no disconnect-survival requirement — so there is **no queue, broker, worker deployment, scheduler, stream, cache tier, projection, or time-series/cold store**; backpressure is HTTP 429/503 against a client that is already a durable retry buffer.\n\n- **Canonical authority** — the sharded, replicated Valkey/Redis Cluster keyspace itself. It is authoritative, not derived; nothing else stores readings, and there is no second copy to reconcile.\n- **Stable ownership key** — `device_id`, applied as the hash tag `{device_id}` on all three key types, identical on day 1 and at vFinal.\n- **Bounded ingest path** — one HTTP POST ≤1,000 readings for one device → ≤4 chunked function calls of 250 on **one slot on one shard**, touching ~8 block keys + index + head; response returns the highest accepted `seq` so the device can release its buffer.\n- **Bounded query paths** — latest: 1 key, O(1). Cursor: ≤32 blocks and ≤500 readings (≤500 KB) per call on one slot, with a continuation cursor; the block index caps the walk regardless of how sparse the device's sequence space is.\n- **Bounded retry path** — `(device_id, seq)` addresses one hash field forever; retry is an idempotent overwrite, out-of-order arrival cannot move the head backward, and a re-uploaded old reading gets a fresh 24 h queryability window from its own acknowledgment.\n- **Durability boundary** — the ack is sent only after the function has applied on the shard primary **and** `WAIT 1 <timeout>` confirms a replica, with `appendfsync everysec` on both. Exposure is the simultaneous loss of primary and replica inside ~1 s; the device's unreleased buffer plus idempotent replay is the backstop, and a head that briefly regresses at failover is self-healing on the next accepted write.\n- **Retention boundary** — each write refreshes its block's 25 h TTL, so retention is always **≥24 h**, typically ≤26 h, and at most ~34 h under a full buffered replay; the latest head is exempt from TTL by design.\n- **Permanent components** — stateless HTTP app replicas + the sharded/replicated Redis-protocol authority. That is the entire component list, day 1 and at 10M devices.\n- **Revisit trigger** — the only thing that reopens this design is the RAM cost of ~29 TB of hot history. If measured mean payload × active devices makes it uneconomic, the smallest correction is to move history-block storage onto an SSD-tiered engine speaking the same protocol with the same hash tags and the same functions — a storage-medium swap inside the existing authority, not a new plane. Also revisit if readings must be queried across devices or beyond 24 h, which this shape deliberately does not serve.",
