      "outputText": "## Recommended architecture\n\nTerminate device traffic at an edge tier that authenticates the device (mTLS or signed token carrying `device_id`) and routes by consistent hash of `device_id` to a device-affine ingest shard, so all writes for one device are serialized on exactly one owner at a time. The ingest shard validates the batch, then writes each reading to ScyllaDB (Cassandra-compatible, quorum-replicated, RF=3) in two tables keyed by the same partition key: `readings(device_id PK, seq CK DESC, payload)` with a 24h TTL and TimeWindowCompactionStrategy (1h windows), and `latest(device_id PK, seq, payload)` with no TTL. History writes are plain idempotent upserts — a retry of `(device_id, seq)` rewrites identical immutable bytes, so retries can never create a duplicate logical reading and no dedup index is needed. The `latest` row is advanced only when the incoming `seq` exceeds the shard's in-memory high-water mark for that device; on cold start or ownership change the shard loads the mark from `latest`, and the first write after a transfer uses a lightweight transaction (`IF seq < ?`) to close the race. Because `seq` is strictly increasing and never reused, it — not arrival time or wall clock — defines \"latest,\" so a late or out-of-order upload is stored in history and silently skipped for `latest`. The reply is per-reading (`accepted` / `duplicate` / `rejected`), returned only after both writes commit at LOCAL_QUORUM.\n\nReads are two shapes and nothing else: a point read of `latest` (single-row, single-partition, optionally fronted by a short-TTL read-through cache) and a paginated descending scan of `readings` within a server-clamped 24h window, with an opaque cursor that is just the last-returned `seq`. Steady state is ~333K readings/s and ~333K requests/s; size the ingest tier at ~120 pods (5K req/s each) and Scylla at ~40 nodes for ~2× write headroom, since each reading costs at most two writes to the same replica set. Reconnect storms are absorbed by admission control rather than queues: a bounded per-shard work queue, per-device token buckets, and 429 + `Retry-After` with jitter when the budget is exceeded — shedding is safe precisely because unacknowledged readings stay buffered on the device and retry idempotently. Assumption worth flagging: `latest` rows are retained indefinitely by default; a reaper for devices inactive beyond a business-defined window (e.g. 90 days) is a policy decision, and at ~10 GB total the table is cheap enough to keep whole.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** ScyllaDB, RF=3, LOCAL_QUORUM reads and writes. `readings` is the authority for history; `latest` is the authority for the current reading. No cache, queue, or in-memory shard state is ever authoritative — the high-water mark is a fast path, rebuildable from `latest`.\n- **Stable ownership key:** `device_id` is the partition key for both tables and the consistent-hash routing key for ingest shards; `(device_id, seq)` is the logical identity of a reading and the idempotency key. Ownership survives rebalance because it is derived, not assigned.\n- **Bounded ingest path:** ≤1,000 readings and ≤1 MB per request, rejected at the edge before parsing; per-device token bucket sized for 1 reading/30s sustained with backfill burst allowance; bounded per-shard queue with load shedding, never unbounded buffering.\n- **Bounded query path:** page size capped at 1,000 rows, time range server-clamped to the trailing 24h, cursor is a single `seq` (no offset scans, no cross-partition queries, no fan-out).\n- **Bounded retry path:** retries are free and unlimited in effect because `(device_id, seq)` upserts are idempotent; clients are bounded by exponential backoff with jitter driven by server `Retry-After`. Devices retain unacknowledged readings and never renumber a `seq`.\n- **Durability boundary:** the ack is sent only after the history write and the `latest` evaluation have both committed at quorum. Nothing is acknowledged from memory or from a pre-durability buffer, so \"acknowledged\" and \"queryable for 24h\" are the same event.\n- **Permanent components:** the `latest` table (no TTL, one row per device, ~10 GB at 10M devices) is the only unbounded-lifetime data; everything else — history rows, cursors, cached reads, shard high-water marks — expires or is reconstructible. This is what keeps the latest accepted reading available after its 24h history copy is compacted away.",
