      "outputText": "## Architecture\n\nRun a stateless ingest tier (HTTP/2 or gRPC, mTLS-authenticated with the device identity bound to `device_id`) behind an L7 load balancer, writing directly into a single partitioned wide-column store — ScyllaDB or Cassandra, RF=3 across three AZs, `LOCAL_QUORUM` for reads and writes. There are exactly two tables. `readings` is keyed `((device_id), seq)` with `CLUSTERING ORDER BY seq DESC` and a 24-hour TTL; at 2,880 readings/day of ≤1 KB, a device partition tops out near 3 MB, so history paging is always a single-partition range scan. `latest` is keyed `(device_id)`, holds one row, and carries **no TTL**. Both writes are issued with an explicit `USING TIMESTAMP <seq>`, which delegates conflict resolution to the storage engine's last-write-wins on cell timestamp. Because `seq` is strictly increasing and never reused per device, this makes every write idempotent and commutative: a retry of `(device_id, seq)` resolves to the identical cell, and a late or out-of-order reading carries a smaller timestamp than the row already stored, so it is discarded by the engine and can never move `latest` backward. No lightweight transactions, no read-before-write, no per-device locking, and no ordered queue is required on the acknowledgment path — which is what makes 333K writes/sec (10M devices ÷ 30s) tractable at roughly 60–100 NVMe nodes holding ~87 TB replicated, and equally makes a 3-node cluster correct at launch.\n\nA batch upload of up to 1,000 buffered readings from one device lands entirely in one storage partition: the ingest tier validates and caps the request, issues the row writes concurrently, and issues exactly one `latest` upsert for the batch's maximum `seq` (coalescing 1,000 pointer updates into one). The response acknowledges the highest contiguous `seq` durably committed, so the device truncates its local buffer deterministically and replays the remainder. Reads are two shapes only: `GET /devices/{id}/latest` is a single-row point read of `latest`, and `GET /devices/{id}/readings?cursor=` is a bounded single-partition scan of `readings` filtered to `ts >= now-24h`, returning ≤200 rows per page with an opaque cursor encoding the last `seq`. A Kafka tee for analytics fans out asynchronously from the ingest tier and is explicitly *not* on the acknowledgment path; it may lag or be replayed without affecting correctness. The one behavior to state plainly: TTL is measured from write time, not event time, so a backfilled reading is guaranteed its full 24 hours of queryability from acknowledgment and the time filter, not the TTL, defines the user-visible window.\n\n- **Canonical authority** — the `readings` and `latest` tables in the quorum-replicated cluster. `latest` is authoritative for the current value; `readings` is authoritative for the 24-hour window. The Kafka stream, any cache, and any device-side buffer are non-authoritative derivatives.\n- **Stable ownership key** — `device_id`, hashed to a token range whose replica set owns it. Stable for the life of the device; `(device_id, seq)` is globally unique forever because sequence numbers are never reused. Ownership is data placement, not process affinity, so node loss or rebalance never creates a stale writer.\n- **Bounded ingest path** — request body capped at 1,000 readings / ~1.2 MB; one storage partition per request; per-device token bucket sized for sustained 2/min plus a backfill burst; fixed write timeout with 429 + `Retry-After` under back-pressure. Cost per request is O(batch), independent of history depth or fleet size.\n- **Bounded query path** — `latest` is one point read; history is one partition scan of at most 2,880 rows, paged at 200 with a server-validated opaque cursor. No cross-partition scans, no fan-out reads, no aggregation.\n- **Bounded retry path** — idempotency comes from the primary key plus `USING TIMESTAMP seq`, so a retry is a byte-identical re-write with no dedup table and no bounded dedup window. Clients use exponential backoff with jitter; the highest-contiguous-`seq` ack bounds how much a device can ever re-send.\n- **Durability boundary** — acknowledgment is returned only after the `readings` rows and the `latest` upsert are quorum-committed to the commit log across AZs. Until that returns, the device's local buffer holds the reading. Nothing is ever acked from an in-memory queue or from the async Kafka tee.\n- **Permanent components** — the `latest` row per device (no TTL, deleted only on explicit device decommission, so it outlives its history copy for silent devices); the device registry and credential/identity records; the partitioning scheme and `seq`-as-write-timestamp convention, which is load-bearing for correctness and cannot be changed without a migration; cluster topology and keyspace configuration. Everything in `readings` is ephemeral by design.",
