      "messageSetId": "b9d1a6423764ba91fa478ff812a0d1239150b01537bea9c3791a200fc9d817af",
      "outputText": "## Recommendation\n\nRun **one stateless HTTP deployable** in front of **one disk-backed, hash-partitioned wide-column authority partitioned by `device_id`** (Cassandra/ScyllaDB family — chosen for five properties the contract needs together: hash partitioning by owner, clustered ordered rows inside a partition, per-row TTL, quorum-durable acknowledged writes, and client-supplied cell timestamps). Every device owns exactly one partition holding two tables: `readings((device_id), seq DESC)` with a per-row 24 h TTL, and `head((device_id))` with **no TTL**, carrying the full payload of the latest accepted reading. Deduplication is structural, not machinery: `(device_id, seq)` is the primary key, so a retried batch upserts the same rows and can never create a second logical reading. Monotonicity is also structural: every write to `head` is issued `USING TIMESTAMP <seq>`, so the storage engine's last-write-wins resolution *is* max-by-seq — a late or out-of-order reading with a lower `seq` deterministically loses, on every replica, with no read-modify-write, no lock, and no Paxos round. Because `head` stores the payload and never carries a TTL, latest stays answerable after the history copy expires, and is replaced only by a strictly higher `seq`. An ingest request writes its ≤1000 rows as one single-partition batch, then writes `head`, then acknowledges; both are idempotent, so a crash between them is repaired by the device's own retry, and an unacknowledged reading is promised nothing.\n\nThere is no queue, worker, scheduler, broker, cache, or projection, because each collapses cleanly: the device's local buffer *is* the ingest queue and its retry is free and convergent, so a broker only adds a second durability boundary in front of the one that already acknowledges; expiry is TTL plus TimeWindowCompactionStrategy (1 h windows), so whole SSTables drop and no reaper job exists; latest is a single-row point read of an owner-local partition, so a cache would duplicate the authority to save one disk seek; and `head` lives in the same partition as its history, written on the same request path, so it is state correctness rather than a derived plane needing a rebuild pipeline. Sizing at the stated bound: 10 M devices × 1 reading/30 s = **333 K writes/s**, 2880 readings/device/day, **28.8 TB logical** for 24 h at the 1 KB cap, ≈86 TB at RF=3 and ≈130 TB provisioned with compaction headroom — roughly 24–32 dense NVMe nodes at vFinal versus 3 at launch, identical topology, scaled only by node count and token ranges. That working set plus the requirement that *acknowledged* readings survive is what rules out a RAM-resident authority here: 28.8 TB in memory costs roughly an order of magnitude more and recovers in hours, and asynchronous primary→replica replication can lose an acknowledged write, which is the one thing this contract forbids.\n\n- **Canonical authority** — the wide-column cluster; `head` is authoritative for latest, `readings` for the 24 h window. No second store, no derived copy, no event log.\n- **Stable ownership key** — `device_id`, used as the partition key from day 1 and unchanged at vFinal. One device = one partition ≤ ~3 MB (2880 × 1 KB), so aggregate scale spreads bounded owners across nodes and no owner ever needs subsharding.\n- **Bounded ingest path** — `POST /devices/{id}/readings`, ≤1000 readings and ≤1 MB per request (413 above), touching exactly 1 partition on 3 replicas with ≤2 round trips and zero cross-shard fanout.\n- **Bounded query paths** — latest: one point read of `head`. Cursor: `WHERE device_id=? AND seq < ? ORDER BY seq DESC LIMIT ≤200`, cursor token = last `seq` returned, single partition per page. Read QPS was not given, so query capacity is stated as a scale function (independent per-device partitions, scaling by node count), **not** a proven throughput number.\n- **Bounded retry path** — replaying a batch re-issues the same PK upserts (no-ops) and re-issues `head` at `TIMESTAMP=seq` (no-op if `seq ≤ head.seq`); convergent regardless of interleaving, duplication, or partial prior success.\n- **Durability boundary** — acknowledgment is returned only after the `readings` batch **and** the `head` write both reach `QUORUM` (RF=3) with commitlog persistence. Before that, the device retains the reading and retries; nothing acknowledged depends on a buffer that a single node loss can drop.\n- **Permanent components** — exactly two: the stateless HTTP replicas and the partitioned authority. Day-1 and vFinal component graphs are identical.\n\n**Top failures and corrections**\n\n- *Reconnect thundering herd* — a fleet-wide reconnect can offer far more than the 333 K/s steady rate. Correction: per-device and global token-bucket admission at the HTTP tier sized to the cluster's sustained write budget, returning 429 with jittered `Retry-After`. Safe precisely because retries are idempotent and the device buffer is the queue; no broker is needed to absorb the burst.\n- *Latest moving backward* — prevented by `head` cell timestamp = `seq`. Constraint this imposes, and it is load-bearing: **no write to `head` may ever use a wall-clock timestamp**, and an administrative delete must use `TIMESTAMP > max(seq)`. Violating this permanently poisons that device's head row.\n- *TTL tombstone load at ~333 K expiring rows/s* — correction: TWCS with ~1 h windows, TTL = 24 h + small grace, and low `gc_grace_seconds` since rows are only ever TTL-expired (deterministic on all replicas), so expiry is whole-SSTable drops rather than tombstone scans during cursor reads.\n\n**Revisit triggers** — mean payload measured far below the 1 KB bound *and* the device population capped well under 10 M would shrink the working set enough to reopen a RAM-resident authority; a requirement for cross-device queries, fanout, or fleet aggregates would break the single-partition access assumption and force a real second read path; and a device-side buffer that cannot survive a reconnect window would break the \"device is the queue\" collapse and force a durable ingest landing zone.",
