      "outputText": "Use stateless ingest/query services over **sharded, replicated transactional key-value storage**, implemented as FoundationDB cells across three availability zones. Route a stable hash of `device_id` to a logical shard; a durable directory maps shards to cells, with fenced ownership changes during migration. Start with one cell and add cells as capacity grows. Store history under `(device_id, sequence)`, permanent deduplication receipts under the same identity, and one permanent latest record per device containing the full reading. Interpret “previous 24 hours” as **server acceptance time**, retaining device timestamps separately; device clocks cannot safely govern this guarantee. At 10 million active devices, baseline traffic is approximately **333,333 readings/second**, **28.8 billion readings/day**, and up to **28.8 TB/day of payload**, before replication, indexes, and receipts. Reconnect bursts are unspecified: capacity-test each cell and enforce admission limits rather than promise unlimited burst absorption.\n\nFor each first acceptance, atomically write the receipt, history, and—only when its sequence exceeds the current latest sequence—the latest record. Receipts contain a payload digest and original acceptance metadata: identical retries return the original result, while conflicting payloads for an existing sequence are rejected. A retry never refreshes history retention or becomes a new acceptance; after history expires, its receipt still prevents resurrection. Accept previously unseen lower sequences into history without changing latest. Process requests of at most 1,000 readings in bounded transactions, return per-reading outcomes, and acknowledge success only after commit; partial completion is safe to retry. Define acknowledgements as server-issued acceptance decisions, with history retained for at least 24 hours from that decision; guaranteeing 24 hours from actual client receipt would require an additional protocol because network delay is unbounded.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** The owning cell’s transactional receipts, history, and latest records. Queues, caches, and replicas outside its consistency boundary are not acceptance authorities.\n- **Stable ownership key:** `hash(device_id)` selects a logical shard; `(device_id, sequence)` identifies a logical reading. Ownership epochs fence stale writers.\n- **Bounded ingest/query/retry paths:** Enforce encoded request-size limits, at most 1,000 readings per request, and transactions of at most 100 readings. Apply per-device and per-cell admission limits before acceptance. Latest is one point read. History uses a `(device_id, acceptance_time, sequence)` index and opaque keyset cursors, with at most 100 readings per page and a fixed 24-hour acceptance window. Pages read committed data without promising a cross-page snapshot. Retry transaction conflicts only a bounded number of times, then return a retryable error.\n- **Durability boundary:** Successful transactional commit to the cell’s configured multi-AZ synchronous durability quorum precedes acknowledgement. Backups support disaster recovery but do not replace that boundary. Expiration workers delete history and its index only after retention eligibility; latest is independent.\n- **Permanent components:** Routing directory and fencing metadata, replicated storage cells, ingest/query services, expiration workers, backups, monitoring, **latest payloads**, and **deduplication receipts**. With no maximum retry age, receipts must persist indefinitely; arbitrary out-of-order acceptance means a sequence high-water mark alone is insufficient. Budget explicitly for this growing metadata footprint.",
