      "outputText": "Use stateless HTTP application replicas and one replicated, disk-backed transactional authority, sharded from launch by a stable hash of `device_id`. Each device’s shard holds readings keyed uniquely by `(device_id, sequence)` and a separate persistent latest-reading record containing the payload and sequence. In one shard-local transaction, insert previously unseen readings and replace latest only when an incoming sequence exceeds its current sequence. Retain history for 24 hours from first acceptance, rather than device timestamp; retries never extend retention. Keep a compact accepted-sequence interval ledger after payload expiry so old retries cannot resurrect expired readings. Since sequences may contain arbitrary gaps, this ledger’s lifetime size is unbounded by the supplied contract and must be included in storage planning.\n\nAt launch and at 10 million active devices, the graph remains `devices/users → HTTP replicas → owner-sharded authority`; capacity grows through replicas and shard reassignment using the authority’s native routing. Steady ingestion reaches approximately 333,333 readings/second and 28.8 billion readings per day—up to roughly 28.8 TB of payload history before indexes, replication, latest records, and reconnect traffic. Query demand and simultaneous reconnect volume are unspecified, so capacity remains unproven until tested against an agreed envelope. The principal protections are quorum-durable acknowledgement against failover loss, transactional deduplication against ambiguous retries, and a persistent maximum-sequence head against late arrivals and history expiry. Benchmark reconnect bursts and storage cleanup alongside ingestion; aggregate device count alone does not justify another processing plane.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** The single transactional store owns history, accepted-sequence intervals, and the full latest-reading record. History expiry cannot delete or regress latest.\n- **Stable ownership key:** `device_id`; all state and every operation for one device touch one logical shard. Authorize device ingestion and user access against that device.\n- **Bounded ingest:** At most 1,000 readings of at most 1 KB each per request, with separately capped metadata. One transaction performs bounded key operations and local interval updates; never scan the device’s entire ledger. Reject conflicting reuse of a retained sequence with a different payload; after payload expiry, acknowledge it only as previously accepted.\n- **Bounded queries:** Latest is one record lookup. History uses an indexed acceptance-time range for the preceding 24 hours, ordered by `(accepted_at, sequence)`, with pages capped at 1,000 readings and a bounded response size. The cursor fixes the range endpoint; expired rows remain subject to retention.\n- **Bounded retries:** Retry the same device/sequence identities with backoff; committed duplicates cause no new logical reading or retention change. A lost response is resolved by replay. Enforce admission limits before starting transactions; overload must be retried and does not count as demonstrated target capacity.\n- **Durability boundary:** Acknowledge only after the complete transaction is durably committed to a synchronous replica quorum. The minority cannot acknowledge writes. Read through the authority’s consistency path so acknowledged data is queryable; the supported failure scope is quorum-preserving node failures, with regional-disaster requirements still unspecified.\n- **Permanent components:** HTTP application replicas and the sharded transactional authority, including its native replication, routing, and bounded retention cleanup. HTTP serves authentication and request handling; the authority supplies persistence, atomicity, deduplication, and indexed queries. No separate broker, cache, projection, or worker service is required.",
