      "outputText": "Use stateless ingest/query gateways backed by **RocksDB storage groups replicated with Raft across three availability zones**. Hash each device ID into one of 16,384 fixed virtual shards; start with one storage group and add groups by moving virtual shards through fenced handoffs. All state for a device stays with its shard. At 10 million devices, steady ingestion is approximately **333,000 readings/second and 29 TB of payload per day**, or 87 TB with three replicas, before indexes, logs, and metadata. Reconnect bursts, query volume, latency targets, and regional disaster-recovery requirements remain unspecified; benchmark group capacity and enforce admission limits accordingly.\n\nUse `(device_id, sequence)` as the immutable logical reading identity. An atomic replicated operation checks a permanent identity/payload-digest record, writes or refreshes the history row and its time index, and replaces the separate latest record—including its payload—only when the sequence is greater. Previously unseen lower sequences are accepted into history. Matching retries reuse the same identity; conflicting payloads are rejected. Define the history window using server acceptance time, with every successful retry refreshing its retention for at least another 24 hours. An expired replay restores the same logical history row. Latest has no TTL. With no retry-age limit supplied, retain identity/digest records indefinitely; this metadata grows with lifetime accepted readings.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** Each shard’s committed Raft state contains the identity ledger, history, history index, and latest payload. These change atomically, so acknowledgment never depends on an asynchronously updated query index.\n- **Stable ownership key:** `device_id`, independent of sequence and time. Moves transfer committed state and retained query versions; ownership epochs fence the previous owner before the destination accepts writes.\n- **Bounded ingest path:** Authenticate one device per request; cap requests at 1,000 readings and 2 MiB. Process at most ten atomic chunks of 100 readings, returning explicit per-reading outcomes. Apply device and global quotas, bounded queues, and request deadlines; reject excess work before acknowledging it.\n- **Bounded query path:** Latest is one linearizable lookup. History uses an index on `(device_id, most_recent_acceptance_time, sequence)`, returning at most 256 readings per page. Signed cursors fix the time window, snapshot version, and last key. Give cursors a five-minute lifetime and retain the necessary versions through that interval.\n- **Bounded retry path:** Retries perform indexed identity lookups through the same owner and transaction path. A successful retry commits renewed retention before responding. Limit internal routing/commit attempts by the request deadline; clients retry uncertain outcomes with unchanged identities and jittered backoff, honoring `Retry-After`.\n- **Durability boundary:** Acknowledge each chunk only after a majority has fsynced its Raft entry and the leader has applied it. Serve reads from committed state. This deployment tolerates one availability-zone failure. Expiration removes history copies while preserving latest and identity records; retention margins cover bounded acknowledgment delays and clock error.\n- **Permanent components:** Gateways with admission control, the replicated shard directory and placement controller, Raft/RocksDB storage groups, backup/restore facilities, and monitoring. Keep this architecture from launch onward and scale its storage groups and gateways.",
