      "outputText": "Use **independently scalable TiKV storage cells**, each replicated across three availability zones, behind stateless authentication and ingest gateways. Assign each device to a fixed virtual bucket using its stable device ID; a durable directory maps buckets to cells, with ownership epochs fencing stale writers during migration. Start with a few cells and add cells by moving buckets. At 10 million active devices, steady traffic is approximately **333,333 readings/second, 28.8 billion readings/day, and 28.8 TB/day of maximum-size payloads**, before replication and metadata. Reconnect frequency is unspecified, so burst capacity cannot be calculated from the 1,000-reading request limit alone; enforce admission limits and provision against measured reconnect traffic.\n\nWithin its owning cell, process each reading in a serializable transaction covering its permanent receipt, retained history, history index, and latest record. Identity is `(device_id, sequence_number)`: retain a permanent receipt with its payload digest and original acceptance time, reject conflicting payloads, and upsert matching retries into the same logical history record. Every successful acceptance—including retries—renews history retention for 24 hours; keep latest separately without expiration and replace it only for a greater sequence number. Define the history window using **server acceptance/renewal time**, preserving original acceptance time and device timestamps as metadata; device-event-time history would require an additional timestamp contract. This makes even an old retried reading queryable again without creating a second logical reading or moving latest backward.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** The owning cell’s transactional records. History, receipts, indexes, and latest commit atomically; caches and asynchronous consumers never determine acceptance.\n- **Stable ownership key:** Device ID, mapped through fixed virtual buckets. All records for a device stay in one cell; migration transfers durable state before publishing a fenced ownership change.\n- **Bounded ingest/query/retry paths:** Cap requests at 1,000 readings and enforce byte limits. Process bounded transaction chunks and return explicit per-reading outcomes; a lost response permits safe whole-request retry. Apply per-device, tenant, and cell quotas with exponential backoff and jitter. Latest is one point read. History uses a device-prefixed acceptance-time index, capped pages, and an opaque cursor carrying the query window, snapshot, and last key. Snapshot leases expire explicitly, requiring a query restart.\n- **Durability boundary:** Acknowledge only after quorum-replicated transaction commit. Partial requests acknowledge only committed readings. Set history expiration at least 24 hours beyond the permitted acknowledgment-send deadline; suppress responses that miss that deadline. Garbage collection checks expiration transactionally so it cannot delete a concurrently renewed reading. This covers acknowledgment issuance; delivery-relative retention would require a bounded network delay.\n- **Permanent components:** Gateways, bucket directory and migration controller, replicated transactional cells, expiration workers, monitoring, and backup/restore infrastructure. Latest records and exact deduplication receipts are permanent; history and its indexes expire. Receipt storage therefore grows with lifetime unique readings, not merely the 24-hour window—budget it explicitly.",
