      "outputText": "Use stateless HTTP replicas and **one replicated, disk-backed transactional authority, sharded from launch by device ID**. Each device owns sequence-keyed history and a permanent latest-reading record on the same shard. An ingest transaction inserts absent readings, preserves existing readings on retries, and replaces latest only when an incoming sequence exceeds its stored sequence. Latest stores the full reading independently of expiring history. Define the 24-hour history window by server acceptance time; device timestamps are not specified. Users receive acknowledgement after durable commit and can immediately read latest or paginate history.\n\nLaunch and final topology are identical: `devices/users → HTTP replicas → device-owner shards`. Grow by adding replicas and reassigning partitions using the authority’s native routing. Ten million active devices produce approximately **333,333 readings/second and 28.8 billion readings per day**, up to **28.8 TB of daily payload** before replication and metadata. These establish the steady-state storage and ingest targets; capacity remains unproven until tested. Reconnect concurrency and response-latency requirements are unknown: all devices uploading full buffers would contribute another 10 billion readings. Benchmark steady traffic, reconnect bursts, and shard failover without treating rejected traffic as successful capacity.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** One transactional store containing history, its expiry/query indexes, and permanent latest records. No second datastore, cache, or projection.\n- **Stable ownership key:** `device_id`; all history, deduplication, and latest mutations for a device share one shard. Authenticate writes and authorize reads against that device.\n- **Bounded ingest/query/retry paths:** One request contains at most 1,000 readings and approximately 1 MB of payload, touches one shard, and commits atomically. `(device_id, sequence)` identifies a logical reading; retained duplicates neither multiply rows nor extend retention. Reject conflicting payloads for an existing sequence. Latest reads return one record; history pages examine at most 1,000 entries and return at most 100 readings, carrying a continuation cursor even when expired entries are skipped. An expired reading uploaded again receives a new acceptance window under the same logical key.\n- **Durability boundary:** Acknowledge only after the history and latest transaction is synchronously persisted to a quorum across independent failure domains. Serve authoritative reads with committed-state consistency. A lost response permits whole-request retry; loss of quorum stops acknowledgement. Expire history no earlier than 24 hours after acceptance, using server-controlled time; never expire latest.\n- **Permanent components:** HTTP replicas and the sharded authority, including native replication, routing, indexes, and expiry. HTTP handles authentication and bounded requests; the authority supplies atomicity and acknowledged-write safety. The three principal failure corrections are quorum persistence for failures and retries, transactional sequence comparison for late arrivals, and bounded admission with device backoff for reconnect overload. No queue or separate worker is required because acceptance completes within the durable transaction.",
