      "outputText": "Use stateless HTTPS ingest/query services backed by a multi-region Google Cloud Spanner database, with keys prefixed by a hash of the stable device ID. Start with a small deployment and increase database capacity and service replicas as load grows. At 10 million devices, baseline traffic is approximately **333,333 readings/second, 28.8 billion readings/day, and 28.8 TB/day of payload**, before metadata, indexes, and replication. Reconnection capacity needs a separate budget: every device uploading 1,000 readings produces 10 billion additional readings. Provision that capacity against a recovery-time target; the task does not specify one.\n\nIdentify each logical reading by **`(device_id, sequence)`**. Store a permanent receipt containing its payload digest, an expiring history row containing its payload, and a separate, non-expiring latest row containing both sequence and payload. In one serializable transaction, check or create the receipt, persist or refresh history, and replace latest only when the incoming sequence is greater. Reject reuse of an existing identity with different content. Use server acceptance time for the history window; preserve device timestamps as data. Successful retries refresh that same logical reading’s history timestamp and retention, reconstructing an expired history row from the digest-verified retry payload. Retain history for at least 24 hours after each successful acknowledgment, using a bounded response deadline and retention grace; renew retention before sending a delayed success.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** Committed Spanner receipt, history, and latest records. Their updates commit atomically; acknowledgment follows commit. Latest contains the full payload, so history expiration cannot break latest reads.\n- **Stable ownership key:** `(hash(device_id), device_id)`. Database ranges can split and migrate while device identity and reading identity remain unchanged.\n- **Bounded ingest path:** Accept at most 1,000 readings per request, enforce body and concurrency limits, and process chunks of at most 100 readings per transaction. Return individual outcomes so partial completion is explicit. Apply admission control before accepting excess work.\n- **Bounded query path:** Latest and individual-reading lookup are point reads. History uses an index on `(device_hash, device_id, last_accepted_at, sequence)`, with a fixed snapshot and time window. Pages stop at 1,000 rows or 1 MiB. Signed cursors expire after 15 minutes, backed by matching database version retention.\n- **Bounded retry path:** Each retry performs indexed identity checks, never a historical scan. Clients retry unsuccessful items with exponential backoff and jitter. Permanent receipts prevent duplication after payload expiration; arbitrary out-of-order delivery means a maximum-sequence watermark alone cannot replace them.\n- **Durability boundary:** Success requires the transaction’s synchronous replicated quorum commit across the configured regions. Expiration conditionally checks the current retention deadline so it cannot delete a concurrently refreshed reading.\n- **Permanent components:** Ingest/query services, the database and backups, expiration workers, and monitoring/admission control. Latest rows and receipt records have no TTL. Receipt storage therefore grows indefinitely under the unspecified retry horizon and must be included in capacity and cost planning.",
