      "outputText": "Use stateless HTTP replicas backed by **one replicated, disk-backed transactional authority**, sharded from launch by `device_id`. Each shard stores sequence-keyed history and a permanent per-device latest record containing both sequence and payload. In one transaction, upsert submitted readings and replace latest only when the submitted sequence is greater. Retries use the same keys and renew retention; out-of-order arrivals cannot move latest backward. Define the 24-hour window by server acceptance time, since device-time semantics are unspecified. Every successful acknowledgement guarantees another 24 hours of history availability, including for retries; latest has no expiry.\n\nThe graph stays **devices/users → HTTP replicas → authority shards**, with three replicas per shard at launch and at final scale. Grow by adding application replicas and reassigning native storage partitions. Ten million active devices imply an average of approximately **333,333 readings/second and up to 28.8 TB of new payload/day**, before replication, indexes, and buffered uploads. These establish a baseline, not proven capacity: synchronized arrivals, reconnect concurrency, retry traffic, and read demand remain unknown. Benchmark that baseline plus an explicit burst envelope; insufficient throughput requires more capacity, while a single device exceeding one shard’s capacity would invalidate this ownership boundary.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** History keyed by `(device_id, sequence)` and the latest payload reside in the same authority and transaction boundary. History expiry cannot delete latest. Retrying an expired reading recreates the same logical identity without requiring a permanent deduplication ledger.\n- **Stable ownership key:** Hash `device_id` through the datastore’s native partition routing. Every ingest, latest read, and history page touches one owner shard. Native rebalancing preserves this routing contract.\n- **Bounded ingest/query/retry paths:** Authenticate device writes and authorize user reads. Accept at most 1,000 readings per request, with explicit payload and metadata limits, in one shard-local transaction. Latest returns one reading. Sequence-ordered history pages examine at most 1,000 entries and return a bounded payload; cursors advance past examined entries even when expiry filtering yields an empty page. Pagination is live: late inserts behind a cursor require a fresh scan. Retries repeat the bounded request with backoff; bounded admission queues apply backpressure before acceptance.\n- **Durability boundary:** Acknowledge only after a consensus commit is fsynced on a quorum of three replicas, with fenced leadership and consistent reads. Persist a retention deadline covering at least 24 hours after the server sends its acknowledgement; include clock uncertainty and renew before replying if necessary. One replica loss is tolerated; quorum loss returns unavailable without acknowledging new writes. Stronger correlated-loss requirements remain unspecified.\n- **Permanent components:** HTTP application replicas and the partitioned database with native replication, transactions, expiry, and recovery. The database is forced by durable history and monotonic latest; application replicas enforce authentication and bounded requests. No separate broker, worker deployment, cache, or latest projection is required.",
