      "outputText": "Use stateless ingest/query gateways backed by an ordered key-value storage service with transactional writes and three durable Raft replicas across three availability zones. Hash devices into 65,536 fixed virtual buckets, mapped to movable storage partitions; start with three storage nodes and add nodes as load grows. At 10 million devices, normal traffic is approximately **333,333 readings/second and 28.8 billion readings/day**, or about **29 TB/day of payload** before replication and indexes. Reconnect traffic and query demand are additional capacity requirements; physical node counts require workload benchmarks.\n\nFor each device, colocate a permanent receipt index keyed by sequence number, expiring history, and a separate persistent latest record. Each acceptance atomically records the receipt, writes history, and replaces latest only when the incoming sequence exceeds its stored sequence. Receipts retain a payload digest: matching retries reuse the same logical reading; conflicting payloads are rejected. Successful retries renew retention, restoring an expired history copy from the verified retry payload when necessary. Define the history window using server acceptance/renewal time, retaining device timestamps separately. Every successful acknowledgment carries a durable retention deadline at least 24 hours after its server-side issuance. Latest stores the full reading independently of history expiration.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** The replicated storage state containing receipts, history, and latest. Gateways acknowledge only authoritative commits; queries use linearizable reads or an explicitly identified snapshot.\n- **Stable ownership key:** `(hash(device_id) mod 65,536, device_id)`. Every operation for a device reaches its owning partition. Migration transfers a snapshot and subsequent committed log, then fences the old owner before activating the new owner.\n- **Bounded ingest path:** Enforce 1,000 readings and approximately 1 MiB of reading payload per request, with a separate metadata limit. Process at most 100 readings per transaction and return per-reading results. Bound queues, concurrency, and device/tenant rates; apply backpressure when capacity is exhausted. A partially completed request is safe to retry.\n- **Bounded query path:** Latest requires one indexed lookup. History uses an index on `(device_id, acceptance_or_renewal_time, sequence)`, returning at most 100 readings per page. Signed cursors bind the device, time window, snapshot, and last index key. Give cursors a five-minute lifetime and preserve the necessary snapshot versions until expiration; pagination uses indexed seeks.\n- **Bounded retry path:** Each reading requires one indexed receipt lookup and a bounded transactional update. Never infer that all sequences below latest were accepted: an unseen lower sequence remains eligible for history. Expired retries restore the existing identity without lowering latest.\n- **Durability boundary:** Acknowledge only after the receipt, history, retention deadline, and conditional latest update are committed and flushed to a replica quorum. Recheck the retention deadline before issuing success and extend it if needed. This targets survival of one availability-zone failure; client receipt time cannot be bounded under arbitrary network delay.\n- **Permanent components:** Gateways, admission control, replicated storage, the ownership directory, and incremental expiry workers remain throughout growth. Latest and receipt metadata persist indefinitely. Receipt storage grows with lifetime accepted readings; the supplied sequence guarantees do not justify discarding receipts or assuming bounded deduplication metadata.",
