      "outputText": "Use one horizontally replicated stateless HTTP application and one replicated, disk-backed ordered datastore, partitioned by `hash(device_id)` from launch. Each device owns history keyed by sequence number and a separate, non-expiring head containing the greatest accepted sequence **and its complete reading**. One device-local transaction upserts an ingest batch, renews its retention deadlines, and advances the head only for a greater sequence. This makes retries converge and prevents late arrivals or history expiry from moving latest backward. Use server acknowledgement time for the guaranteed 24-hour history window; event-time filtering is unspecified and cannot replace retention for delayed uploads. A successful retry renews the reading’s 24-hour guarantee.\n\nDay 1 and the final topology are identical: `devices/users → HTTP replicas → owner shard`. Grow by adding application replicas and datastore shards, using native shard reassignment without splitting a device’s ownership. At 10 million active devices, normal production is approximately **333,333 readings/second and 28.8 billion readings/day**, or roughly **29 TB of daily payload** before replication, indexes, and retry effects. Capacity remains unproven until measured: reconnect concurrency, sustained retry traffic, latency targets, and disaster-survival requirements are unknown. Benchmark normal production and reconnect traffic separately; rejected or deferred requests do not count as meeting an ingest target.\n\n- **Canonical authority:** The datastore owns history, retention deadlines, and the permanent head. History expiry deletes only the history copy. Latest reads use the authoritative head, never a scan of surviving history.\n- **Stable ownership key:** `device_id`; history, head, and retention bookkeeping remain on the same shard. Authenticate devices and authorize users against that device before accessing it.\n- **Bounded ingest/query/retry paths:** Accept at most 1,000 readings of at most 1 KB each, with bounded metadata, per single-device transaction. Latest requires one head lookup. History uses sequence cursors, returning at most 100 readings and inspecting at most 1,000 records per request; advance the cursor across expired records even when a page is empty. Retries upsert the same `(device_id, sequence)` keys. Lost responses are safely retried; reconnect overload receives backpressure without acknowledgement.\n- **Durability boundary:** Acknowledge only after the history, head update, and retention deadlines commit to a synchronously disk-persisted quorum. Each deadline must cover at least 24 hours after the new server acknowledgement. Three replicas per shard tolerate one replica failure; reads require current authoritative state, and quorum loss stops acknowledgements. Broader disaster guarantees require an explicit failure envelope.\n- **Permanent components:** HTTP application replicas and the partitioned datastore, including its native replication, routing, indexes, and bounded expiry maintenance. The application serves requests; the datastore supplies atomicity and durable ownership. Both are necessary. No separate queue, worker deployment, cache, projection, or latest-reading service is required.",
