      "messageSetId": "700b5a520725ec33e09536534315ca3502e0570af5bf977f2513f53804ffd1a8",
      "outputText": "Run a three-tier design: a stateless WebSocket edge, a conversation-sharded message service, and a durable log that feeds everything asynchronous. Clients hold one TLS WebSocket to the nearest of three regions, steered by GeoDNS onto a Layer 4 network load balancer and then onto connection gateways written in Elixir on the BEAM. Each gateway holds about 150k connections, so 10 million users need roughly 70 nodes of raw capacity, and you should run about 120 across regions for failover. A gateway validates a short-lived JWT, records the session in a regional Redis Cluster keyed by user id with a heartbeat TTL, and subscribes to its own delivery subject on NATS. A send is a gRPC call to a Go message service sharded by conversation id with consistent hashing. The owning shard assigns a monotonic per-conversation sequence number, recovered from ScyllaDB on restart, and writes the message to ScyllaDB partitioned by conversation id and time bucket. It then fans out. For direct messages and groups up to a few hundred members, it resolves recipients' gateways with one batched Redis lookup and publishes one envelope per target gateway carrying that gateway's recipient list. For large channels it publishes once to a per-channel NATS subject that gateways subscribe to on behalf of their connected members. ScyllaDB change data capture streams every write into Kafka, which drives push notifications for users without a live session, unread counters, search indexing, and analytics.\n\nDelivery is at-least-once and ordered per conversation. Gateways push, clients acknowledge, and on reconnect the client sends its last sequence number per conversation and receives the gap from ScyllaDB, deduplicating by message id. Presence lives in Redis with TTLs and is published only to users who currently have that contact on screen, which caps the largest fan-out in the system. Each conversation is owned by one region's shard for sequencing, ScyllaDB replicates across data centers with local-quorum writes, and a NATS super-cluster carries delivery envelopes to gateways in any region. Media never touches the message path. Clients upload through presigned URLs to object storage and download through a CDN. Deploys drain gateways slowly and clients reconnect with jittered backoff, because a synchronized reconnect of 10 million sockets is the likeliest way this design fails. The sizing assumes AWS, roughly one message per user every few minutes, and mostly small conversations. If traffic is dominated by large groups, the fan-out and ScyllaDB write budget grow by orders of magnitude and the channel path becomes the main one. Three unknowns materially change the design. End-to-end encryption would move search and moderation to the client. The retention window sets storage cost. And whether brief cross-region double delivery during failover is acceptable decides if the session registry can stay regional.\n\n- **Edge:** Elixir with Phoenix Channels on the BEAM for gateways, AWS Network Load Balancer at Layer 4, Route 53 latency-based routing.\n- **Core services:** Go with gRPC for the message, presence, and fan-out services, sharded by conversation id.\n- **Real-time bus:** NATS with JetStream in a cross-region super-cluster.\n- **Durable log:** Apache Kafka fed by ScyllaDB change data capture.\n- **Message store:** ScyllaDB, multi-datacenter, partitioned by conversation id and time bucket.\n- **Sessions, presence, rate limits:** Redis Cluster with TTL keys.\n- **Accounts and membership:** PostgreSQL with per-region read replicas, cached in Redis.\n- **Push:** APNs and FCM through a Kafka consumer.\n- **Media:** S3 with presigned uploads, CloudFront for delivery.\n- **Auth:** OIDC identity provider issuing short-lived JWTs.\n- **Platform and observability:** Kubernetes for stateless tiers, Prometheus, Grafana, and OpenTelemetry tracing.",
