      "outputText": "A member sends a message, and every other member sees it within about one poll interval while their app is in the foreground. Conversation order and history are preserved, and absent members get a push hint. Run one stateless HTTP deployable, scaled by replica count, in front of one Valkey cluster that is the canonical authority for all chat state. The cluster is sharded from day one by hash tag on the owning key. Conversation id owns the message stream, the member set, and a send dedupe map. User id owns the inbox stream, the conversation-head sorted set, read cursors, a presence key with TTL, and push preferences. A send is one request. A Valkey function on the conversation shard checks membership, dedupes on the client message id, appends the event to the conversation stream, waits for replica acknowledgement, and returns the member list. The replica then appends a compact copy of the event to each member's inbox stream and upserts that member's conversation head. That fanout is bounded by the member cap, and the request is acknowledged only after it completes, so a client retry with the same id converges. A foreground client tails its own inbox with a bounded cursor read about every 200 ms, which is one owner-local read per poll. Polls refresh the presence key at a lower rate than they read. History pages come from the conversation stream by id range. Edits, deletes, reactions, read receipts, and membership changes are events in the same streams. For members whose presence key is absent, the send enqueues a BullMQ delayed job partitioned by recipient with a deterministic job id, so bursts debounce. The same app replicas process it. At execution a recipient-local Valkey function rechecks presence, last-read cursor, preferences, cooldown, and TTL-bounded push history, atomically claims the event, then sends to APNs or FCM or drops it. Push is a best-effort hint. The inbox is the authority. Attachment bytes go to object storage under a content key referenced by the message event. Local development and ten million concurrent users use identical component types and routing. Capacity grows only by adding shards and replicas.\n\nTen million concurrent is a population, not an envelope. Capacity stays unproven until per-shard stream read rate, message rate, group member cap, foreground ratio, and required retention are measured or decided. What the design proves is the scale function. Every poll and every send touches one owner shard, so shard count is polls per second divided by measured per-shard reads, and replica count follows request rate. Three failures matter. A conversation whose measured rate or member count exceeds one shard stays on one owner, and only then does fanout move into a leased BullMQ job on the same replicas. Idle polling is the dominant cost and does not shrink with message volume. The correction inside this topology is adaptive intervals that lengthen after empty polls and stop when backgrounded. Only a measured failure of that would justify a long-lived connection plane. Retention is memory-bound. Day one caps each conversation and inbox stream by length and age, with AOF persistence and one replica per shard, and a client whose cursor falls behind its inbox window resyncs from its conversation-head set. If the product requires unbounded history, a disk-backed store partitioned by the same conversation id takes the cold tail. That is the one unknown that would change the authority type.\n\n- **Stateless HTTP app replicas** for send, poll, history, and BullMQ processing. No dedicated worker deployment.\n- **Valkey Cluster** as the single authority: hash-tag sharding, one replica per shard, AOF persistence, no eviction. Streams for conversation and inbox logs, sorted sets for conversation heads, hashes for cursors and dedupe, TTL keys for presence, server-side functions for atomic owner-local logic.\n- **BullMQ on the same Valkey** for delayed, recipient-partitioned push evaluation with deterministic job ids.\n- **APNs and FCM** as best-effort push transports.\n- **Object storage** for attachment bytes, referenced by content key.\n- **Deliberately absent** until a measured trigger fires: message broker, WebSocket gateway, presence or notification services, cache layer, CQRS projections, and a relational message store.",
