      "messageSetId": "8cd9d527d1ca2eb978982b7f8e9170894a7813bd906495680c3ce4a524bd3b3c",
      "outputText": "**Recommendation: a stateless WebSocket gateway fleet in five regions, NATS for online routing, Kafka as the durable write-ahead log, and ScyllaDB as the source of truth for history.** Every client holds one long-lived WebSocket with binary protobuf frames to a Go gateway, reached through latency-based DNS and a regional Layer 4 network load balancer with TLS terminated on the gateway. Each gateway node holds about 200k connections with a load-tested ceiling of 400k, so 100 nodes spread over five regions give 20M capacity and the loss of a whole region still leaves room for 16M. Authentication is a short-lived signed JWT sent in the first frame and verified locally against cached keys plus a Redis revocation list, so connecting costs no RPC. Draining rolls a small slice of the fleet at a time and tells clients to reconnect with jitter, and reconnect storms are absorbed by client backoff, per-node admission control, and a cursor-based sync pull instead of a replay push. Routing needs no session registry: each gateway subscribes on a NATS supercluster to a per-user-device subject for every connection, and to a per-channel subject once for large channels, then fans out locally. Presence is a replicated per-region Redis copy fed by a NATS presence stream, and clients only subscribe to the users they are actually viewing. Typing indicators are ephemeral NATS publishes, throttled and disabled for large groups.\n\nOn the message path the gateway validates, rate-limits, dedups by client message id, assigns a Snowflake id, and produces to a region-local Kafka topic partitioned by conversation id. The client sees \"sent\" only after the Kafka ack. Three consumer groups then work independently: a persister writes ScyllaDB tables partitioned by conversation and time bucket plus a per-user inbox table, a router publishes once to NATS for online delivery, and a push service sends APNs and FCM notifications to offline devices with collapse rules. Ordering is guaranteed per conversation only. Delivery is at-least-once with client-side idempotency by message id, and every device keeps a per-conversation cursor so reconnect sync pulls from ScyllaDB through a history API. The socket push is an optimization and never the source of truth. Kafka is never mirrored across regions. NATS carries cross-region online delivery and ScyllaDB's multi-datacenter replication carries durability, so losing a region delays but does not lose committed messages. Media never touches the message path: clients upload straight to S3 with presigned URLs and download through CloudFront signed URLs. Six months of hot history stay in ScyllaDB and older data compacts to Parquet on S3.\n\nSizing assumptions behind the design:\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Gateways | 100 nodes at 32 vCPU / 64 GB, 20 per region |\n| Kafka | 6 brokers per region, 256 partitions, 3-day retention |\n| ScyllaDB | 3 datacenters, 60 NVMe nodes each, RF=3, LOCAL_QUORUM writes |\n| Redis | 12-shard cluster per region |\n| NATS | 3 core servers per region, no JetStream |\n| Delivery SLO | in-region p99 under 1s, sync p99 under 2s |\n\nCore technologies:\n\n- **Transport:** WebSocket over TLS 1.3 with protobuf frames, APNs and FCM for backgrounded mobile.\n- **Gateway:** Go service, one pod per node on Kubernetes, goroutine per connection.\n- **Load balancing:** Route53 latency routing plus AWS NLB per region, TLS on the gateway.\n- **Online routing and fan-out:** NATS core supercluster with per-user-device, per-channel, and presence subjects.\n- **Durable log:** Apache Kafka in KRaft mode, region-local, keyed by conversation id.\n- **Message history:** ScyllaDB multi-datacenter keyspace with time-bucketed partitions.\n- **Presence, sessions, dedup, rate limits:** Redis Cluster per region, treated as disposable.\n- **Relational metadata:** Aurora PostgreSQL with regional read replicas for accounts, contacts, device tokens.\n- **Media:** S3 presigned uploads and CloudFront signed URLs with lifecycle tiering.\n- **Observability:** Prometheus with Mimir, OpenTelemetry traces sampled by message id, Loki logs, per-region canary clients.\n\nAssumptions that would change the design if wrong:\n\n- **Message rate.** The sizing assumes a modest per-user send rate with fan-out dominated by small groups. A workload dominated by 100k-member channels shifts cost into NATS fan-out and daily-bucket hot partitions.\n- **AWS-only.** Every managed choice assumes AWS. A multi-cloud mandate replaces NLB, Route53, S3, and Aurora but leaves the core pattern intact.\n- **Region-local durability is acceptable.** If a product requirement demands zero delay on region loss, Kafka needs cross-region mirroring and the cost roughly doubles on that tier.\n- **Data residency.** Hard residency rules would force per-jurisdiction ScyllaDB datacenters instead of three global ones.",
