      "outputText": "Run one horizontally scalable stateless HTTP deployable in front of one Redis/Valkey Cluster that is the sole mutable authority, sharded from day one by domain owner. Every conversation lives on the shard for its hash tag and owns the message stream, the sequence counter, a TTL map from client message ID to sequence for idempotent retries, the member set, receipts, and ephemeral typing state. Every user lives on the shard for their hash tag and owns an append-only inbox stream, per-device cursors, a TTL presence key, sessions, preferences, and a conversation index. A send is one atomic function call on the conversation shard that dedups, assigns the next sequence, and appends, followed by a replica wait before the ack, then bounded pipelined appends into each member's inbox up to an inline fanout cap, then deterministic-ID push jobs. Active clients tail their own inbox with a bounded cursor GET about every 200 milliseconds. That single call also verifies the session, refreshes presence, and stores the cursor, so per-poll cost is constant regardless of how many conversations a user belongs to. Rooms above the inline cap switch to fanout-on-read from the conversation stream, so no single send ever writes more than the cap. Scrollback is one bounded range read inside the in-memory window. Older entries are sealed into immutable chunks in an S3-compatible object store by the same replicas when a sequence boundary is crossed, and media goes there through presigned URLs. The object store is a cold tier of the same log, not a second authority, and no relational database, broker, pub/sub fabric, WebSocket gateway tier, or worker fleet is needed.\n\nOut-of-app push is best-effort. Recipient-partitioned BullMQ queues on the same cluster hold delayed jobs whose deterministic IDs collapse bursts to one push per recipient per conversation per window. At execution a recipient-local function drops the job if presence is fresh, the read cursor is past the message, the conversation is muted, or a cooldown is active, and otherwise claims it atomically and calls APNs or FCM with bounded retries. Day one and full scale use identical component types and routing. Capacity grows only by adding shards, replicas, and app instances, because per-send work is bounded by membership and per-poll work is constant. The two material risks are a hot conversation whose measured load exceeds one shard, and acknowledged-write loss on simultaneous primary and replica failure. The first is handled by fanout-on-read plus replica reads for that room, and the second by the replica wait, every-second AOF fsync, and snapshots to the object store. Ten million concurrent users is a population total, not a load envelope. Message rate, group-size distribution, retention policy, durability SLO, freshness bound, and regional or compliance constraints remain unknown. They set shard counts, the inline fanout cap, and the memory window. Revisit the shape only if a durability SLO forbids any sub-second loss, a single room's measured load exceeds one shard, or a legal retention rule requires deletion guarantees the sealed-chunk model cannot give.\n\nCore technologies:\n\n- **Stateless HTTP application replicas** that also run push delivery and chunk sealing in-process, with no separate worker deployable.\n- **Redis/Valkey Cluster** with hash-tag sharding by conversation and by user, at least one replica per shard, no eviction, AOF fsync every second, and a replica wait before acking sends.\n- **Redis/Valkey server-side functions** for atomic per-shard operations covering dedup, sequencing, inbox tail with presence refresh, and push claim-or-drop.\n- **BullMQ** on the same cluster, partitioned by recipient with deterministic delayed job IDs, for best-effort push notifications.\n- **S3-compatible object store** for media via presigned URLs and for sealed immutable conversation history chunks and cluster snapshots.\n- **APNs and FCM** as external best-effort push providers.",
