      "outputText": "Build it as a stateless connection-gateway tier in front of a durable event log, with a separate routing layer that knows where every user is connected. Each client holds one long-lived WebSocket to a gateway behind a layer 4 load balancer. The gateway keeps only the socket-to-user mapping and a subscription to its own delivery channel. When a client sends a message, the gateway forwards it to a chat service that authenticates the sender, assigns a per-conversation sequence number, appends the message to a log partitioned by conversation, and acknowledges. Log consumers persist the message to a wide-column store keyed by conversation and clustered by sequence number, update unread counters, and emit a fan-out event. A routing service looks up each recipient in a presence map and publishes the message only to the specific gateways holding those users. Offline recipients get a push notification instead of a socket delivery. Large groups take a read-side path: the message is written once, and members fetch it on demand rather than receiving one push per member.\n\nDeploy active-active across at least three regions, pin each user to a home region so conversation ordering has a single writer, and replicate the log between regions asynchronously. Run everything on Kubernetes with autoscaling driven by connection count on gateways and consumer lag on the log. This design assumes one-to-one and small-group chats dominate, that the server can see plaintext under transport encryption, and that history is retained indefinitely. If end-to-end encryption, very large groups, or a hard read-your-writes cross-region guarantee are required, the fan-out and storage layers need different choices and should be revisited before build.\n\n- **Load balancing:** AWS Network Load Balancer or Envoy at layer 4, with connection draining for gateway rollouts.\n- **Connection gateways:** Go services using a low-allocation WebSocket library. About 200 pods at 50,000 connections each covers 10 million with headroom.\n- **Message log:** Apache Kafka, partitioned by conversation ID, with MirrorMaker 2 for cross-region replication.\n- **Message store:** ScyllaDB, partition key conversation ID, clustering key sequence number, with a time-to-live tier for ephemeral content.\n- **Presence and routing:** Redis Cluster holding user-to-gateway mappings with heartbeat expiry, and per-gateway pub/sub channels for delivery.\n- **Push notifications:** Worker pool feeding Apple Push Notification service and Firebase Cloud Messaging.\n- **Media:** Object storage such as S3 behind a CDN, with signed upload URLs so media bypasses the gateways.\n- **Authentication:** OpenID Connect provider issuing short-lived JSON Web Tokens validated at the gateway.\n- **Orchestration:** Kubernetes with horizontal pod autoscaling, plus latency-based DNS for regional steering.\n- **Observability:** OpenTelemetry tracing, Prometheus metrics, Grafana dashboards, with alerts on consumer lag and gateway connection churn.",
