      "messageSetId": "d26aca685ef17f59dfaf05eb77ed251049129af297fd3096cb16f8fef16501c7",
      "outputText": "**Recommended architecture.** The goal is that a user sends a message and every member of that conversation sees it in order within a freshness bound, with history that survives restarts and a push hint when they are away. Run one stateless HTTP deployable and one canonical authority: a replicated, disk-persisted Valkey cluster with eviction disabled, every key hash-tagged by its owner from day one. Conversation is the owner of message truth. A send appends to that conversation's stream using a client-generated message id deduplicated by a short per-conversation ledger, receives the assigned sequence, then appends a small pointer to each member's inbox log and advances a fanout watermark on the conversation shard. User is the owner of delivery. A client tails its own inbox log with one cursor GET against one shard about every 200 ms while foregrounded, backs off when idle, and reads any unseen range of the named conversation stream. That same GET refreshes a TTL presence key. Typing indicators and read receipts are TTL hashes on the conversation shard, read only for the conversation currently open. Push is a recipient-partitioned BullMQ job with a deterministic id, delayed a few seconds, executed by ordinary app replicas. A recipient-local Valkey function checks presence, inbox cursor, preferences, and cooldown, claims the event atomically, then sends through APNs or FCM or drops it. The inbox log is the authority and push is only a hint. Aged stream entries are trimmed into a disk-backed archive keyed by the same conversation id and used only for scrollback. Media bytes go to object storage and the message carries the reference.\n\n**Envelope, scaling, and what breaks.** Ten million concurrent users is a population, not an envelope. The active fraction, message rate, largest group size, and retention period are unknown, so capacity is unproven and only the scale function is claimed. Every path is bounded per owner. A send costs one conversation append plus one pointer per member, a poll costs one key read, and scrollback costs one range read, so capacity grows by adding Valkey shards and HTTP replicas with no change in topology between day one and final. Each retained part survived a collapse test. The inbox log stays because computing which of a user's conversations changed on every poll is fanout on read across every membership. The archive stays only under the assumption of indefinite retention, and should be deleted if retention is bounded to what fits in memory. BullMQ exists only for delayed push evaluation and scheduled sends, never for message truth. Three failures matter. Poll traffic dominates cost at scale, so intervals adapt to activity and backgrounded clients rely on push wake. The revisit trigger is measured read cost, and the first response is serving the same inbox log through a blocking long poll rather than adding a connection plane. A replica dying between the conversation append and the pointer fanout delays hints but loses nothing, because the client retry with the same message id and the fanout watermark re-drive the gap from the authority. A single conversation whose measured rate exceeds one shard, or a membership too large to fan out within the send latency bound, is the only condition that would force a background fanout or a split of that owner.\n\nCore technologies:\n\n- **Stateless HTTP application:** one deployable, scaled purely by replica count, owning send, poll, history, presence, and push execution as modules.\n- **Valkey or Redis Cluster:** canonical authority with replication, append-only-file persistence, and no eviction. Streams hold conversation and inbox logs, TTL keys and hashes hold presence, typing, and receipts, and server-side functions perform atomic claims and dedup.\n- **BullMQ on the same Valkey cluster:** recipient-partitioned with deterministic job ids, used only for delayed push evaluation and scheduled sends.\n- **Disk-backed archive partitioned by conversation id:** any store that serves ordered range reads by conversation and sequence, receiving trimmed stream tails for scrollback.\n- **Object storage:** holds media bytes referenced from messages.\n- **APNs and FCM:** best-effort push hints, subordinate to the in-app inbox log.",
