      "outputText": "For 10M concurrent users, I'd recommend a horizontally sharded WebSocket gateway tier backed by a pub/sub message fabric and a log-structured message store. Roughly 200–400 stateless gateway nodes (each holding ~30–50K persistent connections) terminate WebSocket/TLS behind an L4 load balancer, authenticate via short-lived JWTs, and register each connection in a Redis-based session/presence registry so any node can look up where a user is connected. Messages flow from a gateway into Kafka (partitioned by conversation ID for ordering), where consumer services handle fan-out: for each recipient, they consult the presence registry and publish to a per-node Redis Pub/Sub or NATS channel that the recipient's gateway subscribes to, delivering the message over the existing socket. Offline recipients get the message queued for pull-on-reconnect plus a push notification via APNs/FCM. Messages are persisted asynchronously from Kafka into ScyllaDB/Cassandra partitioned by conversation ID and time-bucketed to avoid hot partitions, giving durable history reads independent of the delivery path. Large groups (>10K members) switch from per-recipient fan-out-on-write to fan-out-on-read against the conversation log to cap write amplification.\n\nRun it across 3+ availability zones on Kubernetes with the gateway tier autoscaled on connection count, and shard the presence registry with Redis Cluster. Ordering is per-conversation (Kafka partition + server-assigned sequence numbers), delivery is at-least-once with client-side dedup on message IDs, and read receipts/typing indicators go through a cheaper ephemeral pub/sub path that skips persistence entirely. This design keeps every tier independently scalable and tolerates the loss of any single node or AZ without dropping durable messages — only live sockets, which clients transparently reconnect.\n\n**Core technologies:**\n- **Gateway/connection tier:** Elixir/Erlang (Phoenix Channels) or Go WebSocket servers — both handle 50K+ connections per node comfortably\n- **Load balancing:** AWS NLB or Envoy at L4 for WebSocket passthrough\n- **Message backbone:** Apache Kafka, partitioned by conversation ID\n- **Presence & session routing:** Redis Cluster (with NATS as a lighter-weight alternative for node-to-node delivery)\n- **Message storage:** ScyllaDB or Cassandra, time-bucketed partitions per conversation\n- **Metadata (users, memberships):** PostgreSQL (sharded via Citus if needed)\n- **Push notifications:** APNs/FCM via a dedicated notification service consuming from Kafka\n- **Auth:** Short-lived JWTs issued by a central auth service\n- **Orchestration & ops:** Kubernetes, Prometheus/Grafana for metrics, autoscaling on connection count and Kafka consumer lag\n- **Media:** S3 + CDN (CloudFront) with presigned uploads, keeping blobs out of the message path",
