      "outputText": "**Recommendation.** Run one horizontally scalable stateless HTTP deployable in front of one canonical data plane: a replicated, disk-persisted (AOF) Redis/Valkey cluster sharded from day 1 by a stable owner hash tag — `conversation_id` for message logs and membership, `user_id` for each user's inbox index, presence, and read cursors. Sending a message is one synchronous request that atomically appends to the conversation's append-only log with a client-supplied request ID (same-key upsert, so retries converge) and updates each member's inbox pointer on their shard (group fanout is bounded by membership size). Clients receive messages by tailing their conversation and inbox logs with bounded cursor GETs roughly every 200 ms while active — no WebSocket fleet, no connection registry, no broker, no separate delivery service. The only asynchronous path is out-of-app push for offline recipients: a recipient-partitioned BullMQ queue on the same Redis cluster, where at execution a recipient-local atomic function checks presence, last-read cursor, preferences, and cooldowns, claims the event, then sends or drops; push is a best-effort hint and the in-app log stays authoritative. At 10M concurrent users the load is ~50M cursor reads/sec, which is intrinsically bounded per user and scales purely by shard count (roughly 500–800 Redis shards at ~100k ops/s each, plus HTTP replicas behind a balancer); vFinal is the same graph with higher counts. Explicit unknowns preserved: message retention and group-size ceilings are unsourced — if retention outgrows memory economics, tier cold history to a disk store keyed by the same `conversation_id` (a storage tier, not a new topology), and if a single conversation's measured load ever exceeds one shard, revisit fanout for that owner only.\n\nEvery plane pays rent: the Redis cluster is forced because chat is shared mutable state with a monotonic per-conversation order; BullMQ push is forced because notifying an offline user is work the request cannot complete; everything else (Kafka, a presence service, a message-history service, CQRS projections, per-connection gateways) fails the collapse test — the sourced contract is served without them.\n\n**Core technologies:**\n- **Stateless HTTP application tier** — one deployable, scaled by replica count; owns send, read-tail, membership, and auth logic as modules, not services.\n- **Redis/Valkey cluster (replicated, AOF persistence, no eviction)** — single canonical authority; per-conversation append-only message logs, per-user inbox/read-cursor/presence keys, all partitioned by owner hash tag.\n- **BullMQ on the same Redis cluster** — recipient-partitioned queue solely for out-of-app push delivery with deterministic job IDs and idempotent handlers.\n- **APNs/FCM** — best-effort push transport; the in-app log remains the source of truth for delivery.",
