      "outputText": "# Chat at 10M Concurrent: Recommended Architecture\n\nRun one horizontally scalable stateless deployable behind ordinary load balancing, with a sharded, replicated, disk-persistent (AOF) Redis/Valkey cluster as the single canonical authority — partitioned by a stable owner key: `{conversation_id}` for message logs and `{user_id}` for each user's inbox index, presence, and read cursors. Sending a message is one synchronous request that atomically appends to the conversation's append-only log (a Redis stream with a client-supplied request ID for idempotent retry) and updates each participant's inbox pointer on their shard; group fanout is bounded by membership size. Clients receive messages by tailing their conversation/inbox logs with bounded cursor GETs (~200 ms while active) over plain HTTP — no WebSocket tier, no broker, no separate presence or delivery service. Push notifications are a best-effort hint: a recipient-partitioned BullMQ queue (on the same Redis plane) evaluates delivery with one recipient-local atomic check of presence, last-read state, preferences, and cooldowns, then sends or drops; the in-app log stays authoritative. Day 1 and 10M-concurrent use identical topology — scale is purely more app replicas and more Redis shards under the same hash-tag routing, since every request touches only one conversation shard plus bounded participant shards. Assumption to state plainly: capacity per shard is unproven until measured; a hot conversation (e.g., a 100k-member channel) exceeding one shard's write or fanout budget is the trigger to revisit — with per-channel fanout batching, not a new plane. Media attachments go to blob storage with only the reference in the log; long-tail history beyond the hot retention window compacts to a disk-backed archive table read on demand, same ownership key, no change to the live path.\n\nCore technologies:\n- **Stateless HTTP app tier** (any runtime) — all chat, presence, and cursor logic as modules; scales by replica count.\n- **Redis/Valkey cluster, replicated, AOF persistence, no eviction** — canonical authority for message streams, inbox indexes, presence, read state, and idempotency keys; sharded by `{conversation_id}` / `{user_id}` hash tags from day 1.\n- **BullMQ on the same Redis plane** — recipient-partitioned push-notification evaluation with deterministic job IDs; no separate worker deployment.\n- **Blob storage** for media; **disk-backed archive store** (e.g., the relational store the stack already owns) for cold history, same partition key.\n\nExplicitly absent, by design: no Kafka/pub-sub broker, no WebSocket gateway fleet, no separate presence/delivery/notification services, no CQRS or projection layer — each was collapsed because bounded cursor tailing and owner-sharded atomic writes satisfy the delivery contract without them.",
