      "outputText": "**Recommendation: a cell-based, gateway + pub/sub architecture.** Terminate the 10M WebSocket connections on a horizontally scaled fleet of stateless Go gateway servers behind L4 load balancers — at a conservative ~100–150k connections per node, that's roughly 100 pods with headroom, autoscaled on Kubernetes. Gateways hold no business logic: they authenticate the socket (JWT), register the user in a Redis-backed presence/session registry, and subscribe to NATS subjects for that user's channels. When a message is sent, a chat service validates it, appends it to Kafka (the durable, ordered source of truth, partitioned by channel ID), and publishes it to NATS for sub-100ms fanout to whichever gateways hold the recipients' connections. Downstream Kafka consumers handle persistence into ScyllaDB (message history, partitioned by channel + time bucket, the proven pattern for trillions of messages), push notifications to offline users via APNs/FCM, and search indexing. Relational metadata — accounts, channel membership, permissions — lives in sharded Postgres (Citus), fronted by Redis caches; media goes to S3 behind a CDN.\n\nPartition the whole system into independent cells of ~500k–1M users (each cell = its own gateways, NATS, Kafka partitions, and Scylla keyspace), with a thin global routing layer mapping users to cells. This bounds blast radius, makes capacity planning linear, and gives you multi-region for free by pinning cells to users' home regions. Two unknowns will materially shape the design and should be nailed down early: maximum channel size (channels above ~10k members need lazy/pull-based fanout instead of push-per-recipient) and data-residency requirements (which constrain cell placement and cross-region replication).\n\n**Core technologies:**\n- **Go** — WebSocket gateway fleet and chat services\n- **AWS NLB / Envoy** — L4 load balancing and connection routing\n- **NATS** — low-latency real-time pub/sub fanout to gateways\n- **Apache Kafka** — durable ordered message log; feeds persistence, push, and search consumers\n- **ScyllaDB** — message history store, partitioned by channel and time bucket\n- **PostgreSQL (Citus)** — sharded users, channels, membership, permissions\n- **Redis Cluster** — presence/session registry and hot metadata cache\n- **Kubernetes** — orchestration and autoscaling of all fleets\n- **APNs / FCM** — offline push notifications\n- **S3 + CloudFront** — media storage and delivery\n- **Prometheus / Grafana + OpenTelemetry** — metrics and distributed tracing",
