      "outputText": "## Recommended architecture\n\nRun one horizontally scalable deployable: a stateless HTTP app tier in front of two sharded authorities, with the same topology on day 1 and at 10M concurrent — you scale by adding shards and replicas, never by changing shape. Durable truth (conversations, membership, message history) lives in a replicated disk-backed relational store sharded by `conversation_id`. Delivery is a per-user append-only log in a replicated, disk-persisted, no-eviction Redis/Valkey cluster partitioned by a `user_id` hash tag: on send, the app writes the message to the conversation shard in one transaction (idempotent by client-generated message ID), then appends a compact copy to each member's log — fanout is bounded by a group-size cap, so it completes inside the request. Active clients read by polling their own log with a bounded cursor GET every ~200 ms; each poll touches exactly one owner shard, so read load is deterministic: 10M concurrent × ~5 reads/s ≈ 50M ops/s, served by ~512–1024 user-log shards at 50–100k ops/s each, with the HTTP tier scaling by replica count since it holds no connection state. No WebSocket fleet, message broker, fanout workers, or presence service is needed: presence is a TTL key on the user's own shard refreshed by polling, and if a recipient's presence key is absent at send time, the app enqueues a best-effort push job on that recipient's shard (BullMQ on the same Redis), which re-checks presence and last-read cursor atomically before sending — the in-app log stays the authority, push is a hint.\n\nFailure contract: sends acknowledge only after the conversation-shard write commits; a failed log append is retried by message ID, and any gap is repaired on the client's next history sync from the conversation authority, so delivery converges without a reconciliation service. Hot groups stay bounded by the membership cap; a shard that outgrows its box splits by reassigning hash slots, not by new components. Unknowns preserved: per-user send rate and media handling aren't specified (media would add only a blob store, referenced by ID); the design would need revisiting only if the contract demanded sub-100 ms delivery or uncapped broadcast channels, which would force server push and async fanout for those paths.\n\n**Core technologies**\n\n- **Stateless HTTP app tier** — all send, poll, presence, and push logic; scales purely by replica count.\n- **Redis/Valkey cluster** (replicated, AOF-persisted, no eviction), partitioned by `user_id` hash tag — per-user delivery logs, presence TTL keys, and recipient-partitioned BullMQ push jobs on the same shards.\n- **Sharded replicated relational store** (e.g., Postgres), partitioned by `conversation_id` — canonical message history and membership; the transaction and idempotency boundary for every send.",
