      "outputText": "**Recommended architecture.** One stateless HTTP deployable in front of two sharded authorities, with the same topology on day 1 and at 10M concurrent — capacity grows only by adding shards and replicas. Canonical message history lives in a replicated disk-backed store sharded by `conversation_id`: sending a message is one synchronous request that writes the message row with the client's request ID (idempotent retry converges on the same row), then appends the message pointer to each recipient's inbox log — a per-user, replicated, no-eviction Valkey append-only log partitioned by `user_id` hash tag. Recipients read by tailing their own inbox with bounded cursor GETs (~200 ms while the app is foregrounded, backing off when idle), and fetch scrollback history straight from the conversation shard. Delivery receipts, last-read cursors, and presence are TTL'd keys on the same recipient shard, updated as a side effect of the polls themselves — no WebSocket gateway tier, no presence service, no broker. Offline push is best-effort: a recipient-partitioned BullMQ job runs a recipient-local Valkey function that checks presence, last-read state, preferences, and cooldowns, atomically claims the event, and calls APNs/FCM; the inbox log remains the authority for what the user actually sees.\n\n**Why this holds at 10M concurrent.** Every hot path is bounded per action (one conversation shard write plus N recipient appends for group size N; one shard GET per poll), so load distributes across user-hash and conversation-hash shards with no cross-shard fanout: worst-case ~50M cursor GETs/sec is served by ~300–500 Valkey shards at ~100–150K ops/sec each, and the stateless tier scales by replica count. The known unknowns don't change the topology, only shard counts: message rate and retention size the conversation store; the one invalidating condition is very large broadcast channels (tens of thousands of members), where per-recipient append fanout exceeds a single request — those conversations flip to fan-out-on-read from the conversation shard, still inside the same components. No Kafka, no dedicated delivery workers, no CQRS: due work (push) leases from its queue in bounded batches, and everything else is compute-on-read against the owner shard.\n\n**Core technologies:**\n- **Stateless HTTP app tier** — the only deployable; scales horizontally by replica count.\n- **Replicated disk-backed message store sharded by `conversation_id`** (e.g., partitioned Postgres) — canonical authority for message history and idempotent send.\n- **Valkey/Redis Cluster, hash-tagged by `user_id`, AOF persistence, no eviction** — per-user inbox logs, read cursors, presence, and cooldown state; the delivery plane.\n- **BullMQ on the same Valkey shards** — recipient-partitioned offline-push jobs with deterministic job IDs; APNs/FCM as best-effort hints only.",
