      "outputText": "## Architecture\n\nRun a three-tier system: a **connection tier**, a **routing tier**, and a **storage tier**, with conversation ownership as the sharding key throughout. The connection tier is a fleet of stateless-ish Go gateway processes behind L4 (not L7) load balancers with TCP/TLS termination at the gateway itself; each node holds ~150k WebSocket connections against a tuned epoll event loop and ~4–6 GB of connection state, so 10M concurrent users lands at roughly 70 gateways plus 40% headroom for rolling restarts and AZ loss — call it 100 nodes across 3 AZs. Gateways do nothing but auth (validate a short-lived JWT on connect), framing, backpressure, and delivery; they hold no business logic, which keeps deploys cheap and lets you drain a node by sending a `reconnect` frame with jittered backoff rather than dropping 150k sockets at once. Each gateway registers its connections in a Redis Cluster session registry (`user_id → {gateway_id, session_id, seq}`, TTL-refreshed by heartbeat), which is the only global lookup on the hot path.\n\nMessage flow: a client publishes to its gateway, which writes to Kafka partitioned by `conversation_id` — this single choice buys you per-conversation total ordering for free, which is the property that actually makes chat correct. A stateful **chat service** consumes those partitions (partition-affine, so one owner per conversation at a time), assigns a Snowflake-style monotonic `message_id`, writes to ScyllaDB keyed `((conversation_id, time_bucket), message_id)`, then resolves recipients and pushes delivery instructions onto a per-gateway Redis Stream that each gateway tails. Use **fanout-on-write** for DMs and groups under ~500 members, and **fanout-on-read** above that (large rooms get a shared per-room ring buffer in Redis; clients pull deltas), because writing 100k rows per message to a broadcast room will melt Scylla long before the gateways sweat. Offline users get a per-user unread cursor rather than a per-user copy of the message; push notifications go out through a separate Kafka topic to an APNs/FCM worker pool so a provider outage never backs up the delivery path. Clients reconnect with `(conversation_id, last_seen_message_id)` and replay from Scylla, which makes at-least-once delivery plus client-side dedupe on `message_id` sufficient — do not attempt exactly-once. Presence is deliberately weak: gateways aggregate heartbeats into 10-second batched deltas and publish coarse online/offline state, since naive presence fanout is the classic way a chat system dies at this scale (a 10M-user system with 200 contacts each is 2B edge updates per interval otherwise).\n\nUnknowns that would materially change this: whether you need **E2EE** (kills server-side search and fanout-on-write of plaintext, forces per-device key fanout and roughly doubles gateway state), whether users are **single-region or global** (global means regional cells with conversation home-region pinning and cross-region Kafka MirrorMaker, adding ~100ms to cross-region DMs), and your **retention policy** (unbounded history changes Scylla capacity by an order of magnitude and likely adds a cold tier). I've assumed a single-region-primary, non-E2EE, 1-year-retention deployment with mostly DM and small-group traffic.\n\n## Core technologies\n\n- **Edge/connections:** Go gateways (`gobwas/ws` or `nhooyr/websocket`), L4 load balancing via AWS NLB or Envoy in TCP mode; WebSocket with a binary protobuf frame format\n- **Session registry & ephemeral state:** Redis Cluster (session map, per-gateway delivery streams, large-room ring buffers, presence aggregation)\n- **Message bus:** Kafka, partitioned by `conversation_id`, ~2000 partitions, RF=3, `acks=all`\n- **Chat/routing service:** Go or Java, partition-affine consumers with cooperative rebalancing\n- **Message store:** ScyllaDB (Cassandra-compatible), `((conversation_id, time_bucket), message_id)`, RF=3, LOCAL_QUORUM\n- **Metadata store:** PostgreSQL (users, group membership, device registry) with PgBouncer and read replicas\n- **ID generation:** Snowflake-style 64-bit IDs, time-ordered, per-node worker IDs\n- **Push:** dedicated Kafka topic → worker pool → APNs / FCM\n- **Media:** direct-to-S3 via presigned URLs, CDN-fronted; never through the WebSocket path\n- **Coordination:** etcd for gateway membership and shard assignment\n- **Observability:** Prometheus + Grafana, OpenTelemetry tracing, with connection count, p99 delivery latency, and Kafka consumer lag as the three page-worthy SLIs\n- **Deploys:** Kubernetes for chat/routing services; gateways on dedicated instances with long connection-drain windows",
