      "outputText": "## Recommendation\n\nServe chat from **one stateless HTTP deployable and one canonical authority: a Redis/Valkey Cluster sharded from day 1 by hash tag**, with two key families — `{conv:<id>}` owning each conversation's append-only message stream, and `{user:<id>}` owning that user's conversation index, last-read cursors, preferences, presence, and TTL-bounded push history. A send is a single-shard atomic append (Redis function: assign monotonic stream ID, dedupe on client-supplied `request_id` via same-key upsert, update conversation head) and returns the committed message ID synchronously; the sender's success is that ID, not a delivery receipt. Readers do not hold connections: an active client issues bounded cursor `XRANGE`-style GETs from its last-read ID roughly every 200 ms for the open conversation and every 2–3 s for its conversation index, so read cost is a function of *foreground-active* clients, not connected ones. Recipient fanout is the one forced async path — group fanout is bounded but exceeds the sender's latency budget, and push providers are unreliable — so the append enqueues **recipient-partitioned BullMQ jobs on the same cluster with deterministic job IDs** (`msg:<id>:<recipient>`); each job runs one recipient-local function that appends the conversation pointer, bumps unread, checks presence/last-read/preferences/cooldown/push history, atomically claims the notification, and then sends to APNs/FCM. Push is a best-effort hint; the in-app log is the authority, and a dropped push costs nothing because the next cursor GET reconciles.\n\nDay 1 and vFinal are the same component graph — stateless app replicas, one Redis/Valkey Cluster, BullMQ queues inside those same replicas — differing only in shard and replica counts. With 10 M concurrent users and the stated assumption that ~15 % are foreground-active (200 ms polls) and the rest poll every ~3 s, steady read load is ≈10 M cursor GETs/s, which lands around **128 primaries with 1–2 replicas each** (~150 k ops/s per shard, headroom for hot group skew) and **~400 app replicas** at ~25 k rps each; growth is met by adding shards and reassigning hash-tag slots under the same routing contract. Message *write* rate and history retention are unknown, so capacity there is unproven: persistence is AOF `everysec` + replica + `maxmemory-policy noeviction`, and if measured retention × volume makes memory cost dominate, the single bounded correction is shard-local sealing of cold stream segments to object storage, addressed by the same conversation key with Redis remaining authoritative for the hot tail — routing, ownership, and failure contract unchanged. I explicitly reject a stateful WebSocket gateway fleet, Kafka, and a separate message database: each requires a presence/routing directory, a second authority, or replay machinery whose only forcing requirement is its own existence, and the 200 ms cursor GET already meets the human-perceptible chat latency contract. Top risks: (1) a very large or celebrity group makes one conversation shard hot — cap group size and shard-split only that conversation's stream when its measured load exceeds one shard, never preemptively; (2) fanout backlog delays unread counts while messages are already durable — surface unread as eventually-consistent, with the conversation log as the authority the client can always tail directly; (3) an AOF-window loss on primary failover can drop an acknowledged tail — acknowledge only after `WAIT 1 <timeout>` for chats where the sender's receipt must survive failover.\n\n**Core technologies**\n\n- Stateless HTTP application replicas (no long-lived connections, no per-user affinity)\n- Redis/Valkey Cluster as canonical authority: append-only streams per conversation, hashes/sorted sets per user; hash-tag partitioned by `{conv:<id>}` and `{user:<id>}`; AOF `everysec`, `noeviction`, ≥1 replica per primary\n- Redis/Valkey functions (Lua) for atomic append-with-dedupe and atomic notification claim\n- BullMQ on the same cluster, recipient-partitioned, deterministic job IDs, retry-safe handlers, running in the ordinary app replicas\n- APNs / FCM as best-effort out-of-app hints only\n- Object storage for sealed cold conversation segments — only if measured retention cost forces it",
