      "outputText": "## Recommendation\n\nRun one stateless HTTP deployable in front of one sharded, replicated, disk-backed Redis/Valkey cluster that is the canonical authority for chat. Every key carries a hash tag of its owning domain key: conversation state (`{conv:<id>}`) holds the append-only message log as a Stream plus a small hash of conversation metadata; per-user state (`{user:<id>}`) holds the inbox log, per-conversation last-read cursors, presence with TTL, and a TTL-bounded push-dedupe history. Send is a single owner-local call: one Lua/Redis function on the conversation shard appends the message with a deterministic ID derived from the client's request ID (same-key upsert, so retries converge without arbitration) and returns the assigned stream ID; a second recipient-partitioned append writes the inbox pointer for each member. Receive is a bounded cursor GET — `XRANGE` from the client's last-seen ID, capped page size, polled about every 200 ms while the app is foregrounded and backing off when idle. That poll is what makes delivery feel live; there are no long-lived sockets to place, no connection registry, no pub/sub fanout plane, and therefore no reconnect-storm or presence-drift failure mode to repair. Out-of-app push is best-effort: recipient-partitioned BullMQ jobs on the same Redis/Valkey evaluate presence, last-read, preferences, and cooldown at execution time, atomically claim the event, then send or drop. The in-app log is the authority; push is a hint.\n\nDay 1 and vFinal are the same component graph — stateless HTTP replicas, one Redis/Valkey cluster, one BullMQ namespace — differing only in shard count, replica count, and partition assignment. 10M concurrent users at a ~200 ms cursor cadence is roughly 50M reads/sec of bounded, owner-local `XRANGE` work; that is a shard-count problem, not a topology problem, because no request touches more than one conversation shard plus the sender's user shard, and fanout per send is bounded by group size. Large groups are the one real skew: cap membership (e.g. 1000) so a send stays bounded, and for anything larger switch that conversation to pull-only — members tail the conversation stream directly instead of receiving inbox writes, which removes the fanout entirely rather than adding a plane to absorb it. Collapse tests: a broker is deleted because due work leases from BullMQ on the existing authority; a second store is deleted because Redis/Valkey Streams with `MAXLEN`/`MINID` trimming plus AOF `everysec` and replica failover meet the retention and recovery contract; a separate socket/gateway service is deleted because polling needs no connection ownership; a projection is deleted because reads are already an indexed range scan on the write path's own structure. Unproven capacity, stated plainly: the sourced envelope is concurrency only — message rate per user, history retention beyond trim, and media size are unknown. If retention must be unbounded-and-searchable, or if per-user message rate makes AOF write volume exceed what a shard's disk sustains, add a Postgres archive fed by a trailing cursor over the same streams (derived, not authoritative) — that is the trigger, and nothing else here changes.\n\n- **Stateless HTTP application replicas** — one deployable, all send/receive/read-cursor logic as modules\n- **Redis/Valkey, sharded by hash tag, replicated, AOF `everysec`** — canonical authority; Streams for message and inbox logs, hashes for cursors/metadata, TTL keys for presence and push dedupe, Redis functions for atomic append-and-claim\n- **BullMQ on the same Redis/Valkey, partitioned by recipient** — scheduled and out-of-app push evaluation, deterministic job IDs, retry-safe handlers\n- **APNs / FCM** — best-effort push transport only\n- **Object storage (S3-compatible) with presigned direct upload** — media bytes; only the key lands in the message",
