      "outputText": "## Recommended architecture\n\nRun a stateless-logic, stateful-connection design: a thin WebSocket gateway tier in Go that holds the sockets, and a subject-addressed message bus that moves messages between gateways. Terminate TLS at L4 (anycast NLB) and hash connections across ~200 gateway pods at 50k sockets each — 8 vCPU / 16 GB per pod, tuned for `epoll` with ~40 KB of per-connection state — deployed across three regions with GeoDNS steering users to their nearest edge. Clients authenticate once at connect with a short-lived JWT; the gateway writes `user_id → {gateway_id, conn_id}` into a Redis Cluster session registry with a heartbeat-renewed TTL, which doubles as the presence store. Sends go gateway → gRPC to a stateless message service, which assigns a per-channel monotonic ULID sequence, writes to ScyllaDB, then publishes to NATS JetStream on subject `chat.<channel_id>`. The critical scaling trick is per-node subscription dedup: a gateway subscribes to a channel subject **once** regardless of how many of its 50k local users are in that channel, then fans out in-process over its local socket table. At an assumed 1 message per user per 30s (~330k msg/s ingest) with 10x fanout amplification, this keeps bus traffic near ingest rate instead of ~3.3M deliveries/s, and caps large-channel cost at one subscription per node rather than per member.\n\nFor durability and correctness, ScyllaDB is the message of record, partitioned by `(channel_id, time_bucket)` and clustered by ULID so history reads are a single-partition range scan; JetStream retains ~5 minutes per subject purely as a reconnect replay buffer. Clients track a last-seen sequence per channel, so reconnect is: resubscribe, replay from JetStream if the gap is inside retention, otherwise backfill via the history API — and every send carries a client-generated nonce so retries dedupe idempotently. User/channel/membership metadata lives in CockroachDB for multi-region reads; read cursors and unread counts live in Redis with periodic flush to Scylla. Offline delivery forks off a Kafka topic into a notification service fronting APNs/FCM, decided by the session registry showing no live connection. Two operational hazards dominate at this size and must be designed for up front: deploys of the gateway tier need staggered, slow connection draining with server-assigned jittered reconnect backoff, or you get a 10M-socket thundering herd; and every outbound socket needs a bounded queue with an explicit drop-and-force-resync policy so one slow mobile client cannot balloon gateway memory. Media never touches the message path — presigned S3 uploads, and messages carry only the CDN URL.\n\n**Core technologies**\n\n- **Connection tier:** Go WebSocket gateways (`gobwas/ws` or `nbio`), ~200 pods × 50k connections, 3 regions\n- **Load balancing:** Anycast + AWS NLB (L4, PROXY protocol) with GeoDNS region steering\n- **Message bus:** NATS JetStream — subject-per-channel, per-node subscription dedup, ~5 min replay retention\n- **Session registry / presence:** Redis Cluster, TTL-based with heartbeat renewal\n- **Message store:** ScyllaDB — partition `(channel_id, time_bucket)`, clustered by ULID\n- **Metadata store:** CockroachDB (users, channels, membership, multi-region)\n- **Internal RPC:** gRPC + Protobuf for gateway ↔ message service\n- **Offline/push pipeline:** Kafka → notification service → APNs / FCM\n- **Media:** S3 + CloudFront with presigned uploads, out of band from the message path\n- **Ordering/idempotency:** server-assigned per-channel ULID sequence; client nonce for dedupe\n- **Orchestration:** Kubernetes with long-drain `preStop` hooks and staggered rollouts for the gateway tier\n- **Observability:** OpenTelemetry → VictoriaMetrics + Grafana; per-connection queue depth and fanout lag as primary SLIs",
