      "outputText": "## Recommendation\n\nRun **one stateless HTTP deployable against one Redis/Valkey Cluster authority, sharded from day 1 by two stable hash tags — `{c:<conversation_id>}` and `{u:<user_id>}`** — with no gateway fleet, connection registry, broker, or read-model service. A send is a single Redis Function on the conversation's shard: it dedupes on the client-supplied message ID (returning the prior sequence on retry), `INCR`s `{c:X}:seq`, and appends the body to the `{c:X}:log` stream — one writer per conversation gives total order for free. It then enqueues one deterministic BullMQ job per recipient (`fanout:<msg_id>:<user_id>`) that performs a monotonic `max()` upsert of `(conversation_id → latest_seq)` into `{u:Y}:inbox` on that recipient's own shard; the upsert is idempotent, so retries converge without CAS or a uniqueness ledger. Clients read by **bounded cursor GET against their own user shard only** — one call returns which conversations advanced past `{u:Y}:read`, and the open conversation's bodies come from one ranged read of its `{c:X}:log`. Badge counts are bounded compute-on-read (`latest_seq > read_seq` over the inbox ZSET), so there is no unread projection to keep consistent. Cadence is adaptive: ~200 ms for the foreground-open thread, ~1.5 s for foreground-idle inbox, nothing in background. Fanout is capped at **1,000 members**; above that, members poll the conversation shard directly instead of receiving pointers. Backgrounded delivery reuses the same plane — a recipient-partitioned BullMQ job runs a Redis Function on `{u:Y}` that checks presence TTL, `read_seq`, preferences, cooldown, and TTL-bounded push history, atomically claims the event, then hands it to APNs/FCM. Push is a best-effort hint; the in-app log is the authority. Attachments and **cold history** are the only things leaving Redis: past the hot window, the conversation shard seals segments into immutable object-store blobs and keeps a tiny `(seq_start → object_key)` index plus an `archived_below_seq` boundary, so exactly one authority owns any sequence range and scrollback is a ranged GET. That deletes the archive database entirely.\n\nDay 1 and vFinal are the same graph — stateless replicas, one Redis/Valkey Cluster, one object store — differing only in counts. Under the assumed active mix (~10% foreground-open, ~40% idle, ~50% background) 10M concurrent produces ~7–8M reads/s, sized at **~128 shards** and ~200 replicas; the pessimal case where all 10M poll at 200 ms is 50M reads/s at **~512 shards**, reached by resharding within the same routing contract, not by changing topology. Three failures dominate. **(1) Poll amplification** if the idle population is larger than assumed — correction: long-poll the inbox with a ≤10 s hold, woken by a per-shard pub/sub hint that each replica subscribes to once (≈1k subscribers per shard, not per user); the hint is advisory and the cursor GET stays authoritative, so a dropped hint costs ≤10 s, and the 200 ms path is retained only for the small open-thread population. **(2) Hot-entity skew** from a broadcast channel exceeding one shard — correction: serve its reads from that shard's replicas; do not subshard an owner or add a placement directory. **(3) Acknowledged-write loss** in the AOF-everysec window during failover — correction: require replica acknowledgment before returning \"sent,\" and let the client retry on the same message ID into the dedupe path. Unknowns preserved: message rate, conversation-size distribution, retention target, and **region topology** — this assumes single-region; a global or data-residency requirement is satisfied by making home region part of the routing key, which adds shards, not component types. Revisit if measured cost per delivered message at target cadence exceeds a connection-plane's operating cost, or if a sub-100 ms delivery SLO is sourced. Cross-archive search is out of scope until a recall target exists; hot-window search fits a per-conversation index on the same shard.\n\n**Core technologies**\n\n- **Redis/Valkey Cluster** — canonical authority for messages, ordering, inbox cursors, read state, presence, and push history; sharded by `{c:…}` / `{u:…}` hash tags, replicated, AOF-persisted, no eviction on authoritative keyspaces.\n- **Redis Functions (Lua)** — atomic sequence assignment + dedupe on send, and the atomic presence/preference/cooldown claim on push.\n- **BullMQ on the same Redis/Valkey** — deterministic-ID, owner-partitioned jobs for recipient fanout and out-of-app delivery evaluation; workers run inside the same app replicas, not a separate deployment.\n- **S3-compatible object store** — immutable sealed history segments and media attachments via presigned direct upload; indexed from the owning conversation shard.\n- **APNs / FCM** — external push transport only, treated as unreliable and best-effort.\n- **One stateless HTTP deployable** — every replica serves every request; language and load balancer are interchangeable and not architecture-bearing.",
