      "outputText": "## Recommended architecture\n\nRun **one stateless HTTP deployable** and **one Redis/Valkey Cluster sharded by `conversation_id`** as the canonical live authority. A send is `POST /conversations/{id}/messages` routed by the `{conversation_id}` hash tag to exactly one shard, where a single Valkey function does the whole write atomically: dedupe on the client-supplied message ULID, `XADD` to the conversation stream (the stream ID *is* the ordering authority), and append a `(conv_id, seq, preview)` entry to each member's inbox stream. Reads are a **hanging GET** — the client holds one `XREAD BLOCK 25000` per session against its own inbox stream, returns immediately on new traffic, and reconnects. This is the same stateless request contract as short polling, but 200 ms polling is the one place the envelope forces a change: 10 M concurrent × 5 rps is 50 M rps of pure overhead, whereas a 25 s hanging GET costs ~400 k rps of reconnects plus real message deliveries. No sticky sessions, no connection registry, no presence service — any replica can serve any user because all state lives in the shard that owns the key. Capacity scales by adding shards and replicas only: ~10 M held requests at ~25 k per replica ≈ 400 replicas; at an assumed 1 msg/user/min ≈ 167 k writes/s spread over conversation shards, so ~64–128 primaries with replicas is the day-1 *and* vFinal shape.\n\nTwo planes earn rent beyond that. **Durable history** goes to Postgres sharded by the same `conversation_id`, written by the same app replicas leasing a trim cursor per shard; the collapse test fails on arithmetic, not taste — at the assumed rate, ~3 TB/day of messages cannot live in RAM under unbounded retention, so Valkey holds a capped rolling window (`XADD MAXLEN`) and Postgres owns anything older. **If retention is bounded (e.g. 30 days) or the real message rate is an order of magnitude lower, delete Postgres and run Valkey-only with AOF** — that is the single revisit trigger. Media goes to object storage because blobs don't belong in either row store. Out-of-app push is **recipient-partitioned BullMQ on the same Valkey**, where the job checks presence, last-read, preferences, and cooldown, then sends — push is a best-effort hint; the inbox stream is the user-facing authority. Fanout-on-write is bounded by a hard **1000-member group cap**; broadcast channels above that cap invert to reader-pull directly from the conversation shard. Recovery is by construction: an inbox entry carries `(conv_id, seq)`, so a lost or duplicated fanout write is repaired by diffing the client's cursor against the conversation stream, and the ULID dedupe makes every retry converge. Top failures to watch: **hot conversation shard** (one viral group exceeds a single shard — corrected by reader-pull, not subsharding); **inbox fanout partial failure** (corrected by seq-diff reconciliation on the next poll); **Valkey primary loss with AOF `everysec`** (up to 1 s of acknowledged writes at risk — send `WAITAOF` before ACKing if the contract demands zero loss).\n\n**Unknowns preserved, not invented:** actual message rate, retention policy, and group-size distribution. The topology holds under any of them; the *number* of shards is unproven until those are measured.\n\n### Core technologies\n\n- **Redis/Valkey Cluster** — canonical live authority; streams for conversation logs and per-user inboxes, hash-tagged by `{conversation_id}` / `{user_id}`, replicated, AOF-persisted, no eviction.\n- **Stateless HTTP app replicas** — one deployable, hanging-GET reads, all mutations as single-shard Valkey functions.\n- **Postgres, sharded by `conversation_id`** — cold message history only; drop this plane if retention is bounded.\n- **Object storage (S3-compatible)** — media blobs, referenced by URL from the stream entry.\n- **BullMQ on the same Valkey** — recipient-partitioned out-of-app push evaluation, executed by the same app replicas.\n- **APNs / FCM** — best-effort push transport.",
