      "outputText": "## Recommended architecture\n\nBuild a two-tier system that separates connection handling from conversation logic, with a durable log as the source of truth. The edge tier is a fleet of stateless Go WebSocket gateways behind L4 load balancers (TLS terminated at the gateway, not at a proxy, to avoid doubling the connection count); at ~75k connections per 16-core/32GB node you need roughly 135 nodes, so provision ~200 across three AZs in each region to absorb AZ loss and reconnect storms. Gateways do auth, framing, backpressure, and heartbeats — nothing else. Behind them sits a partitioned chat-core service where each `conversation_id` is owned by exactly one shard via consistent hashing (ownership leases in etcd), so every message for a conversation is serialized by a single owner that assigns a monotonic per-conversation sequence number. That single-owner property is what buys you ordering, idempotent dedup on client-supplied message IDs, and correct read cursors without any distributed locking. Gateways talk to core over long-lived multiplexed gRPC streams, not per-message HTTP.\n\nThe critical design decision is that **the WebSocket is a delivery optimization, not the correctness mechanism**. A send is acknowledged only after the core appends it to Kafka (partitioned by conversation, so the partition inherits the ordering) and writes it to ScyllaDB keyed by `(conversation_id, time_bucket)` clustered by `seq DESC`. Clients hold a per-conversation cursor and, on every reconnect, request the gap from their last known seq — so dropped sockets, gateway crashes, and rolling deploys degrade into a cheap catch-up query instead of lost messages. Fanout is dual-mode: for DMs and groups under ~1k members, the core resolves recipients against a Redis session-routing table (`user_id → gateway_id`, 60s TTL, refreshed by heartbeat) and pushes directly; for large channels, gateways subscribe to a per-channel Redis pub/sub topic so the core publishes once per *gateway* rather than once per user, capping fanout cost at fleet size instead of member count. A separate Kafka consumer group drives APNs/FCM for offline users and read-model updates for unread counts. Go multi-region, pinning each conversation to a home region and having remote gateways proxy to the owner — cross-region active-active writes to the same conversation are not worth the conflict resolution. Two things to pin down before committing: your peak inbound message rate and fanout amplification (this design assumes roughly 50k msgs/sec ingest and ~500k deliveries/sec, and Scylla/Kafka sizing scales linearly from there), and whether you need end-to-end encryption — E2EE forces per-device fanout and kills server-side search and unread counts, which changes the storage tier substantially.\n\n## Core technologies\n\n- **Edge/LB:** AWS NLB (or Envoy on bare metal) for L4, DNS-based region steering; no L7 proxy in the WebSocket path\n- **Gateway tier:** Go with `gobwas/ws` or `nhooyr/websocket` (epoll-based, no goroutine-per-buffer), tuned `net.core.somaxconn` and ephemeral port ranges\n- **Gateway ↔ core transport:** gRPC bidirectional streams over HTTP/2\n- **Chat core:** Go service, consistent-hash sharded by `conversation_id`, shard ownership leases in **etcd**\n- **Durable log:** **Kafka** (or Redpanda), partitioned by `conversation_id`, 7-day retention, feeds push/search/analytics consumers\n- **Message store:** **ScyllaDB** — `(conversation_id, bucket)` partition key, `seq DESC` clustering, LeveledCompaction\n- **Session routing, presence, typing indicators:** **Redis Cluster**, TTL'd keys plus pub/sub for large-channel fanout\n- **Metadata (users, memberships, groups):** **PostgreSQL** with read replicas, fronted by Redis cache\n- **Media:** S3 with presigned direct upload + CloudFront; messages carry references only\n- **Push:** dedicated Kafka consumer → APNs/FCM, with per-user throttling\n- **Search:** Elasticsearch, populated asynchronously from the Kafka log\n- **Orchestration/observability:** Kubernetes with `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` tuned for connection drain, Prometheus + Grafana, OpenTelemetry tracing keyed by message ID",
