      "outputText": "## Recommendation\n\nRun one stateless HTTP deployable in front of one Valkey (Redis) Cluster sharded from day 1, and make the message log itself the transport. A send is a single request that runs one Valkey function on the conversation's shard: it checks a TTL-bounded seen-set for the client-supplied ULID, `XADD`s the message to the `{conv:<id>}` stream (which supplies the monotonic sequence), and `ZADD GT`s one entry per member into each recipient's `{user:<id>}:inbox` zset — an O(1)-per-member, collapse-on-repeat pointer, not a copy of the message. Receivers do not hold connections; each client issues one bounded cursor `GET` covering all its conversations (inbox zset delta + `XRANGE` tail of the open conversation + presence/typing TTL keys) at ~200 ms while actively conversing and ~2 s while present-but-idle. Presence, typing, read cursors, and unread counts are TTL keys and zset scores in the same user shard, returned by that same poll — no presence service, no pub/sub, no connection registry, and therefore no reconnect/resubscribe failure mode to repair. Out-of-app delivery is a recipient-partitioned BullMQ job on the same cluster with a deterministic job ID; at execution one recipient-local Valkey function checks presence, last-read cursor, preferences, cooldown, and TTL-bounded push history, atomically claims the event, and only then calls APNs/FCM. Push is a best-effort hint; the in-app log is the authority. Media never touches this plane: clients PUT to object storage via presigned URLs and the message carries only a reference.\n\nAt 10M concurrent this is roughly 9.5M polls/s (assuming ~10% actively conversing at 200 ms and the rest at 2 s), which at an assumed 100–150k pipelined polls/s per primary is ~64–128 primaries with two replicas each, fronted by ~400–800 stateless replicas; treat both per-node figures as unproven until load-tested, since they are the only numbers that move the shard count. vFinal is the same graph with larger counts — no new component types. The one plane beyond Valkey is forced by arithmetic: at an assumed ~500k messages/s × ~300 B, chat history accrues ~13 TB/day, so Valkey holds only a hot tail (last N messages or ~24 h per conversation) and a conversation-partitioned BullMQ job seals trimmed stream segments into immutable object-storage blobs *before* `XTRIM`, with the segment index kept in the conversation's own shard; scrollback is an index lookup plus an object GET and never touches the live path. Three failures to plan for: **hot rooms** — cap write-fanout at ~512 members, above which members read-fanout directly from the conversation stream (one collapsing zset entry per room keeps sends O(1) regardless of room size); **acknowledged-write loss** — AOF `everysec` plus a failover can drop ~1 s of appends, so return 200 only after replica ack (`WAIT 1`) and let the client retry the same ULID to converge; **poll cost** — if adaptive cadence and per-client poll batching still exceed the shard budget, lengthen cadence before adding hardware. The design is invalidated, and only then, if a sourced contract requires sub-100 ms delivery to *idle* users (forces a connection-holding tier) or zero-loss durability on acknowledgment (forces a disk-transactional write before ack). Unknowns preserved: per-user message rate, group-size distribution, retention obligation, and whether E2EE is required — E2EE changes payloads to opaque ciphertext and strips push content, but not this topology.\n\n**Core technologies**\n\n- **Stateless HTTP app replicas** — one deployable, no connection or session state, scaled by count.\n- **Valkey/Redis Cluster** — canonical authority, sharded day 1 by hash tag `{conv:<id>}` and `{user:<id>}`, AOF `everysec` + replicas, `noeviction` on log keys.\n- **Redis Streams** — per-conversation message log, source of monotonic sequence and cursor reads.\n- **Sorted sets + TTL keys** — per-user inbox head (`ZADD GT`), read cursors, presence, typing.\n- **Valkey Functions (Lua)** — atomic dedup-and-append on send; atomic claim-and-decide for push.\n- **BullMQ on the same cluster** — recipient-partitioned push evaluation and conversation-partitioned segment sealing, deterministic job IDs, retry-safe handlers.\n- **Object storage** — media via presigned URLs, plus sealed cold-history segments indexed from the owning shard.\n- **APNs/FCM** — best-effort out-of-app hint only.",
