      "outputText": "Use a multi-region, active-active architecture with DNS/Anycast routing users to the nearest region. Terminate TLS and WebSockets at globally distributed edge proxies, then route connections to stateless chat gateways; each gateway maintains millions-scale connection capacity and uses consistent hashing by user ID. Store durable messages in a partitioned, replicated log/database keyed by conversation ID, while regional brokers fan messages out to connected users. Keep presence, typing indicators, and session mappings in an in-memory regional store with expiration; replicate only essential state across regions and use asynchronous cross-region delivery with idempotent message IDs.\n\nScale horizontally by monitoring active connections, messages per second, fan-out rate, gateway CPU, broker lag, and per-shard hot spots. Apply backpressure, bounded offline queues, rate limits, reconnect jitter, and graceful draining during deployments. Use multiple availability zones per region, automated failover, encrypted storage and transport, and periodic load tests targeting at least 10 million persistent connections plus the expected peak message/fan-out workload.\n\n- Anycast DNS or global load balancer\n- Envoy or HAProxy edge WebSocket proxies\n- Stateless Go or Rust chat gateways\n- Kafka or Redpanda for durable message streaming\n- ScyllaDB/Cassandra for partitioned message history\n- Redis Cluster for presence, routing, and ephemeral state\n- Kubernetes with multi-AZ node pools and autoscaling\n- Object storage for attachments and media\n- Prometheus, Grafana, OpenTelemetry, and centralized logs\n- mTLS, OAuth2/OIDC, KMS-managed encryption, and WAF/DDoS protection",
