      "outputText": "Use one horizontally scalable chat deployable with HTTP and WebSocket endpoints, backed by a single sharded, replicated, disk-backed Valkey cluster. Messages are durably appended to conversation-owned streams (`conv:{conversation_id}`) with request-id deduplication. App replicas consume those streams and idempotently append delivery records to recipient-owned streams (`user:{user_id}`); WebSocket connections receive best-effort push hints, while cursors enable replay after disconnects. Connections hold only socket state and cursors, so replicas can be added or replaced freely.\n\nDay 1 and the 10-million-user topology use the same components: add application replicas for connections, API traffic, and fanout, and add Valkey shards by stable conversation/user hash slots. The design assumes bounded message size and recipient fanout; message rate, maximum group size, retention, regional scope, and availability targets remain unknown, so capacity is unproven until load-tested. Valkey Streams consumer groups retain pending fanout work across crashes, deterministic IDs make retries converge, and writes are acknowledged only after the configured replication/durability guarantee. User-stream retention must cover the reconnect window; expired cursors require history reads from conversation streams.\n\n- Valkey Cluster: sharding, replication, AOF persistence, Streams, consumer groups, and server-side functions for atomic idempotency.\n- Stateless HTTP API.\n- WebSockets over TLS for realtime delivery.\n- Sharded Valkey Pub/Sub as a best-effort low-latency hint, never the message authority.\n- Signed authentication tokens with server-side conversation-membership checks.",
