      "outputText": "Use a multi-region, active-active architecture with regional WebSocket gateway fleets behind Anycast DNS and an L4 load balancer. Gateways remain stateless except for connection state, authenticate users via short-lived tokens, and route each conversation to a consistent-hash shard. Regional brokers distribute messages between gateway instances; messages are durably appended before acknowledgment, replicated across availability zones, and asynchronously replicated to a secondary region for disaster recovery. Presence and typing indicators use short-TTL in-memory state, while chat history is stored by conversation-partitioned shards with separate object-storage archives. Backpressure, per-user rate limits, reconnect replay, and load shedding protect the system during spikes.\n\nScale horizontally by adding gateway instances, broker partitions, and database shards independently. Use connection-aware autoscaling, capacity reserved for failover, and observability based on connection count, outbound fanout, broker lag, delivery latency, and dropped/replayed messages. Exact hardware counts depend on message rate, average fanout, payload size, retention, and regional distribution; the design assumes those values will be capacity-tested before production.\n\n- WebSockets over TCP, HTTP/2 or HTTP/3 for ancillary APIs\n- Anycast DNS, global traffic manager, and L4 load balancers\n- Kubernetes across multiple availability zones and regions\n- Envoy or HAProxy\n- Apache Kafka or Apache Pulsar\n- Redis Cluster for presence, sessions, and rate limiting\n- ScyllaDB or Cassandra for chat history and message metadata\n- PostgreSQL for users, billing, and configuration\n- S3-compatible object storage for archives and attachments\n- Protobuf with gRPC for internal services\n- OpenTelemetry, Prometheus, Grafana, and Loki\n- KMS-managed encryption, OAuth/OIDC, and mTLS",
