      "outputText": "Use one horizontally scaled application deployment serving HTTP and WebSockets, backed by a sharded, replicated, disk-backed Redis/Valkey Cluster as the canonical authority. Conversation state, membership, message logs, and durable fanout outboxes are owned by `conversation_id`; recipient inbox logs and presence are owned by `user_id`. A send synchronously appends the message and an outbox record; app replicas asynchronously lease bounded outbox batches and idempotently append message references to recipient inbox streams. WebSocket connections tail those streams and resume from cursors after reconnects, so gateway loss causes reconnect—not message loss.\n\nThe day-1 and vFinal topology are identical: clients → application replicas → Redis/Valkey shards; capacity grows by adding application replicas and Redis shards/replicas. Ten million concurrent connections is a connection target, not a complete capacity envelope: message rate, reconnect storms, maximum conversation membership/fanout, retention, and recovery time remain unknown and must be load-tested. The design is invalidated if a single conversation exceeds one shard’s measured capacity or if fanout is unbounded; retries use deterministic request/message IDs, and writes remain pending or reject when the owning shard cannot safely acknowledge them.\n\n- WebSockets over TLS for live delivery; HTTP for authentication, sends, history, and cursor recovery.\n- Redis/Valkey Cluster with replicas, AOF persistence, no-eviction policy, and stable hash-tagged ownership keys.\n- Redis Streams for message/inbox logs; hashes and sets for membership; TTL keys for presence.\n- Redis/Valkey Functions or Lua for atomic deduplication, message append, leasing, and idempotent fanout.\n- One stateless application deployment handling HTTP, WebSockets, bounded stream tails, and outbox retry loops.",
