      "outputText": "Use **stateless HTTP app replicas and one replicated, disk-backed transactional authority, sharded from day one by stable owner IDs**. A sender submits a message with a request ID; the app checks conversation membership and atomically records the message, its conversation sequence, and pending recipient delivery on the conversation’s shard. The sender then sees “sent”; “delivered” requires recipient acknowledgement. Existing app replicas lease pending delivery in bounded batches and append retry-safe entries to recipient-owned inbox logs in the same storage system. Connected clients fetch bounded inbox pages using cursors, polling about every 200 ms while actively chatting and backing off when idle. Conversation messages and membership are authoritative; inbox entries provide durable delivery and reconnect catch-up. Dispatch survives disconnects because its pending work is stored with the accepted message, without a separate broker or worker service.\n\nDay one and the 10-million-user deployment have identical component types and paths: capacity grows through additional app replicas, storage shards, and replicas. **Ten million concurrent users is not enough to establish capacity**: if all poll at 200 ms, the system must complete roughly **50 million fetches per second**, before sends and delivery fanout. Message rate, payload size, group-size distribution, retention, latency targets, and geographic requirements remain unknown. Cap page bytes and delivery batch sizes; paginate large fanouts without silently excluding large groups. Validate the full agreed workload, including three material failures: hot conversations exceeding one shard’s capacity; crashes between acceptance and delivery, handled by durable leases and deterministic recipient-entry IDs; and storage failover, handled by quorum-durable acknowledgement and cursor replay. A conversation exceeding one shard’s measured capacity or an unmet polling latency/cost budget invalidates this design. The database earns its place through durable shared truth; persisted dispatch earns its place through disconnect-surviving delivery. Keep both inside this single application-and-authority topology.\n\n- **Stateless HTTP:** idempotent sends, acknowledgements, bounded cursor polling.\n- **Owner-sharded transactional storage:** replicated durable logs, conversation-local transactions, recipient inboxes, native shard routing.\n- **Database-backed dispatch:** leased pending rows, bounded fanout batches, deterministic delivery IDs, retries in ordinary app replicas.",
