      "outputText": "Recommend **one stateless application deployment, scaled by replicas, backed by one replicated, disk-backed transactional authority sharded by owner from day one**. Use `conversation_id` to own membership, ordered message history, and delivery progress; use `user_id` to own inbox logs and read cursors within the same storage system. An authorized send atomically commits the message, its retry identifier, and pending delivery work on the conversation shard, then acknowledges success. Existing application replicas lease that work in bounded batches and append idempotently to recipient inboxes, using membership at send time. Clients fetch inbox events through authenticated HTTP cursor requests about every 200 ms while actively viewing chat, with item and byte limits per response. Recipients see messages in conversation order and resume after disconnect; interrupted delivery resumes from durable progress. Day one and the final deployment share the same graph: **clients → application replicas → owner shards**; delivery processing runs inside those replicas.\n\n**Ten million concurrent users is a connection population, not a complete capacity specification.** If all ten million actively poll every 200 ms, this design must serve **50 million fetches per second**, before sends and catch-up traffic. For message rate \\(M\\) and average recipient count \\(F\\), inbox writes grow approximately as \\(M × F\\). Message sizes, group sizes, retention, latency targets, durability requirements, and hottest-owner traffic remain unknown, so capacity is unproven until tested against those bounds. HTTP avoids persistent connection coordination but incurs substantial polling traffic; recipient logs simplify reconnects but amplify writes. Durable storage survives the collapse test because acknowledged messages and disconnected recipients require retained state; separate brokers, caches, and worker deployments are unnecessary because the authority already owns resumable work. Protect against three material failures: retries and partial delivery through stable IDs and resumable leases; shard loss through durable replicated commits, fenced leadership, and tested recovery; and overload through bounded processing and admission control. Admission rejection does **not** count as supporting the target load. Add shards and replicas for aggregate demand; revisit this design if measured owner hotspots exceed one shard or the required polling and fanout exceed the budget.\n\n- **HTTP APIs and bounded cursor polling** for sending, receiving, and reconnecting.\n- **Owner-sharded transactional storage** with native replication, durable commits, ordered logs, and owner-local atomic operations.\n- **Native leases and idempotent writes** for delivery processing within application replicas.",
