      "messageSetId": "670c77a850bc355d3855766926b444ca63aa4e609d79dbde5a6b3e676e6f33a2",
      "outputText": "Use a hybrid fan-out feed with a single read-time authorization gate. Every post commits to a sharded relational store that is the only source of truth, is appended synchronously to its author's timeline, and is then published to a durable event log. A fan-out worker consumes the log and appends the post ID to each follower's inbox, but skips authors whose follower count exceeds a threshold. Viewers who follow those high-follower authors pull their timelines at read time instead. Inboxes and timelines hold only post IDs with timestamps, never content or visibility decisions. On a feed request, the feed service gathers candidates from the viewer's inbox, the pulled timelines of the high-follower authors they follow, and a separate recommendation candidate service. A ranker orders the union, stores the ordered IDs as a short-lived page session, and each page is hydrated through batched lookups that check the post record, the author's privacy state, blocks, and the viewer's relationship before anything is returned.\n\nThe two correctness requirements fall out of where state lives. Eligibility within the window is a background latency budget, so the log and fan-out workers run continuously and pull timelines are updated inside the post write itself. Deletion and privacy changes are correctness, so they never depend on background work. A delete or privacy change commits to the canonical store, then writes a versioned visibility record to the write-through cache that hydration reads, and the client is acknowledged only after both writes succeed. Every page, including later pages of an existing session, is re-hydrated and re-authorized against those records, so an acknowledged change cannot leak regardless of stale IDs left in inboxes, timelines, or session snapshots. Lookup failures fail closed and drop the item. Launch runs this same topology on a single primary database, a single Redis node, and a single log broker. Growth is sharding along the ownership keys below, not a redesign.\n\n**Canonical authorities**\n- Post store: post existence, content, per-post visibility, deletion tombstones, and the author's fan-out tier.\n- Account store: account privacy setting and block lists.\n- Social graph store: follow edges and approval state for private accounts.\n- Derived and rebuildable: inboxes, author timelines, page sessions, the post and visibility cache, and recommendation indexes. Cache fills read the primary and never overwrite a newer version.\n\n**Ownership keys**\n- Post records and visibility records: post ID.\n- Author timelines, account privacy, and fan-out tier: author ID.\n- Inboxes and page sessions: viewer ID, with session ID as the secondary key.\n- Follow graph stored twice: by follower ID for what a viewer follows, by followee ID for fan-out.\n- Event log partitioned by author ID so a creator's posts and deletes stay ordered.\n\n**Bounded request path**\n- Inbox read: newest 400 IDs.\n- Pull merge: at most 50 high-follower authors, 20 recent posts each, chosen by most recent activity.\n- Recommendation candidates: at most 200, public authors only.\n- Rank once per session; page size 20, with at most 2 backfill fetches when authorization drops items.\n- Hydration: batched multiget on post cache, author cache, and viewer relationships, bounded by distinct authors on the page, with a hard timeout.\n- Degradation order under load: drop recommendations, then drop the pull merge, never skip authorization.\n\n**Bounded background paths**\n- Fan-out: authors above 50k followers are skipped; jobs run in batches of 1,000 idempotent inbox appends; target under 5 seconds at P99, alarm at 30 seconds.\n- Inbox trim to 400 entries on each append; inboxes of viewers inactive for 30 days expire and rebuild on demand from followed authors' timelines.\n- Lazy cleanup: delete and privacy events remove IDs from inboxes and recommendation indexes off the acknowledgement path.\n- Recommendation candidate generation runs on its own schedule with no eligibility deadline.\n\n**Permanent components**\n- Sharded canonical stores for posts, accounts, and the graph; the event log; fan-out workers; the Redis cluster holding inboxes, timelines, sessions, and the visibility cache; the feed assembler with its authorization gate; and the candidate service interface.\n- Replaceable: the ranker model, the recommendation candidate generator, and every threshold above.\n\n**Unknowns to settle with data**\n- The fan-out threshold and pull cap depend on the measured follower distribution and how many high-follower authors a typical viewer follows.\n- Whether new posts should be inserted into an in-progress session or wait for the next refresh; the default here is next refresh.\n- Whether an unfollow must hide posts already fanned out; the default here is yes for followed candidates.",
