      "messageSetId": "2374511edf098c6129c48f8dcd0f103dcee237238656ef843238885eee9ed66d",
      "outputText": "**Recommendation.** Build a hybrid push/pull feed with exactly two systems of record: a strongly consistent post store and a social graph store. Every other stateful piece is a derived, disposable hint that never decides visibility. On publish, the post and an outbox row commit in one post-store transaction, the creator's own timeline gets a synchronous append, and the client is acknowledged. An outbox relay emits to a Kafka topic partitioned by author, and fan-out workers copy the post reference into follower inboxes only for creators below the push tier threshold. Creators above the threshold get no fan-out at all. Their posts are merged at read time from a bounded set of followed creator timelines chosen by precomputed affinity. Because every post of every tier lands on its creator timeline, the pull path is also the safety net when fan-out lags behind the freshness target.\n\nA feed request gathers three legs in parallel, the viewer's inbox, the pull-tier creator timelines, and the recommendation candidate list. It merges and dedupes them, then batch-authorizes every candidate against a write-through visibility and relation cache that is backed by strong reads on miss and fails closed. Survivors are hydrated by immutable post version, ranked under a tight timeout with a recency fallback, and returned with an opaque watermark cursor. A deletion, audience change, privacy flip, suspension, or block is acknowledged only after the authoritative commit and the synchronous write-through of the new state into the visibility cache in every serving region. Any request that starts after the acknowledgment therefore observes the change, and the asynchronous purge of inboxes and timelines is only an optimization. The pull tier is eligible in about a second through the synchronous timeline append. The push tier meets the target through fan-out, with lag alarms and a degraded mode that widens pull fan-in when the event log falls behind.\n\n**Canonical authorities**\n- **Post store** owns content, author, creation time, audience, state, and a monotonic visibility version. Every visibility decision traces back to this record.\n- **Social graph store** owns follow edges with acceptance state, blocks mirrored in both directions, mutes, account privacy, account state, follower count, and creator tier.\n- **Event log** is authoritative only for ordered at-least-once delivery. It holds no state of record and is never consulted for visibility.\n- **Inboxes, creator timelines, recommendation candidates, and the visibility cache** are derived. Each can be flushed and rebuilt, and each carries zero authority.\n- **Ranking service** is a stateless scoring function over already-authorized candidates. It never filters or grants visibility.\n\n**Ownership keys**\n- **Post** by time-ordered post_id, sharded by hash, with a secondary index on author and creation time for timeline rebuilds.\n- **Creator timeline** by creator_id as a sorted set scored by creation time, capped at 1,000 entries.\n- **Inbox** by viewer_id as a sorted set of packed post and author references, capped at 1,000 entries, expiring after 30 idle days.\n- **Follow edges** forward by follower_id for read-time relation checks and reverse by followee_id for fan-out enumeration, written in one transaction.\n- **Blocks** by both orderings of the pair so either direction is a single point lookup.\n- **Account state, privacy, and tier** by user_id.\n- **Recommendation candidates** by viewer_id with a list version, capped at 500 and expiring after 48 hours, with cold-start lists keyed by country and language.\n- **Visibility cache** by post_id, author_id, and the viewer-author pair, each with a 60 second TTL and write-through on the same keys.\n- **Content cache** by post_id and version, immutable per version, so it is safe for in-process caching.\n- **Event partitions** by author_id for post events and by subject user_id for graph events, preserving create-then-delete order per creator.\n\n**Bounded request path**\n- **Budget** of 250 ms at p99 and a hard deadline of 400 ms, after which the page returns with whatever passed authorization.\n- **Gather** in parallel: up to 200 inbox entries below the watermark, up to 64 pull-tier timelines at 20 posts each capped to 300, and 100 recommendation candidates. A leg that times out is skipped and metered, never retried inline.\n- **Merge** and dedupe by post_id to at most 500 candidates.\n- **Authorize** every candidate in one pipelined batch, with at most 50 strong-read misses per request. A candidate not authorized within budget is dropped. The check requires an active post, an active author, no block in either direction, and either a public post from a public account or an accepted follow.\n- **Hydrate** survivors by immutable version through in-process, Redis, and replica tiers in batches of 100.\n- **Rank** up to 300 candidates in a single call with a 30 ms timeout, falling back to recency decay with a recommendation interleaved every fourth slot.\n- **Return** 20 items with a signed cursor carrying the watermark, the recommendation list version and offset, and the pull seed. A deleted post as the cursor is harmless because the watermark is a timestamp and id, not a lookup.\n\n**Bounded background path**\n- **Publish** commits the post and outbox row, appends the creator timeline, and acknowledges. The outbox relay then emits to the event log.\n- **Fan-out** for push-tier creators writes inbox entries in chunks of 500, idempotent by post_id, hard-capped at 10,000 writes per post.\n- **Tier changes** promote a creator to pull at 10,000 followers and demote at 7,000, so a creator does not flap across the boundary.\n- **Deletion and privacy events** write the authoritative state first, write through to the visibility cache in every region, acknowledge, and only then enqueue inbox and timeline purges.\n- **Freshness guard** alarms when event-time lag reaches 15 seconds and at 20 seconds extends pull fan-in to the viewer's top push-tier creators, bounded at 128 timelines.\n- **Recommendation generation** runs offline and near-line into the per-viewer candidate list, and inbox trimming keeps every sorted set under its cap.\n\n**Permanent components**\n- **From launch and still present at 10 million daily users:** the post store, the social graph store, the transactional outbox and event log, the creator timelines, the viewer inboxes, the write-through visibility cache, the read-time authorization check, and the stateless feed service.\n- **Start simple and swap without changing contracts:** the ranking service begins as recency decay and becomes a model, the recommendation pipeline begins as a country and language popularity list, and event log partitions grow from 48 to 512.\n\n**Open questions that change tuning, not structure**\n- **Tier threshold and pull fan-in** depend on the real follower distribution and read-to-write ratio. The chosen values are defensible defaults, not measured.\n- **Cross-region acknowledgment latency** sets the floor on how fast a deletion can be confirmed. If regions are far apart, the acknowledgment waits on the slowest region's write-through, and that cost must be accepted rather than bypassed.\n- **Inbox storage** at roughly 1.2 TB for 30 million monthly users assumes the caps above hold. Raising the inbox cap raises cost linearly.",
