      "messageSetId": "9520867c5bec4259f6aca10af969893e8cbd250e31c476baaa371a19d6f70004",
      "outputText": "**Recommendation.** Build the feed as fan-out-on-read over one owner-sharded Redis/Valkey cluster that is the canonical authority for every piece of feed-bearing state, served by one stateless HTTP deployable. A creator's post is one atomic write on the creator's shard: a post hash plus a ZADD into that creator's recent-posts sorted set, acknowledged after replication to one replica. Nothing propagates to followers, so a creator with millions of followers costs the same to publish, delete, or make private as a creator with ten, and a new post is eligible the instant the write is acknowledged, well inside the 30 second bound. A feed page request reads the viewer's following set, block set, and top topics from the viewer's shard, pipelines one bounded range read per followed creator plus a fixed number of topic hot buckets for recommendations, merges to a page of candidates, and then runs one visibility function on each candidate's creator shard that returns the post only if it is not deleted, its creator is public or has this viewer in the approved follower set, and the viewer is not blocked. That last check is the entire privacy mechanism: there is no rendered-feed cache, no per-viewer inbox, and no replica read on the check path, so a deletion or privacy change is enforced on the very next request with zero invalidation work. Pagination is a client-held signed cursor that freezes the maximum post id at first page, so continuation pages never duplicate and never see a stale server-side snapshot.\n\nDay one and 10 million daily users use the same component types and the same key layout. Growth is absorbed by adding Redis shards and HTTP replicas. Per-request work is capped by product constants, not by creator popularity or total population. The unknowns are the peak aggregate rates and the memory cost of the post corpus, and both only change shard counts, not topology, until the revisit triggers below fire.\n\n**Canonical authorities**\n- **One Redis/Valkey cluster** with AOF every second, one replica per primary, and every acknowledged mutation issued as a single-slot function followed by a replication wait. Feed-path reads go to primaries only.\n- **Object storage** holds media bytes referenced by URL from the post hash. It is not a feed authority and never gates visibility.\n\n**Ownership keys**\n- **Posts, recent-posts index, privacy flags, creator-side blocks, and follow edges** are all hash-tagged by creator id. The follow edge is canonical here because access to private content is decided on the same shard as the posts.\n- **Viewer following set, viewer-side blocks, and topic affinities** are hash-tagged by viewer id. The following set is a convergent copy written with idempotent set adds and removes. A stale entry is harmless because the creator-side visibility check filters it.\n- **Recommendation pools** are hash-tagged by topic as hourly sorted-set buckets, trimmed on every write and expired after the horizon.\n- **Post ids** are a creator-local sequence packed with a millisecond timestamp into 53 bits, so cursor comparison works and sorted-set scores stay exact.\n\nKey shape for reference:\n\n```\n{u:<creatorId>}:post:<postId>   HASH  body, vis, deleted, created, topic\n{u:<creatorId>}:posts           ZSET  score=postId member=postId\n{u:<creatorId>}:followers       SET   (canonical edge)\n{u:<creatorId>}:pending         SET   (private accounts)\n{u:<creatorId>}:profile         HASH  private, deleted\n{u:<creatorId>}:blocked         SET\n{u:<viewerId>}:following        SET   (convergent index, capped)\n{u:<viewerId>}:topics           ZSET\n{rec:t:<topic>}:<hourBucket>    ZSET  trimmed to K, EXPIRE H hours\n```\n\n**Bounded request paths**\n- **Feed page:** three viewer-shard reads, then at most one range read per followed creator up to the following cap, plus topics times hourly buckets for recommendations, then at most page size times refill rounds visibility checks. All caps are product constants.\n- **Create, delete, privacy change, block, follow:** each is one atomic function on one shard, constant work, acknowledged after replication. Follow is a two-step idempotent upsert: reserve on the viewer shard under the cap, commit on the creator shard, remove on reject.\n- **Deletion** leaves a tombstone with an expiry. The visibility check fails closed on a missing hash, so tombstone expiry cannot resurrect content.\n\n**Background paths**\n- **Account purge only.** Deleting an account is unbounded per action, so it runs as chunked, owner-partitioned BullMQ jobs inside the app replicas with deterministic job ids. The profile is marked deleted first, so visibility fails closed before any chunk runs.\n- No feed fan-out workers, no scheduled feed builders, no recommendation service. Recommendation pools are maintained inline by engagement writes.\n\n**Permanent components**\n- Stateless HTTP replicas.\n- The sharded Redis/Valkey cluster with its replicas.\n- Object storage for media.\n- BullMQ on the same cluster for purge. Nothing else survives the collapse tests.\n\n**Top failures and corrections**\n- **Viewer at the following cap misses the latency budget.** Test assumption: latency budget is unknown. Correction if measured: precompute a bounded candidate list for active viewers on a schedule, keyed by viewer id, and keep the render-time visibility check unchanged so privacy still holds. This is the only condition that reintroduces a projection.\n- **Acknowledged deletion resurfaces after primary failover or replica lag.** Correction already in the design: replication wait before acknowledgement and primary-only reads on the check path. Invalidated only if a deployment disables the wait.\n- **Post corpus outgrows affordable memory.** Retention is unknown. Correction: tier post bodies older than the feed window to a disk-backed store sharded by the same creator key while headers, visibility flags, and the recent index stay in Redis. Routing and ownership do not change.",
