      "outputText": "Build the feed as stateless HTTP replicas in front of one replicated, disk-persisted Valkey cluster, with every key hash-tagged by its owner and no other plane. Posts, their visibility state, block lists, and follower approvals live on the creator's shard. Follows, interest buckets, the seen set, and the current feed session live on the viewer's shard. The feed is pull-based. Creating a post is one atomic write on the creator shard that makes it immediately eligible for every follower, so a creator with millions of followers costs one write and no fanout. Deletion and privacy changes are one atomic function on the same creator shard, acknowledged only after a replica confirms the write. Every feed page ends by re-reading the posts it is about to return from creator-shard primaries, so stale sessions or recommendation buckets can never reveal deleted, private, or blocked content after an acknowledgment.\n\nA refresh reads the viewer's follow set, gathers a bounded slice of recent post IDs from each followed creator's timeline via pipelined per-shard reads on replicas, adds bounded recommendation candidates from the viewer's interest buckets, scores the candidates in the replica using post metadata, and stores the ranked ID list as a short-TTL session on the viewer shard. Later pages slice that session, verify visibility against primaries, and return. There is no queue, worker, broker, cache tier, ranking service, or second datastore on day one. Capacity grows by adding shards and read replicas under the same key contract, and vFinal has the same component types with higher counts.\n\n- **Canonical authorities.** One Valkey cluster with AOF persistence and replicas is the authority for post metadata and body, visibility state, follows, approvals, blocks, interest buckets, and feed sessions. Object storage holds media blobs only, keyed by post.\n- **Ownership keys.** Creator ID owns the post timeline sorted set, post hashes, private flag, approved-follower set, and block set. Viewer ID owns the follow set, seen set, interest buckets, and session list. Topic ID owns a capped recommendation bucket that is derived and never trusted for visibility.\n- **Bounded request path.** Post creation writes one hash and one timeline entry atomically on the creator shard, then appends to at most a handful of topic buckets best-effort. Refresh touches one viewer shard plus at most one pipelined batch per creator shard, capped by the follow limit and the candidate cap. Every page re-checks at most page-size posts against primaries for deleted state, private-and-unapproved state, and blocks.\n- **Background path.** None. The only revisit trigger is measured refresh latency exceeding its bound because of follow count, which would add owner-partitioned BullMQ fanout-on-write into a viewer-owned inbox key for creators under a follower threshold, with large creators staying pull.\n- **Permanent components.** Horizontally scaled stateless app replicas, the sharded replicated Valkey cluster, and object storage for media. Nothing else.\n- **Assumptions and unknowns.** The bounds below are product assumptions to confirm, not measured facts. Recommendation candidate quality and any future retrieval model are unknown. Retention is unknown; if retained bytes per shard outgrow what adding shards affords, post bodies move to a disk-backed row store keyed by the same creator ID, leaving ownership and routing unchanged.\n\n| Bound | Value | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| New post eligible to followers | Immediately on write, inside the 30 s contract | Sourced |\n| Follows per viewer | Product cap of a few thousand | Assumption |\n| Candidates scored per refresh | A few hundred | Assumption |\n| Post text size | A few KB, media external | Assumption |\n\n- **Top failures and corrections.** A lagging replica could serve a deleted post after acknowledgment, so candidate gathering uses replicas but the final visibility check reads primaries. A primary loss could drop an acknowledged delete, so create, delete, and privacy acks wait for replica confirmation and retries reuse the same request ID as an idempotent upsert. A viral creator concentrates reads on one small key, which stays within one shard's read capacity and scales by adding replicas, while several colliding hot creators are resolved by reassigning slots rather than changing shape.",
