      "outputText": "# Personalized Home Feed Architecture\n\n**Recommendation: hybrid fan-out with read-time authorization.** Use fan-out-on-write for ordinary creators and fan-out-on-read for high-follower creators. When a post is published, a background fan-out service pushes the post ID into per-viewer feed inboxes (Redis sorted sets keyed by viewer, capped at ~800 entries, backed by a durable store) for creators below a follower threshold (~50k). For creators above that threshold, the post is written only to a per-creator outbox; at request time the feed service merges the viewer's precomputed inbox with recent posts from the small set of celebrity outboxes they follow, plus a candidate slate from the recommendation service, then ranks the merged set. This keeps the 30-second eligibility SLA — fan-out for normal creators completes within seconds, and celebrity posts are visible on the very next read since the outbox write is synchronous with publish. Feeds store only post IDs plus ranking features; content is hydrated at read time.\n\n**Correctness comes from hydration-time filtering, never from the feed indexes.** Feed inboxes are treated as hints that may be stale. On every page request, after merging and ranking, the feed service hydrates post IDs against the posts store and checks each against the privacy/authorization service; deleted or newly restricted posts are dropped before rendering. A deletion or privacy change is acknowledged only after the posts store and privacy authority are durably updated and their caches invalidated — so any later request filters correctly regardless of what stale IDs linger in inboxes. Asynchronous cleanup then lazily removes tombstoned IDs from inboxes, but it is an optimization, not the safety mechanism. This design scales from launch (the same read path works with tiny fan-out volumes) to 10M DAU by sharding inboxes by viewer ID and outboxes by creator ID.\n\n- **Canonical authorities:** the posts store is authoritative for post existence and content (a delete writes a tombstone here first); the privacy/authorization service is authoritative for who may view what; the social-graph service is authoritative for follow edges. Feed inboxes, outboxes, and caches are derived data and never authoritative.\n- **Ownership keys:** viewer inboxes sharded by `viewer_id`; celebrity outboxes and follower lists sharded by `creator_id`; posts keyed by `post_id`; follow edges keyed by `(follower_id, creator_id)` with a reverse index for fan-out.\n- **Bounded request path:** read inbox (capped list) + read ≤K followed celebrity outboxes (K is small per viewer) + fetch ≤N recommendation candidates → rank → hydrate and authorize one page (~20–50 items) → return. Every step is O(page size), independent of the creator's follower count.\n- **Bounded background paths:** fan-out workers consume a publish event stream and write to follower inboxes in batches with per-creator caps (celebrity creators skip fan-out entirely, bounding worst-case work); tombstone cleanup and inbox trimming run as rate-limited async jobs; recommendation candidate generation runs offline/near-line, not on the request path.\n- **Permanent components:** posts store, social-graph service, privacy/authorization service, publish event log, feed read service with hydration-time filtering. Inboxes, outboxes, ranking models, and the recommendation pipeline are replaceable derived layers that can be rebuilt from the event log and canonical stores.",
