      "outputText": "**Recommendation: stateless read-time fan-in over owner-sharded stores; no fan-out-on-write, no feed materialization.** All durable truth lives in one replicated disk-backed authority sharded by its domain owner: posts (with visibility state) by `creator_id`, the follow graph and viewer mutes/preferences by `viewer_id`. A Redis/Valkey cluster, partitioned by the same owner hash tags, holds each creator's capped recent-post head list (~last 100 post IDs + scores) and a small set of cohort/topic popularity ZSETs; both are updated synchronously in the post-write and engagement-write request paths, which makes new posts eligible in single-digit seconds — well inside the 30-second bound — with no pipeline. A feed request in an ordinary stateless HTTP replica reads the viewer's follow list (capped, e.g. 5,000 follows), pipeline-fetches the head lists of those creators plus top-K from a few popularity sets, scores and merges in-process, filters every candidate against the creator-shard visibility flags and post state, and returns a page with a score/timestamp cursor. Because every page re-filters against canonical state, an acknowledged deletion or privacy change — which updates the post row and creator flags and trims the head list before acknowledging — can never surface to an unauthorized viewer on any later request; the head list is a bounded index, never the authority. Celebrity creators are read-hot, not write-hot, in this shape: millions of followers reading one small head list is served by adding read replicas of that shard, not by new topology.\n\nDay 1 and 10M-DAU vFinal are the same graph — stateless replicas, the sharded authority, the sharded Redis/Valkey cluster, and one owner-partitioned BullMQ path (in the same replicas) that periodically refreshes cohort popularity/recommendation candidate sets, since model-scored candidate generation exceeds request latency but tolerates minutes of staleness. Scale is purely by count: more HTTP replicas, more shards, more read replicas on hot creator shards. The known risks and their bounded corrections: (1) fan-in cost for heavy followers is capped by the follow limit and pipelined multi-gets, and degrades gracefully by sampling followed creators rather than changing shape; (2) per-item visibility filtering on every page is the price of the revocation guarantee — the revisit trigger is measured read amplification exceeding a shard's capacity, corrected by pushing the visibility flag into the head-list entries (still invalidated synchronously in the ack path); (3) skew from a single viral creator is absorbed by shard read replicas, and only a measured single-owner load exceeding one shard would justify anything more.\n\n- **Canonical authorities:** one replicated disk-backed store — posts + visibility state sharded by `creator_id`; follow graph, blocks/mutes, and viewer prefs sharded by `viewer_id`. Redis/Valkey head lists and popularity sets are derived, capped, and rebuildable from the authority.\n- **Ownership keys:** `creator_id` for anything about content; `viewer_id` for anything about the reader. Same hash-tag routing in Redis/Valkey and the authority, day 1 through vFinal.\n- **Bounded request path:** feed read touches ≤ follow-cap head lists + a constant number of popularity sets, filters ≤ page-size × small-factor candidates against creator shards, and returns one page — no unbounded fanout, no cross-viewer state.\n- **Bounded background path:** owner-partitioned BullMQ jobs (deterministic job IDs, retry-safe) in the same app replicas refresh cohort recommendation candidate sets on a schedule. No other queues, workers, brokers, or streams.\n- **Permanent components (rent paid):** stateless HTTP replicas; the owner-sharded authority (durable truth, revocation source); the owner-sharded Redis/Valkey cluster (read-time fan-in at feed latency fails against disk shards alone at this candidate volume — the one measured forcing function); BullMQ for recommendation refresh (candidate scoring can't fit in the request). Everything else — feed materialization per user, Kafka, a fan-out service, CQRS, a separate recommendation service — fails the collapse test: the contract is met without it.",
