      "outputText": "**Recommendation: pull-based feed assembly at read time, no fan-out-on-write.** Posts live in one replicated, disk-backed post store sharded by `creator_id`; follow edges in the same authority sharded by `follower_id`. A feed request in a stateless HTTP replica reads the viewer's follow list (bounded — cap the followed set consulted per request, e.g. the ~1–2k most recently active followed creators tracked in a viewer-local Redis/Valkey sorted set), fetches each creator's recent post heads from a per-creator Redis/Valkey recent-posts index (a small sorted set the creator's write path updates transactionally-adjacent on publish, giving eligibility well inside 30 s), merges them with a recommendation candidate list, ranks in-process, and re-checks visibility of every candidate against the canonical post store before returning. That authoritative read-time check is what makes deletions and privacy changes take effect on the next request: the delete/privacy write updates the creator-shard row and its recent-posts index in the same acknowledged operation, and no derived copy is authoritative, so there is no fan-out invalidation to chase across millions of follower feeds. Million-follower creators cost nothing extra: reads fan in to one creator shard's small hot index (replica-served), rather than writes fanning out to followers. Day-1 and 10M-DAU topology are identical component types; scale is added by increasing post/follow shards, Redis partitions, and stateless replicas.\n\nRecommendation candidates come from a periodically refreshed per-viewer candidate list written by an ordinary BullMQ job (partitioned by `viewer_id`) running in the same app replicas — it is derived, loss-tolerant, and filtered through the same read-time visibility check, so staleness never leaks unauthorized content. Pagination is a cursor of (rank score, post ID) — stateless, re-validated each page.\n\n- **Canonical authorities:** post store (posts + visibility state) sharded by `creator_id`; follow-edge store sharded by `follower_id`; both in one replicated disk-backed database. Redis/Valkey holds only derived data: per-creator recent-post index (`{creator_id}` hash tag), per-viewer followed-active set and recommendation candidates (`{viewer_id}` hash tag).\n- **Ownership keys:** `creator_id` for posts and recent-post indexes; `viewer_id`/`follower_id` for follows, candidate lists, and feed cursors.\n- **Bounded request path (SYNC):** one follow-list read, ≤N creator-index fetches (capped), one candidate-list read, one batched visibility check against post shards, in-process rank — fixed fanout per request regardless of creator popularity.\n- **Bounded background path (ASYNC):** viewer-partitioned BullMQ refresh of recommendation candidates with deterministic job IDs and idempotent overwrite; no queue for publishes, deletes, or privacy changes — those are single-shard synchronous writes.\n- **Permanent components:** stateless HTTP replicas, the sharded database, sharded Redis/Valkey, and BullMQ on that same Redis. No fan-out workers, no per-user materialized timelines, no separate feed or recommendation service, no event bus.\n- **Top risks and corrections:** (1) creator-index miss or Redis loss → rebuild lazily from the post store on read; correctness never depends on Redis. (2) Extreme follow counts blowing the read fanout → the per-request cap on consulted creators is the contract, with the remainder surfaced via the recommendation candidate path. (3) A measured p99 showing the capped merge still misses latency targets at scale is the trigger to add per-viewer cached first-page snapshots (still visibility-checked on serve), not fan-out-on-write.",
