      "outputText": "**Recommendation: a hybrid fan-out feed built on ID-only inboxes with read-time authorization.** Every post write goes to a canonical post store (Postgres at launch, sharded by author; ScyllaDB/Cassandra if write volume demands it later) and emits a `post-created` event to Kafka. Fan-out workers consume that log and push the **post ID only** into each follower's feed inbox (Redis sorted sets keyed by viewer, trimmed to the most recent ~800 entries) — this comfortably meets the 30-second eligibility window at normal follower counts. Creators above a follower threshold (~100k) are exempt from fan-out entirely: their recent post IDs are pulled live from their author timeline at read time, so a post from a 5M-follower creator is eligible instantly and never triggers a multi-million-row write storm. A feed request assembles candidates from three bounded sources — the viewer's inbox page, the recent posts of their followed hot creators, and a precomputed recommendation candidate set — then hydrates and ranks them. The same shape works at launch on three small services and at 10M DAU (~50M feed loads/day is only low thousands of QPS peak); you scale by resharding, not redesigning.\n\nThe deletion/privacy invariant is enforced structurally, not by cleanup jobs: inboxes and rec caches hold only IDs, and the sole path from an ID to rendered content is hydration against the canonical post store plus a batch visibility check against the privacy authority. A delete or privacy change commits to the canonical store and synchronously invalidates the affected post-content cache keys **before** the acknowledgment is returned; therefore any feed request served after the ack filters the content out at hydration, regardless of how many stale IDs still sit in inboxes. Asynchronous tombstone sweepers later purge those IDs from inboxes, but that is hygiene for efficiency — correctness never depends on it.\n\n- **Canonical authorities:** the post store (existence, content, tombstones, per-post audience), the social graph store (follow edges), and the privacy/settings store (per-creator audience rules and blocks). These three are the only sources of truth; a post is visible if and only if they say so at read time.\n- **Ownership keys:** feed inboxes owned by `viewer_id`; posts and author timelines owned by `author_id` (with `post_id` as row key); follow edges dual-indexed by `follower_id` (for \"whom do I follow\") and `followee_id` (for fan-out); privacy rules owned by `author_id`; rec candidate sets owned by `viewer_id`.\n- **Bounded request path:** per feed page — read ≤500 inbox IDs, pull the ~10 most recent posts from at most ~20 followed hot creators, take ≤200 rec candidates, one batch hydration, one batch visibility check, rank, return ~30 items. Every constant is fixed regardless of follower counts, so tail latency doesn't grow with graph size.\n- **Bounded background paths:** fan-out capped at the 100k-follower threshold (above it, zero fan-out — pull only); inbox trim to ~800 entries on every insert; recommendation candidate refresh every few minutes per active user; best-effort tombstone sweeper with retries but no correctness obligation.\n- **Permanent components:** the post store, social graph store, privacy store, and the Kafka event log are durable systems of record. Feed inboxes, the post-content hydration cache, rec candidate sets, and ranking features are all rebuildable caches — any of them can be flushed and reconstructed from the permanent stores without data loss or authorization risk.",
