      "outputText": "**Recommendation: pull-on-read feed assembly over creator-sharded post logs — no per-follower fanout, no materialized timelines.** Every post is written exactly once to its creator's shard (canonical row plus a small \"recent posts\" head in Redis/Valkey keyed by `creator_id`), so publishing costs O(1) whether the creator has ten followers or ten million, and a post is eligible for feeds the instant that write commits — comfortably inside the 30-second bound. A feed refresh reads the viewer's follow list, does a pipelined fetch of those creators' recent-post heads, merges in a few hundred candidates from shared recommendation pools, scores them in-process, and stores a ranked ID snapshot under the viewer's key with a short TTL. Paging walks that snapshot, but every served item is re-verified against the creator-partitioned visibility state at serve time; deletion and privacy acknowledgments write the authority row and the visibility marker before returning, so a stale snapshot or cached head can never leak acknowledged-removed content. This is why fanout-on-write is rejected: materializing per-follower timelines turns one celebrity post into millions of writes and, worse, turns every deletion into a propagation race that structurally conflicts with the \"acknowledged means invisible\" contract. Read-time assembly makes both guarantees fall out of the shape instead of requiring repair machinery.\n\nThe load-bearing assumption is a product cap on follow-list size (assume low thousands); the refresh cost is O(follow cap + rec pool) and page cost is O(page size), both intrinsically bounded, so capacity from launch to 10M DAU is reached by adding app replicas, database shards, and Redis shards/replicas under the same routing contract — day-1 and vFinal differ only in counts. The two real failure modes and corrections: celebrity head keys become hot reads under many concurrent refreshes — served by read replicas and a few-second head cache, which is safe because the per-item serve-time visibility check, not the head, protects the deletion invariant; and authority/head drift (a committed post missing its head append) breaks 30-second eligibility — prevented by making publish acknowledgment require both idempotent writes, backed by a bounded reconciliation sweep of recent rows. Revisit trigger: if follow lists turn out to be uncapped or measured refresh latency exceeds budget at scale, the correction is bounded partial fanout for small creators, not a wholesale switch to push timelines.\n\n- **Canonical authorities:** a disk-backed relational store, sharded by owner, holds posts, privacy settings, and the follow graph — it is the sole durable truth for content and visibility. Sharded, replicated Redis/Valkey (no eviction on these keyspaces) holds only derived and ephemeral state: per-creator recent-post heads, per-post/per-creator visibility markers, per-viewer ranked snapshots, shared recommendation candidate pools, and BullMQ queues. Redis loss degrades latency, never correctness, because serving falls back to authority point reads.\n- **Ownership keys:** `creator_id` owns posts, the recent-post head, visibility markers, and privacy flags; `viewer_id` owns the follow list, seen-state, and the short-TTL feed snapshot. Every Redis key carries the owner hash tag; the same routing contract holds at every scale.\n- **Bounded request paths:** publish = one authority insert plus one head append (O(1), no follower fanout); feed refresh = one follow-list read plus a pipelined bounded multi-get of heads plus in-request scoring (O(follow cap)); page serve = next K IDs from the viewer snapshot, K per-item visibility checks, K hydration reads (O(page size)); delete/privacy change = transact on the creator shard and update the visibility marker before acknowledging (O(1) per post; account-level privacy is a creator-level flag checked at read time, not a post rewrite).\n- **Bounded background paths:** owner-partitioned BullMQ jobs running inside the ordinary app replicas refresh the shared recommendation pools and run the authority-to-head reconciliation sweep over recent posts. No dedicated worker deployment, broker, stream, or fanout pipeline.\n- **Permanent components (day-1 = vFinal by type):** stateless horizontally scaled HTTP app replicas (feed assembly, ranking module, and queue processors live inside), the owner-sharded relational authority, and the owner-sharded replicated Redis/Valkey plane. Nothing else earns rent: no fanout workers, no per-viewer materialized timelines, no separate recommendation or feed service, no event bus.",
