      "outputText": "**Recommendation: hybrid fan-out with read-time authorization.** Run a write path where the post service commits each post to a canonical post store, then publishes to a fan-out worker pool that pushes post IDs (not bodies) into per-user Redis/KV feed inboxes for followers of ordinary creators — this comfortably meets the 30-second eligibility target. For creators above a follower threshold (say 100k), skip fan-out entirely: their recent post IDs live in a per-creator timeline that the feed service pulls at read time. A feed request merges three ID sources — the viewer's pushed inbox, pulled timelines of the few mega-creators they follow, and a batch of recommendation candidates from an async candidate-generation pipeline — then ranks them, and critically **hydrates every ID against the canonical post store with an authorization check** (exists, not deleted, visibility allows this viewer) before rendering. Because inboxes hold only IDs and hydration is authoritative, a deletion or privacy change takes effect on the very next request the moment the canonical store acknowledges the write; background cleanup of stale inbox entries is a lazy optimization, not a correctness requirement. Pagination uses an opaque cursor over the ranked merged stream, with the ranked page cached briefly per user and still re-hydrated per render.\n\n- **Canonical authorities:** the post store (Postgres/Spanner-class) is the sole authority for post existence, content, and visibility; the social-graph service is the sole authority for follow edges and creator follower counts; the user/privacy service is the sole authority for account-level privacy state. Inboxes, timelines, ranking caches, and recommendation candidates are all derived and disposable.\n- **Ownership keys:** feed inboxes keyed by `viewer_id`; creator timelines keyed by `creator_id`; posts keyed by `post_id` with `author_id`; follow edges keyed by `(follower_id, followee_id)` with a reverse index for fan-out; pagination cursors scoped to `(viewer_id, session/feed_version)`.\n- **Bounded request path (must stay O(page)):** read inbox head + pull ≤K mega-creator timelines + fetch cached rec candidates → rank ~few hundred candidates → hydrate + authorize the page (batched point reads) → return. No fan-out, graph traversal, or model training on this path.\n- **Background paths:** fan-out workers off the post-event stream (30-second SLO); recommendation candidate generation and model scoring refresh; lazy/async purge of deleted or privacy-changed post IDs from inboxes; inbox trimming and cold-user inbox rebuild from the graph on demand.\n- **Permanent components (survive every scale stage):** the canonical post store with read-time authorization on hydration, the social-graph service, the event stream (Kafka-class) between write and fan-out, and the feed service's merge-rank-hydrate contract. What changes with scale is capacity and the fan-out threshold — at launch you can pull everything at read time and add push inboxes as traffic grows, without changing any interface.\n\nThe one invariant to protect in code review: nothing renders to a viewer without passing hydration-time authorization, no matter which derived store the ID came from.",
