      "outputText": "**Recommendation: fan-out-on-read (pull) feed with read-time authorization, on one sharded authority plus a sharded Redis/Valkey data plane — no fan-out-on-write, no per-user feed store.** Posts live in one replicated disk-backed authority sharded by `creator_id`; follows and per-viewer state shard by `viewer_id`. When a viewer requests a page, a stateless HTTP replica reads their follow list, fetches each followed creator's small \"recent posts\" head (a capped Redis list per creator, written synchronously with the post insert — this makes 30-second eligibility trivial, since a post is eligible the moment its write commits), merges those candidates with a precomputed per-viewer recommendation candidate list, ranks in-process, and returns a page. Every post ID that survives ranking passes a final visibility check against the creator-shard authority (batched MGET/select on current status + privacy) before it is serialized. That read-time check is what satisfies the deletion/privacy contract: the acknowledgment is the transactional write of the status flag on the creator shard, and no feed response is assembled from stale copies because there are no materialized per-viewer feed copies to invalidate. Million-follower creators cost nothing extra — nobody fans their post out; readers pull one hot, replicated key, and read replicas of that creator's Redis shard absorb the skew.\n\nThe only work outside the request is recommendation candidate refresh: a `viewer_id`-partitioned BullMQ job with a deterministic job ID recomputes each active viewer's candidate list (bounded, e.g., 500 IDs) on a lazy schedule; if the list is stale or missing, the request path serves followed-posts-only, so the job is loss-tolerant and retry-safe. Fan-in per request is explicitly bounded: at most K followed creators consulted per page (the most recently active, tracked in a per-viewer sorted set), H head entries per creator, and one batched visibility check — constants independent of total users or follower counts, so capacity from launch to 10M DAU is added purely by increasing HTTP replicas and shard/replica counts under the same routing contract. Fan-out-on-write with hot-creator exceptions would add a second delivery path, a per-user feed store, and an invalidation problem that directly conflicts with the privacy requirement; it earns its rent only if measured read-time fan-in latency exceeds the page budget, which the bounded K×H merge is designed to prevent.\n\n- **Canonical authorities:** posts + visibility/privacy status — replicated SQL sharded by `creator_id` (single source of truth for the deletion/privacy contract); follow graph and viewer preferences — same store sharded by `viewer_id`.\n- **Derived state (Redis/Valkey, sharded, replicated):** per-creator recent-post head list (hash-tagged by `creator_id`, written in the post path, capped length); per-viewer recommendation candidate list and recently-active-follows sorted set (hash-tagged by `viewer_id`). All derived; loss degrades ranking, never correctness, because visibility is re-checked at the authority on every page.\n- **Ownership keys:** `creator_id` for anything about a post; `viewer_id` for anything about a feed consumer. Same keys day 1 and at 10M DAU; scaling adds shards and replicas only.\n- **Bounded request path (SYNC):** 1 follow-set read → ≤K creator-head reads → 1 candidate-list read → in-process rank → 1 batched visibility check → page. Constant work per request regardless of follower-count skew.\n- **Bounded background path (ASYNC):** viewer-partitioned BullMQ recommendation refresh, idempotent same-key overwrite, bounded output size, safe to drop and retry; no queue, stream, or worker fleet for post delivery exists.\n- **Permanent components:** stateless HTTP replicas, the sharded SQL authority, the sharded Redis/Valkey plane, and BullMQ on that same Redis. Nothing new appears at scale.\n- **Revisit triggers:** if measured p99 page assembly exceeds budget at the K×H bound, raise Redis read replicas for hot creator heads before considering any push path; if recommendations later require sub-minute freshness (currently unknown — assumed minutes-stale is acceptable), tighten the refresh schedule, not the topology.",
