      "outputText": "**Recommendation: a hybrid push/pull feed built around an append-only event log, per-viewer inbox of post IDs, and mandatory read-time hydration against authoritative stores.** Every post write lands first in the post store (the source of truth for content and visibility) and emits an event to a durable log (Kafka). Fan-out workers consume that log and push post IDs into each follower's feed inbox (a Redis/ScyllaDB sorted structure keyed by viewer ID) — but only for creators below a follower threshold (~50k). Posts from mega-creators are never fanned out; instead, the read path pulls their recent post IDs directly from a small per-creator \"recent posts\" index covering the viewer's followed mega-creators. This caps write amplification (a 5M-follower post costs one index write, not 5M inbox writes) while keeping the read path bounded, and the log-to-inbox pipeline comfortably meets the 30-second eligibility SLO since it does no ranking or hydration on the write side.\n\nThe read path is where correctness lives: a feed request merges three ID lists (viewer inbox, mega-creator pull, precomputed recommendation candidates from an offline/near-real-time pipeline), then hydrates those IDs with a batched read against the post store, **filtering out anything deleted or no longer visible to this viewer at that moment**, then ranks and returns a cursor-paginated page. Because deletion and privacy changes are synchronous writes to the authoritative post store, any feed request after the change is acknowledged cannot serve the content, regardless of stale IDs lingering in inboxes or caches — background tombstone propagation cleans those up eventually, but the safety guarantee never depends on it. This design runs fine at launch on a handful of nodes and scales to 10M DAU by sharding inboxes on viewer ID and the post store on author ID, with no architectural rewrite.\n\n- **Canonical authorities:** the post store owns post content, deletion state, and visibility rules; the social-graph service owns follow edges; the identity service owns viewer authentication. Feed inboxes, recommendation candidate sets, and all caches are derived data and are never trusted for visibility decisions.\n- **Ownership keys:** posts keyed by `post_id`, partitioned by `author_id`; follow edges keyed by `(follower_id, creator_id)` with a reverse index for fan-out; feed inboxes keyed by `viewer_id`; mega-creator recent-post indexes keyed by `creator_id`.\n- **Bounded request path:** one feed page = inbox range read + pull from the viewer's followed mega-creators (bounded because few creators cross the threshold) + a fixed-size recommendation candidate fetch, then one batched hydration/visibility check and rank over O(page size × small constant) items. No request ever iterates a follower list or an unbounded history.\n- **Bounded background paths:** fan-out workers process the event log with per-creator work capped by the follower threshold; tombstone workers lazily purge deleted/private IDs from inboxes; the recommendation pipeline refreshes candidate sets on its own cadence. All are retryable, idempotent (keyed on `post_id`), and off the request path.\n- **Permanent components:** post store, social-graph store, durable event log, per-viewer inbox store, ranking service, and the read-time hydration/visibility filter. The filter is permanent and non-bypassable — it is the enforcement point for deletions and privacy, so no optimization may ever serve unhydrated feed entries directly.",
