      "outputText": "## Recommended architecture\n\nBuild this as a **read-time-assembled (pull) feed** served by one stateless, horizontally scalable HTTP deployable, backed by two authorities sharded by domain owner from day 1: a disk-backed relational store holding posts (sharded by `creator_id`) and the follow graph (sharded by `viewer_id`), plus a sharded, replicated Redis/Valkey cluster holding each creator's small recent-post head list (`{creator_id}`-tagged, written through synchronously in the same request that acknowledges a post, delete, or privacy change). A feed page request fetches the viewer's follow list, batch-reads the recent heads of followed creators (grouped per Redis shard), merges them with recommendation candidates drawn from cohort/interest candidate pools in the same Redis cluster, scores the resulting few hundred candidates in-process, then hydrates the top page of post IDs against the canonical post rows with a visibility check in that same request. Pull assembly is forced by two of your constraints together: celebrity creators with millions of followers make fan-out-on-write unbounded per post, and the deletion/privacy contract would require scrubbing millions of materialized per-user timelines — whereas here a delete or privacy change touches exactly one creator shard (post row plus recent-head list, atomically on the same owner key) and every later read is correct by construction. The 30-second eligibility bound is met trivially because a new post is readable the moment its write-through completes.\n\nDay 1 and 10M-DAU use the same component types and flow; capacity grows only by adding API replicas, database shards, and Redis shards/read replicas. Hot celebrities are read-hot keys served by replica reads of a tiny immutable-ish list, not a topology change. The only asynchronous work is recommendation-candidate refresh — scheduled BullMQ jobs (running inside the same app replicas, partitioned by cohort) that rebuild bounded candidate pools; the pools are derived and disposable, since hydration re-checks visibility, so a stale candidate ID filters out harmlessly and pool loss just means regeneration. This design assumes a product cap on follows-per-viewer (e.g., low thousands) to bound per-request fan-in; if measured follow counts must exceed one request's merge budget, the correction is paging the merge over the most-recently-active follows via the cursor — still pull, same topology.\n\n- **Canonical authorities:** disk-backed relational store for posts + visibility/privacy state (sharded by `creator_id`) and the follow graph (sharded by `viewer_id`). Redis/Valkey holds derived state only: per-creator recent-head lists (write-through on the post/delete/privacy ack path) and recommendation candidate pools.\n- **Ownership keys:** `creator_id` for posts and recent heads; `viewer_id` for follows and interest profile; cohort/interest key for candidate pools. Same routing contract at launch and at 10M DAU.\n- **Bounded request path (SYNC):** feed page = 1 follow-list read + batched recent-head reads for ≤ F follows + in-process scoring of a few hundred candidates + hydration of ~1 page of posts with per-post authoritative visibility check. No unbounded fanout; every read is owner-local or batched per shard.\n- **Bounded background path (ASYNC):** scheduled BullMQ candidate-pool refresh per cohort, deterministic job IDs, retry-safe, loss-tolerant (pools regenerate). No fan-out-on-write, no per-follower delivery work, no scrub jobs.\n- **Permanent components (each pays rent):** stateless API deployable (collapse test: something must serve requests); relational authority (durable business truth: posts, privacy, follows); Redis/Valkey cluster (forced by read-time merge latency over follows and hot-creator read replication — computing recent heads from the relational store per page fails the celebrity read-load shape); BullMQ inside app replicas (recommendations can't be computed from scratch per request). Nothing else: no timeline store, no fan-out workers, no ranking service, no event bus.\n- **Top failures and corrections:** (1) hot-creator key saturation → replica reads on that shard, scaled by replica count; (2) privacy leak via stale derived data → invariant: no post ID reaches a client without an authoritative visibility check in the serving request, and delete/privacy acks synchronously update the owner's recent-head list; (3) oversized follow lists → follow cap now, cursor-paged merge if measured distributions break the cap — that measurement is the trigger to revisit, not a reason to preinstall fan-out-on-write.",
