      "outputText": "## Recommendation\n\nCompute the feed on read from creator-owned state; never materialize per-follower inboxes. One stateless HTTP deployable (ordinary app replicas) sits in front of one replicated, AOF-persisted Valkey cluster sharded by a stable hash tag on the content owner. A post write is a single transaction on `{creator_id}`: the post hash, an entry in that creator's 48h recent-candidate ZSET scored by timestamp, a `last_post_ts` string, and an INCR into the ZSETs for its topic pools. Because nothing fans out, a post is eligible the instant its write commits — the 30-second bound is met with three orders of magnitude of headroom, and a creator with 8M followers costs exactly the same write as one with eight. A feed request reads the viewer's follow set from `{viewer_id}` (product-capped at 2,000), pipelines one `MGET` of `last_post_ts` per shard to find which followed creators posted inside the retention window, takes the 300 most recently active of those, pipelines a `ZRANGE` for their 20 newest post IDs, and unions that with the top 200 of each of four recommendation pools (the viewer's three strongest affinity topics from `{viewer_id}` plus a global pool). That yields ≤2,300 candidate IDs, truncated to 1,500 by recency, scored in-process against the viewer's affinity vector and recency/engagement features, then only the ~30 posts on the requested page are hydrated.\n\nDeletion and privacy are the reason this shape is forced rather than merely cheap. Hydration is a per-shard batched read against the **primary** that fetches the post hash together with `visibility:{creator_id}` and membership in `blocks:{creator_id}` — all co-located under the same hash tag as the post, so the acknowledged write and the authorization check are the same shard-local state. A delete or privacy flip is one acknowledged transaction on the owning shard; every later request re-reads it and drops the item. A materialized fanout feed fails this test outright: honoring an acknowledged delete would require removing the post from millions of follower inboxes before the next request, which no fanout path can bound. Candidate discovery (`last_post_ts`, `ZRANGE`, pools) may read shard replicas, since replica lag only delays eligibility (bounded well under 30s) and can only surface a stale *ID*, which the primary-side visibility check then rejects — a tombstone that outlives the retention window guarantees the reject. Paging uses an opaque cursor carrying a frozen snapshot timestamp and ranking seed, plus a viewer-owned seen-list of the last 500 post IDs with a 24h TTL, so pages neither repeat nor shuffle. Scaling from launch to 10M DAU adds shards and replicas; component types, routing, and the failure contract are identical on day 1 and at vFinal.\n\n- **Canonical authorities** — one replicated, AOF-persisted, sharded Valkey cluster owns posts, per-creator recent-candidate windows, creator visibility and block sets, follow sets, viewer affinity vectors, seen-lists, and topic trending pools. Trending pools and affinity vectors are derived and rebuildable from the post/engagement keyspace; everything else is authoritative.\n- **Ownership keys** — `{creator_id}` for posts, recent window, `last_post_ts`, visibility, blocks; `{viewer_id}` for follow set, affinity vector, seen-list, cursor state; `{topic_id}` for trending pools. Routing contract is identical locally and at scale.\n- **Bounded request path** — ≤2,000 follow IDs read, one `MGET` per shard, ≤300 creator `ZRANGE`s, 4 pool reads, ≤1,500 scored candidates, ~30 hydrated. Roughly four pipelined round trips per shard touched; no unbounded fanout, no follower-list read, no cross-request coordination.\n- **Bounded background path** — owner-partitioned BullMQ inside the same app replicas, deterministic job IDs, retry-safe: trim per-creator candidate windows past 48h, and decay topic pool scores on a fixed schedule. No dedicated worker deployment, no broker, no scheduler service.\n- **Permanent components** — stateless HTTP app replicas; sharded replicated Valkey. No feed service, no fanout workers, no search/vector store, no model server, no CQRS projection, no distributed locks. Ranking runs in-process from periodically loaded weights.\n- **Unknowns preserved** — follow-count distribution, feed requests per user per day, and the recommendation quality bar are unsourced; capacity is therefore unproven, though the scale function (ops/sec ÷ shards) is linear in shard count. The load-bearing assumption is the 2,000-follow cap. **Revisit trigger:** if the product allows viewers to follow tens of thousands of creators, or if in-request pool ranking proves insufficient for recommendation quality, add a viewer-owned recency index maintained by a post-time fanout restricted to recently active followers — do not preinstall it now.",
