      "outputText": "**Recommendation**\n\nUse a hybrid fan-out: push on write for ordinary creators, pull on read for \"celebrity\" accounts above a follower threshold (~100k). When a post is created, it lands in the Posts store (source of truth) and emits an event; a fan-out worker pool writes `(post_id, timestamp, author_id)` entries into per-user timeline lists in Redis (capped at ~2000 entries, plus a cold overflow in a wide-column store for deep pagination). For high-follower authors, no fan-out happens; instead, read time merges the viewer's materialized timeline with a small per-author \"recent posts\" list for the handful of celebrities they follow, plus a recommendation candidate slice. That merge is bounded: at most N celebrity lists (cap the number merged, backfilling from the materialized timeline if a viewer follows an extreme number), each a short range scan. Ranking runs over a bounded candidate set (~500 items) using a feature-store lookup and a ranker with a strict latency budget and a chronological fallback. Pagination uses an opaque cursor encoding the ranking session seed plus position, so page 2 is consistent with page 1 rather than a re-ranked shuffle.\n\nCorrectness on deletion and privacy is enforced at read time, not by chasing fan-out entries. Timeline entries are treated as *hints*; every candidate is hydrated through a batch fetch against the Posts store and an authorization check against the current visibility/block state before it is returned. Deletes and privacy changes write synchronously to the authoritative stores and invalidate the relevant cache keys before the mutation is acknowledged, so any request that begins after the ack reads post-change state; hydration drops anything that no longer exists or is no longer visible, and the page is topped up from the remaining candidates. Fan-out is asynchronous but bounded — sharded by author, chunked into batches of followers, idempotent, and monitored so p99 post-to-eligible stays well under 30 seconds; a lagging or failed fan-out degrades to the pull path rather than showing stale or unauthorized content. At launch this runs as a single service with Redis and Postgres; the same seams (fan-out worker, ranker, hydration/auth layer) split into separate services as load grows, without changing the model.\n\n- **Canonical authorities:** Posts/content store (post existence, body, visibility) — the only truth for whether a post exists; Social graph store (follows, blocks, mutes); Account/privacy store (account-level private/public, suspension). Timeline lists, feature store, and all caches are derived and disposable — rebuildable from these three.\n- **Ownership keys:** Posts sharded by `post_id` (author-prefixed for locality); timelines owned by `viewer_id` (all writes to a viewer's list go through that shard); fan-out jobs keyed by `author_id` for ordering and idempotency; graph edges keyed by `(follower_id, followee_id)` with a reverse index by `followee_id`; ranking sessions keyed by `(viewer_id, session_seed)`.\n- **Bounded request path:** cursor decode → fetch ≤2000-entry timeline slice → merge ≤N celebrity author lists → recommendation candidates → cap at ~500 candidates → batch hydrate + authorize → rank under a fixed time budget (chronological fallback on timeout) → return one page. No unbounded scans, no per-item round trips, no synchronous fan-out.\n- **Bounded background path:** fan-out workers consuming the post event stream in fixed-size follower batches, idempotent by `(post_id, viewer_id)`, with per-author concurrency limits; timeline trimming to the cap; feature-store refresh; periodic reconciliation that rebuilds timelines from canonical stores. Backlog is visible and shed by falling back to pull, never by dropping correctness.\n- **Permanent components:** the read-time hydration + authorization gate (never removable — it is what makes deletes and privacy changes safe); the hybrid push/pull split with a follower threshold; opaque cursors for stable pagination; capped candidate sets and per-stage timeouts; canonical-store-first writes with cache invalidation before acknowledgment; timelines as rebuildable derived state.",
