      "outputText": "## Recommendation\n\nServe the feed by **read-time merge over the canonical post authority**; never materialize per-viewer feeds. One horizontally scalable stateless HTTP deployable plus one Redis/Valkey cluster sharded from day 1 by a stable owner hash tag: `{author_id}` for post bodies, per-author capped recent-post logs, and visibility/tombstone state; `{viewer_id}` for follow sets, feed cursors, and last-read position. Publishing a post is a single transaction on the author's shard that writes the post hash and appends its ID to that author's capped recent log (last N ids / 7-day window), so eligibility is immediate — far inside 30 s — and costs nothing per follower, which is what makes million-follower creators safe. Deletion or a privacy change is likewise one transaction on the same author shard flipping visibility and writing a tombstone; because every feed read resolves candidate IDs back to that same authority before serving, any request that starts after the acknowledgement cannot render the content. There is no fanout table, feed store, projection, broker, or feed worker to invalidate, and therefore no window in which a stale copy can leak.\n\nA feed request reads the viewer's follow set (capped at 5,000 follows), pipelines one bounded batched read per Redis shard for those authors' recent-log slices within the cursor's time window, unions in a bounded slice of recommendation candidates from a per-shard candidate pool keyed by the recommendation partition, then does one bounded `MGET`-style visibility+body fetch (≤ ~300 IDs) and ranks in the app replica. The cursor is opaque and carries `(score, last_id, window)` — no server-side pagination state to drift. Growth from launch to 10M DAU is served by adding stateless replicas and adding/reassigning Redis shards and read replicas; component types and routing contract at vFinal are identical to day 1, differing only in counts.\n\n- **Canonical authorities.** Redis/Valkey (replicated, AOF, no eviction on authoritative keys) is authoritative for posts, per-author recent logs, visibility/tombstones, follow edges, and cursors. Recommendation candidate pools are *derived* and may be stale; they carry no authorization or existence truth.\n- **Ownership keys.** `{author_id}` — post hash, capped recent-post log, visibility/tombstone. `{viewer_id}` — follow set, blocks, cursor, last-read. `{rec_partition}` — recommendation candidate pool. Same hash-tag routing locally and at 10M DAU.\n- **Bounded request path.** ≤5,000 follows read → one pipelined batch per shard → ≤300 candidate IDs → one bounded body/visibility fetch → in-replica ranking → one page. Work per request is capped by follow limit and page size, not by follower counts or corpus size.\n- **Bounded background path.** Only recommendation candidate-pool refresh, as owner-partitioned BullMQ jobs with deterministic IDs on ordinary app replicas. Its loss degrades recommendation freshness only; followed-post eligibility and all authorization are request-path.\n- **Permanent components.** Stateless HTTP app replicas; sharded replicated Redis/Valkey; BullMQ on that same Redis for candidate refresh. No feed store, no fanout-on-write, no Kafka/SQS, no CQRS, no separate feed or ranking service.\n- **Top failures and corrections.** (1) Hot celebrity shard on read — add read replicas and a ≤5 s in-replica cache of *candidate ID lists only*, with visibility always re-resolved authoritatively, preserving the deletion guarantee. (2) Deep pagination widening the merge — cursor pins the time window so per-page work stays capped; older pages truncate rather than scan. (3) Retention pressure as the corpus grows — recent logs are capped structures; feed eligibility is defined over the retained window, and the full post archive moves to a disk-backed authority only if measured RAM cost forces it.\n- **Revisit triggers.** A single author's read load exceeding one shard plus replicas; a required follow cap above ~5,000; or a ranking model needing unbounded cross-shard features per request. Absent those measurements, no additional plane is justified.",
