      "outputText": "## Recommendation\n\nRun one stateless HTTP deployable (feed read, post write, follow write) over one canonical Redis/Valkey Cluster authority, sharded from day 1 by hash-tagged owner keys — `{author_id}` for posts, per-author recency windows, block sets and privacy epoch; `{viewer_id}` for the following edge, affinity scores and the paging buffer. Feeds are built by pull, never by per-follower fanout. A feed build reads the viewer's `follows:{viewer}` ZSET (scored by affinity), takes the top K≈500 authors plus a rotating ~100-author tail sample so low-affinity follows aren't starved, and pipelines `ZRANGEBYSCORE` over those authors' recency windows plus a bounded recommendation pull (≤4 interest tags × 16 write-spread buckets × last 6 ten-minute slots, ~380 keys) — total ~1,000 keys collapsed into one round trip per shard. Ranking runs in-process in the same replicas over that bounded candidate pool; the ranked **post ID** list is stored as `feedbuf:{viewer}` with a ~10 min TTL, and each page then hydrates only ~25 IDs. Hydration is the authorization gate: a single shard-local Lua call under `{author_id}` reads the post record, the author's privacy epoch, and `blocks:{author}` together, and returns a post only if it is live, its visibility is satisfied by the viewer's already-loaded following set, and neither party blocks the other. Index entries are candidates, never content, so staleness can only shrink a page (backfilled from the buffer), never reveal. New posts write the record and `ZADD` the author window in one shard-local transaction, so eligibility is sub-second against the 30 s bound. Delete and privacy-change acks return only after `WAITAOF 1 1` plus `WAIT 1 <timeout>`, which makes the revocation durable and replica-visible *before* the client is acknowledged — which is what lets read-heavy window reads be served from replicas without breaking the revocation contract.\n\nThe fanout-on-write plane collapses: a 50 M-follower post would require ~50 M inbox writes inside 30 s (~1.7 M writes/s for one post) versus one write here, and materialized inboxes would additionally require scrubbing millions of rows on every deletion or privacy flip — the deletion contract is *easier* to hold with no copies. The per-viewer feed projection collapses into the TTL'd ID buffer, which survives only because personalization scores are non-stationary, so a score cursor would duplicate and skip rows across pages. A second datastore collapses because every access is owner-keyed point/range work Redis structures already serve; a feed or ranking service collapses because a module in the existing replicas owns it. No broker, no worker deployment, no scheduler, no distributed lock, no CQRS. Day 1 (3 shards + replicas, a few app replicas) and vFinal use identical component types and identical routing; only shard, replica, and app-replica counts change.\n\n**Canonical authorities and ownership keys**\n- `post:{author_id}:<post_id>` (record, visibility, `deleted_at`), `win:{author_id}` (ZSET, last ~200 posts / 7 days, trimmed inline on write), `priv:{author_id}` (privacy epoch), `blocks:{author_id}` — authority for content existence and visibility; key = `author_id`.\n- `follows:{viewer_id}` (ZSET author_id → affinity) — canonical follow-edge authority and the follower-only authorization input; key = `viewer_id`. `followers:{author_id}` and follower counts are **derived**, gated by the viewer-shard `ZADD NX` return, never on the feed path.\n- `trend:{tag:b}:<10min-slot>` (b∈0..15, TTL'd) — recommendation candidates; buckets spread one hot tag's writes across up to 16 shards, slots expire instead of being swept.\n- `feedbuf:{viewer_id}` — TTL'd ranked ID list for paging; IDs only, key = `viewer_id`.\n- Object storage for media bytes (Redis memory is the wrong fit above ~KBs); the post record holds the reference.\n\n**Bounded request path** — feed build: 1 follow-set read + ~600 author-window reads + ~380 trend reads, pipelined to ≤ shard-count round trips, once per session or on buffer exhaustion/staleness. Page: ~25 hydrate+authorize calls under `{author_id}`, over-fetched ~1.4× to survive filtering. Post write: 1 shard-local transaction. Follow: 1 viewer-shard write + 1 derived author-shard delta.\n\n**Bounded background path** — none on day 1. Trimming and trend decay are inline or TTL-driven. The only conditionally forced async work is media transcode if video is in scope: one author-partitioned BullMQ queue inside the same replicas, deterministic job ID = upload ID.\n\n**Permanent components** — stateless HTTP app replicas; one Redis/Valkey Cluster (AOF + replicas) as canonical authority; object storage for media. Nothing else is added at scale.\n\n**Top failures and corrections**\n- *Hot-creator read skew* on `win:{author}`: serve window and hydrate reads from replicas — safe precisely because revocation acks require replica confirmation. Scales by replica count per shard; revisit if one key's read bandwidth exceeds a shard's replica set.\n- *Wide-follow viewers* (10 k follows) blowing up merge cost: bounded by top-K + rotating tail. Tradeoff: long-tail follows surface less often; reverse if measured recall on long-tail follows is unacceptable, by raising K (cost is linear, topology unchanged).\n- *Crash between the two follow writes*: follower count drifts by one until the next mutation of that edge repairs it. Feed correctness and authorization never read that index.\n\n**Unproven capacity and revisit triggers** — Under assumed ~4 builds/user/day at 10 M DAU (~2 k builds/s peak × ~1 k keys ≈ 2 M ops/s), ~128 shards at ~16 k ops/s each suffices; posts/sec, session count, and the follow-count distribution are **unknown**, so this envelope is unproven and the shard count is a count, not a shape. Revisit the shape only if: ranking requires accelerator hardware (then, and only then, an inference tier behind the same replicas), or per-owner working set exceeds one shard's memory for a single owner (not aggregate — aggregate growth is answered by more shards).",
