      "outputText": "## Recommended architecture\n\nUse a **hybrid fan-out with read-time authorization**: a single Posts service writes each post to the durable post store (Postgres, sharded by `author_id`, with an outbox), and an outbox consumer publishes to Kafka partitioned by `author_id`. A fan-out worker materializes the post into per-follower timeline lists in Redis (sorted by post timestamp, capped at ~1000 entries per user) for authors below a follower threshold (~50k). Authors above that threshold are marked `pull_author` and are **not** fanned out; instead each is written to a per-author recent-posts list. At read time the Feed service unions three sources: the viewer's materialized push timeline, a pull-merge over the viewer's `pull_author` follow set (typically tens of authors, each a single cached list read), and a recommendation candidate list produced by an offline/near-line pipeline and stored per user. The union is deduplicated, scored by a ranking service over cached features, and paginated by an opaque cursor encoding `(score_bucket, post_id, ranking_model_version, seen_set_bloom)` so pagination is stable and stateless. Fan-out lag targets p99 under 10s, well inside the 30s eligibility budget; pull authors are always fresh because their posts are read live.\n\nThe timeline lists are **treated as a hint cache, never as an authority**. Every candidate post ID is hydrated through a batched fetch against the post store fronted by a versioned cache, and a visibility check runs on the hydrated result before it can enter a response page. Deletion and privacy changes write synchronously to the post store and bump a per-post `visibility_version` plus a per-author `visibility_epoch` in the strongly consistent path *before* the mutation is acknowledged; hydration reads at or after that version, so any post that fails the check is dropped from the page and backfilled from the next candidate. Stale IDs may linger in Redis and are lazily reaped, which is safe because they can never survive hydration. Asynchronous fan-out retraction is a storage-hygiene job, not a correctness mechanism.\n\n- **Canonical authorities**\n  - Post content, deletion tombstones, and per-post visibility: sharded Postgres post store (the only source of truth for what a viewer may see).\n  - Follow graph and block/mute relations: graph service backed by its own sharded store.\n  - Account state and privacy mode: identity service.\n  - Everything else — Redis timelines, feature cache, recommendation candidates, search indexes — is derived and rebuildable.\n\n- **Ownership keys**\n  - `author_id` — shards post storage, Kafka partitions, and fan-out work; guarantees per-author ordering.\n  - `viewer_id` — shards materialized timelines, recommendation candidate lists, seen-state, and feed request routing.\n  - `post_id` (Snowflake, time-ordered) — hydration cache key and cursor tiebreaker.\n  - `(viewer_id, author_id)` — follow edge and per-edge visibility decisions.\n\n- **Bounded request path** (target p99 ≈ 150ms)\n  - Fixed page size (≤ 30 returned) and a hard candidate cap (~600 pre-rank).\n  - Pull-merge capped at the top N=100 most-recently-active pull authors the viewer follows; the remainder are covered by the recommendation path.\n  - Batched hydration, bounded concurrency, per-dependency deadlines with degraded fallbacks: ranking timeout → recency ordering; recommendation timeout → followed-only feed. **Hydration and visibility checks have no fallback and never degrade** — if they fail, the page fails.\n  - At most one backfill round to refill slots dropped by visibility filtering.\n\n- **Bounded background paths**\n  - Fan-out workers: chunked follower scans (5k per batch), per-author rate caps, idempotent writes keyed on `(viewer_id, post_id)`, dead-letter queue.\n  - Timeline trimming to the cap and TTL eviction of inactive viewers, with on-demand rebuild from source on cache miss.\n  - Feature/candidate generation on a fixed cadence, decoupled from the write path.\n  - Tombstone reaper and follower-threshold reclassification jobs.\n\n- **Permanent components**\n  - Posts service + sharded post store with transactional outbox.\n  - Kafka post-event log, partitioned by `author_id`.\n  - Fan-out workers and Redis materialized timelines (hint cache).\n  - Graph service for follows and blocks.\n  - Feed service: candidate union, dedup, cursor pagination, degradation policy.\n  - Ranking service and feature store.\n  - Recommendation candidate pipeline and per-viewer candidate store.\n  - Hydration + visibility layer, versioned against `visibility_version` / `visibility_epoch` — the single mandatory gate on every returned post.",
