      "outputText": "## Recommendation\n\nBuild this as one horizontally scalable stateless HTTP deployable over a single sharded, replicated, disk-backed Redis/Valkey authority, with **fanout-on-read only**. A post write lands in exactly two places on the author's shard: the immutable post record (body, visibility, deleted flag) and the author's capped recent-post log (a sorted set of the last N posts / last few days, hash-tagged `{author_id}`). Nothing is written per follower, so a creator with ten million followers costs the same write as a creator with ten. A feed request reads the viewer's following list from the viewer's shard (hash-tagged `{viewer_id}`), takes the top-K authors by stored affinity when the list exceeds a fixed cap, issues K bounded cursor `ZRANGEBYSCORE` reads against those author logs, merges with a bounded slice of recommendation candidates, ranks, and returns one page behind an opaque cursor encoding the time floor and per-source offsets. K, the page size, and the per-author window are hard caps, so each request touches a bounded number of shards and rows regardless of population — that is the property that makes 10M DAU a matter of adding shards and replicas, not changing shape.\n\nCorrectness for deletion and privacy comes from never letting a copy of a post outlive the authority: candidate IDs may be merged and ranked from logs and pools, but **hydration always reads the authoritative post record on the author's shard and drops anything deleted, made private, or not permitted for this viewer**, after ranking and before serialization. Because the delete/privacy write and the hydration read hit the same key on the same shard, an acknowledged change is visible to every later request; short pages are returned rather than backfilled from stale state. Eligibility within 30 seconds is satisfied by the same path — the log append is part of the post transaction, so followed posts are readable immediately, and recommendation pools refresh on a bounded post-write job well inside the bound. Recommendations use a small set of shared candidate pools (global and per-topic/locale sorted sets, capped and TTL'd, updated by recipient-free owner-partitioned BullMQ jobs triggered on post write and on periodic decay), personalized at read time by scoring against the viewer's affinity vector and filtering against a capped, TTL'd per-viewer seen-set on the viewer's shard. No per-viewer materialized feed exists anywhere, which is why no deletion needs to chase millions of inboxes.\n\n- **Canonical authorities** — sharded replicated Redis/Valkey (AOF, no eviction on authoritative keyspaces) owns: post records, author recent-post logs, follow edges, viewer affinity and seen-sets, recommendation pools. Candidate pools and affinity are derived and rebuildable; post records and follow edges are authoritative.\n- **Ownership keys** — posts and author logs by `{author_id}`; following list, affinity, seen-set, and cursor state by `{viewer_id}`; follow edges written to both sides in one operation per side, each idempotent on `(follower_id, author_id)`. Recommendation pools by `{segment_id}` (topic/locale), a bounded fixed set.\n- **Bounded request path** — 1 following-list read + ≤K author-log cursor reads (K fixed, e.g. 500 top-affinity authors) + ≤1 pool read + ≤page-size×small-factor hydration reads with authorization filter. No unbounded scatter, no cross-shard transaction, no request that grows with follower count or post count.\n- **Bounded background path** — owner-partitioned BullMQ on the same Redis/Valkey, deterministic job IDs, retry-safe: post-write pool insertion, periodic pool decay/trim, affinity update. Loss of any job degrades recommendation freshness only; it never changes durable truth or visibility.\n- **Permanent components** — stateless HTTP replicas; sharded replicated Redis/Valkey; BullMQ on that same instance; blob storage + CDN for media bytes, with signed short-TTL URLs so a privacy change stops new URL issuance. That is the entire component graph on day 1 and at vFinal; scaling is more shards, more replicas on hot author shards, more app replicas.\n- **Top failures and corrections** — (1) *Hot author shard read pressure*: millions of viewers tail one creator's log; correction is read replicas on that shard plus a 1–5s in-process TTL cache of **candidate IDs only**, never hydrated content, preserving the privacy invariant. (2) *Very large following lists* break the K cap; correction is the affinity top-K with a \"less-followed authors\" rotation slot so tail creators still surface. (3) *Deep pagination* drifting past the author-log window; correction is cursor time-floor plus a fixed max page depth, degrading to recommendations beyond it.\n- **Invalidating assumptions** — per-viewer following counts and per-author post rates are unknown; if measured K-scatter latency exceeds the page SLO at p99, the first correction is narrowing K and widening the pool slice, not introducing per-follower fanout-on-write. If ranking ever requires a model whose features cannot be computed within the bounded read, that — and only that — forces a scoring plane.",
