      "outputText": "## Recommendation\n\nBuild a **hybrid fan-out feed over a pointer-only timeline index, with authorization and content resolved at read time.** On post creation, the write commits to the canonical post store, which emits an ordered event to a partitioned log; fan-out workers append *pointers* — `(post_id, author_id, created_at, source_tag)`, never post content — to per-viewer timeline partitions for authors below a hot-creator threshold (start at 50k followers). Authors above that threshold are never fanned out; instead their post lands synchronously in a per-creator `recent_posts` list, and the read path pulls from at most the viewer's top-K hot follows (K = 150, ranked by affinity, cached per viewer). A feed request therefore does a fixed number of bounded fetches in parallel: one timeline range read, K hot-creator list reads, and one recommendation-candidate fetch from a precomputed per-viewer rec store; these merge into a capped candidate pool (~800), get scored by a single-pass ranker over a feature-store lookup, and the top 20 are hydrated and returned. Pagination uses an opaque cursor carrying a watermark timestamp, rank seed, and a seen-set digest, so pages are stable and non-duplicating without server-side session state.\n\nCorrectness for deletion and privacy falls out of the pointer-only design: **timeline entries are unauthoritative hints, and every candidate is re-authorized and re-hydrated against canonical state before it can be rendered.** A delete or privacy mutation is acknowledged only after it is durable in the canonical store *and* the subject's visibility epoch has been bumped in the authorizer; all read-path caches are keyed by that epoch, so stale entries become unreachable rather than needing invalidation, and any post whose authorization lookup fails is dropped (fail-closed, never rendered). Fan-out cleanup is thus a background garbage-collection concern, not a correctness dependency, which is what lets fan-out lag without leaking. New-post eligibility inside 30 seconds is met on both paths — hot-creator posts are visible immediately via the synchronous `recent_posts` write, and fanned-out posts need only the log-to-worker append to land, with the worker pipeline sized so p99 fan-out completion stays under ~10s. At launch this runs as one service over Postgres plus Redis with the log as a single topic; the component seams and ownership keys below are the same at 10M DAU, where they become separately deployed and separately sharded services.\n\n## Canonical authorities\n\n- **Post store** — sole authority for post existence, body, and audience setting; partitioned by `post_id`, with an `author_id` + `created_at` secondary index that backs `recent_posts`.\n- **Social graph store** — sole authority for follow edges, blocks, and mutes.\n- **Authorizer / visibility service** — sole authority for the *decision* \"may viewer V see post P now\"; owns tombstones and per-subject visibility epochs; the only component permitted to say yes.\n- **Event log** — sole authority for the ordering of post/edge/visibility mutations consumed by background workers.\n- **Feature + model registry** — sole authority for the ranker's feature definitions and deployed model version.\n- Explicitly *not* authorities: timeline index, rec candidate store, and all caches. These are derived, may be stale, may be rebuilt from the authorities at any time.\n\n## Ownership keys\n\n- `viewer_id` — timeline partition, seen-set, hot-follow list, rec candidate slate, cursor decoding. One viewer's feed state lives on one shard.\n- `creator_id` — follower list, `recent_posts` list, fan-out job range. Hot creators are isolated so their write amplification never touches viewer shards.\n- `post_id` — canonical post row and its tombstone.\n- `(subject_id, epoch)` — every read-path cache entry, where subject is a post, an author, or a viewer. Epoch bump is the invalidation primitive.\n- `(viewer_id, creator_id)` — follow edge, stored twice (by viewer for \"who I follow\", by creator for \"who follows me\"); the by-viewer copy is canonical, the by-creator copy is a derived index rebuilt from the log.\n\n## Bounded request path\n\n- One timeline range read (≤300 pointers) + ≤150 hot-creator list reads + one rec slate read, all issued in parallel, all with per-call deadlines.\n- Candidate pool hard-capped at 800; ranker is single-pass, no per-candidate remote calls beyond one batched feature fetch and one batched authorization check.\n- Hydration is one batched multi-get sized to the page (20 items, over-fetch factor 2 to absorb authorization drops).\n- End-to-end budget p99 ≤ 300ms. No synchronous fan-out, no unbounded scans, no cross-shard joins, no retry storms — a source that misses its deadline is dropped from the merge and the page ships short.\n- Degradation ladder: rec store unavailable → follow-only feed; feature store unavailable → recency-ordered; authorizer unavailable → serve nothing rather than serve unfiltered.\n\n## Bounded background paths\n\n- **Fan-out workers**: follower lists processed in fixed 10k-follower chunks, rate-limited per creator, idempotent by `(post_id, viewer_id)`.\n- **Timeline trimming**: retain 5,000 pointers or 30 days per viewer, whichever is smaller.\n- **Tombstone GC**: lazily purges pointers for deleted/hidden posts; correctness-neutral, purely a cost optimization.\n- **Rec slate generation**: per-viewer candidate refresh on a fixed cadence (~hourly, plus event-triggered), writing a capped slate of ~500.\n- **Backfill / rebuild**: replays the log to reconstruct timelines, the by-creator edge index, or rec slates after a schema or model change.\n- **Hot-creator classifier**: promotes and demotes creators across the fan-out threshold with hysteresis, so a creator crossing the line does not thrash.\n\n## Permanent components\n\nThese exist at launch and at 10M DAU; only their implementation and sharding change.\n\n- Canonical post store and social graph store.\n- Ordered event log between writes and all derived state.\n- Timeline index (pointer-only, per viewer) and per-creator `recent_posts` index.\n- Merge-and-rank service with an opaque, stateless-server cursor.\n- Authorizer with epoch-keyed caching and fail-closed semantics — the single choke point every rendered item passes through.\n- Hydration service (batched, authorization-gated).\n- Feature store and rec candidate store.\n- Per-viewer seen-set for cross-session dedup.",
