      "messageSetId": "17aa1e9b8009726c17b1d5e2ce2c1d5cfb84f9fcd034107b82e5d3d5062347ac",
      "outputText": "## Recommendation\n\nBuild the feed as **merge-on-read over per-author recent-post indexes** — no per-follower timeline, no fanout-on-write, no broker. Posts live in one replicated disk-backed transactional store sharded by `author_id`; the row carries visibility state (`public|followers|private|deleted`) and a monotonic `visibility_version`. Publishing is a single transaction on the author's shard plus an inline `ZADD` into that author's capped recent-post index in a sharded Redis/Valkey cluster keyed `{author:<id>}:recent` (score = publish time, trimmed to the last ~500 posts / 7 days). Because publish is one write to one owner, a post with ten million followers is eligible the moment it commits — eligibility latency is a single write, not a fanout, so the 30 s bound is met by construction and holds identically at any follower count. A feed request runs entirely in a stateless HTTP replica: read the viewer's follow set from `{viewer:<id>}`, issue one pipelined batch per Redis shard over those authors' recent indexes (≤ shard-count round trips, ≤ 20 IDs per author), pull recommendation candidates from the viewer's top ~10 affinity topics via `{topic:<id>}:hot` hourly-bucket ZSETs, score the merged ≤ 600 candidates in-process, and persist the ranked candidate ID list plus seen-set in a TTL'd session key under `{viewer:<id>}` so pages 2..N slice from it instead of re-merging.\n\nDeletion and privacy correctness is enforced by **authority hydration on every page**, never by invalidation. The indexes hold candidate IDs only; before returning, the replica hydrates the top ~1.5× page size from each post's owning author shard and evaluates the visibility predicate against the freshly read row plus the viewer↔author relationship (follow / block / private-approval, read from the viewer's and author's shards in the same request), dropping failures and backfilling from the remaining ranked candidates. A delete or privacy change is acknowledged only after the authoritative row commits; index removal is best-effort, and a hydration that finds a gone/downgraded post deletes the stale index entry inline (read repair). No copy of a post exists anywhere that could outlive its authority, which is precisely what a fanout-on-write timeline would create — that is the collapse test the timeline store fails, alongside the unbounded 10M-row write per celebrity post. The cost of this shape is read-side work proportional to follows-per-viewer at session start; that is the tradeoff I am accepting, and it reverses only if measured p99 follow counts or session rates push per-shard index reads past what shard count can absorb.\n\n- **Canonical authorities** — Posts, visibility state, and follow/block edges: one replicated disk-backed transactional store, sharded. Redis/Valkey holds only derived, rebuildable state (recent-post indexes, topic candidate ZSETs, affinity vectors, session/paging state); nothing user-facing is authoritative there.\n- **Ownership keys** — `author_id` for posts and per-author recent indexes (`{author:<id>}`); `viewer_id` for follow set, affinity, session/seen-set (`{viewer:<id>}`); `topic_id` for recommendation candidate lists (`{topic:<id>}`). Same hash-tag routing contract on day 1 (1 shard) and at 10M DAU (tens of shards).\n- **Bounded request path** — 1 follow-set read → ≤ shard-count pipelined index batches (≤ 5,000 authors hard-capped, ≤ 20 IDs each) → ≤ 600 candidates → in-process scoring → ≤ 45 authority hydrations → 30 rendered items. Every bound is a constant or a product cap, independent of follower count and total users.\n- **Bounded background path** — none permanent. Index trim, topic-bucket increment, and stale-entry removal happen inline on write or read. Index rebuild after a shard restore is an operational leased-batch scan from the author shard, executed in ordinary replicas.\n- **Permanent components (day 1 ≡ vFinal, differing only in counts)** — stateless HTTP app replicas; sharded transactional store; sharded Redis/Valkey with replicas. Deleted by collapse tests: per-follower timeline store, fanout workers, queue/broker, invalidation bus, ranking service, ANN/feature store, CDC pipeline.\n- **Stated assumptions / unknowns** — Unsourced: posts/day, follows per viewer (assumed p50 ~300, hard cap 5,000), sessions/day (~4), page size 30, recent window 7 days. Capacity is therefore **unproven**; what is proven is topology continuity and that per-request work is capped by product limits rather than by user or follower totals.\n- **Top failures and corrections** — (1) *Celebrity hot key*: one author's recent index is read by every follower; serve per-author index reads from Redis replicas plus a ≤ 5 s process-local slice cache — safe because visibility is decided at hydration, and 5 s fits inside the 30 s budget. (2) *Deep paging duplication/skips* as new posts arrive mid-session: cursor pins a snapshot cutoff and carries the session key; seen-set capped, paging depth capped at ~500 items then \"caught up.\" (3) *Index drift after restore*: reads are correct regardless (authority hydration), stale entries self-heal; a full rebuild is a bounded scan. **Revisit triggers**: measured p99 follow count or session rate pushing per-shard index reads beyond shard capacity; a ranking model too large to run in-process (would force a scoring plane, not a new authority); recommendation quality requiring embedding retrieval that topic ZSETs cannot express.",
