      "outputText": "Use a hybrid fan-out architecture. A post is committed with an outbox record to a sharded, strongly consistent Post Store; change-data capture publishes it to Kafka, where workers update the creator’s recent-post index, recommendation index, and follower inboxes. Fan-out-on-write is used below a configurable follower threshold; high-fan-out creators remain fan-out-on-read. A feed request merges a bounded set—such as 1,000 candidates—from the viewer’s inbox, recent-post heads for up to 256 high-fan-out follows, and recommendation retrieval, then ranks and stores the top 500 IDs as a short-lived feed session. A signed cursor pages through that session, preventing reorder and duplicate problems. The event pipeline is monitored against a 20-second internal deadline, leaving margin for the 30-second eligibility requirement.\n\nEvery returned page passes through a final batched authorization gate using linearizable reads of current post tombstones, creator privacy state, and viewer-specific access. Deletion or privacy-change acknowledgement occurs only after that authority commits the new version; caches and asynchronous cleanup improve performance but are never trusted for disclosure decisions. Each page—including pages from an existing feed session—is reauthorized, and media is served through an authorization-aware gateway using short-lived signed URLs. Capacity must ultimately be provisioned from measured peak request, posting, follow, and regional traffic rates; 10 million DAU alone does not determine QPS.\n\n- **Canonical authorities:** Post Store for content metadata and tombstones; Visibility Store for privacy and ACL versions; Follow Graph Store for follow relationships; Creator Store for account status; Object Store for media bytes. Kafka, indexes, inboxes, caches, and feed sessions are derived state.\n- **Ownership keys:** posts and creator indexes by `creator_id`; follower fan-out work by `(creator_id, follower_shard)`; inboxes, feed sessions, and seen history by `viewer_id`; recommendations by `(viewer_id, model_version)`; media by immutable `media_id`.\n- **Bounded request path:** read at most 1,000 candidates, inspect at most 256 high-fan-out creator heads, rank once per session, return at most 50 items per page, and perform one batched authorization check for those items. Use deadlines and partial candidate-source results rather than unbounded graph scans.\n- **Bounded background path:** partition each fan-out job into fixed-size follower shards, use idempotent event/version keys, cap retries with dead-letter queues, and apply backpressure. High-fan-out posts create index entries rather than millions of synchronous inbox writes.\n- **Permanent components:** API gateway, feed/session service, candidate merger, ranking service and feature store, authorization/media gateways, Post/Visibility/Follow/Creator stores, Kafka-compatible event log, stream processors, inbox and creator-post indexes, recommendation retrieval system, object storage/CDN, cache layer, schema registry, observability, replay tooling, and regional disaster-recovery replicas.",
