      "outputText": "Use one horizontally scaled application deployment with a replicated, disk-backed Redis/Valkey authority, sharded by stable user or creator ownership. Publishing atomically records the post and an outbox entry on the creator shard; ordinary app replicas consume that outbox and enqueue deterministic, recipient-partitioned fanout chunks that append post IDs to follower inboxes. The same background path generates bounded recommendation candidates. Feed requests read a bounded candidate window from the viewer’s inbox and recommendation set, rank it, then bulk-check every selected post against its authoritative visibility/version record before returning it. Deletion or privacy-change acknowledgement occurs only after the authoritative visibility update commits; therefore stale inbox entries can reduce yield but can never disclose content. Capacity grows by adding shards, replicas, and fanout consumers without changing component types.\n\nThe 30-second eligibility target requires fanout capacity sized to the largest measured follower distribution and publish burst; those envelopes are unknown, so capacity is unproven. Fanout is deliberately chunked and backpressured, with celebrity posts using the same chunk protocol rather than synchronous writes. A product-defined maximum page size and bounded over-fetch window are required; extreme follow counts do not increase request work. Recommendations may be stale or absent during background-path failure, while followed-post ingestion retries from the durable outbox.\n\n- **Canonical authorities:** Creator-sharded post records and visibility/version heads; user-sharded follow edges and privacy grants. Inbox and recommendation lists are derived, rebuildable indexes.\n- **Ownership keys:** `{creator_id}` for posts and publication outboxes; `{viewer_id}` for follows, inboxes, recommendation candidates, and pagination cursors.\n- **Bounded request path:** Read fixed candidate windows, rank a fixed over-fetch limit, bulk-authorize at most that limit, and return one capped page; no request fans out across all followed creators or followers.\n- **Bounded background path:** Deterministic publication jobs enumerate followers in fixed-size cursor chunks and append idempotently to recipient-owned inboxes; recommendation jobs emit capped candidate sets. Retries resume from durable cursors, and backlog age is monitored against 30 seconds.\n- **Permanent components:** Stateless application replicas containing feed, ranking, authorization, and job handlers; sharded replicated Redis/Valkey authority and native queue/outbox structures. No separate feed service, broker, cache, CQRS system, or dedicated worker deployment.",
