      "outputText": "Use a hybrid fan-out architecture. A publish transaction in an externally consistent database writes the post, its visibility version, an author-timeline entry, and an outbox event. Ordinary creators’ post IDs are asynchronously copied into follower inboxes; high-fanout creators remain fan-out-on-read. The first feed page also performs a batched “freshness lane” read of recent author-timeline heads, so the synchronous timeline write—not million-recipient fanout—makes every accepted post rank-eligible within 30 seconds. Recommendations are precomputed as per-viewer candidate IDs, then mixed and ranked online with followed-post candidates.\n\nInbox, timeline, recommendation, and rendered-card caches are only hints. Before returning anything, the feed service batch-hydrates candidates through a linearizable authorization read covering the post tombstone, current post/account privacy version, follow relationship, and blocks. Deletion or privacy APIs acknowledge only after that authoritative commit; any feed request beginning afterward must observe it. Stale candidate IDs are discarded, media URLs are issued only after authorization, and authority failures fail closed.\n\n- **Canonical authorities:** The strongly consistent database owns posts, tombstones, account/post privacy policies, visibility epochs, follow/block edges, and the transactional author timeline. Private object storage owns media bytes; database policy—not the CDN—controls access.\n\n- **Ownership keys:** Posts use `post_id`; author timelines use `(creator_id, reverse_timestamp, post_id)`; home inboxes and recommendation sets use `(viewer_id, reverse_timestamp/score)`; follows use `(viewer_id, creator_id)`. Reverse follower scans use `(creator_id, hash(viewer_id), viewer_id)` buckets so celebrity creators do not create one hot partition.\n\n- **Bounded request path:** Enforce a finite following limit, initially 5,000. In parallel, read capped batches from the viewer inbox, recommendation set, and fixed-shard author-head aggregator; admit at most 600 candidates, authorize them in one batch, rank 30, and permit at most one bounded refill. Opaque cursors carry source offsets, ranking version, and deduplication state; every page reauthorizes candidates.\n\n- **Bounded background paths:** Fanout applies only below an initial 50,000-follower threshold and is split into idempotent jobs of at most 500 inbox writes. Larger creators require one timeline write regardless of follower count. Outbox events are at-least-once, keyed by `creator_id`, with deduplication by `post_id`, retries, dead-letter handling, and lag alarms. Purging stale derived entries after privacy changes is asynchronous cleanup, never the security mechanism.\n\n- **Permanent components:** Stateless post/graph/feed services, the consistent authority database, durable event log and workers, sharded timeline/inbox store, cache, private object store/CDN, authorization layer, online ranker, offline/stream recommendation pipeline, and observability. They can begin co-deployed at small capacity and split horizontally without changing ownership or correctness boundaries.\n\n- **Important unknowns:** The fanout threshold, candidate budgets, retention, ranking model, regional topology, and capacity targets must be tuned from post rate, feed-request rate, follow distribution, and latency measurements; DAU alone does not determine them.",
