      "outputText": "Use a cell-based architecture, partitioned by `viewer_id`, with globally replicated Spanner tables as the source of truth and Kafka as the change stream. A post transaction writes metadata, visibility policy, and an outbox event atomically. Creators below a configurable fanout threshold—initially 50,000 followers—are fanned into per-viewer inboxes in ScyllaDB; larger creators are stored once in creator timelines and pulled at read time. Each feed request combines inbox entries, batched recent timelines for followed high-fanout creators, and precomputed recommendation candidates, then ranks at most 2,000 candidates. Store the resulting top 500 IDs in a short-lived feed-session record and paginate by session ID and offset, providing stable pages and deduplication. A 5,000-account following limit keeps pull work bounded; the fanout threshold and candidate quotas remain capacity knobs that must be tuned from measured posting and follow distributions.\n\nGuarantee 30-second eligibility by giving post events a priority Kafka lane, targeting 25 seconds for ordinary fanout, and synchronously placing high-fanout posts in the pull index before acknowledging creation. Inbox entries and caches are only hints: immediately before serialization, the feed service batch-checks every selected `post_id` against a strongly consistent visibility service containing tombstones, policy versions, and viewer authorization. Deletion or privacy-change acknowledgement occurs only after that authoritative gate commits; therefore any feed request beginning afterward observes the new state. Cache invalidation, inbox cleanup, search removal, and media purge remain asynchronous optimizations, while media URLs are issued only after this final check and expire quickly.\n\n- **Canonical authorities:** Spanner post/visibility rows for existence and audience policy; Spanner follow/block edges for authorization; object storage for media bytes; versioned ranking configuration and models for ranking behavior. Kafka, ScyllaDB inboxes, recommendation indexes, Redis caches, and feed sessions are derived state.\n\n- **Ownership keys:** `creator_id` owns posts, creator timelines, and outbound fanout events; `viewer_id` owns follows, inboxes, preferences, recommendation sets, and feed sessions; `post_id` owns the visibility tombstone and policy version. Kafka partitions use `creator_id`; feed cells and request routing use `viewer_id`.\n\n- **Bounded request/background paths:** One request contacts one viewer cell, reads at most 1,500 inbox candidates, 500 followed high-fanout candidates through batched shard RPCs, and 500 recommendations; ranks at most 2,000 after deduplication and performs one batched authoritative visibility check. Fanout jobs process fixed-size follower pages, checkpoint progress, retry idempotently, and automatically move creators to pull mode when projected completion would exceed 25 seconds.\n\n- **Permanent components:** API gateway; post, graph, visibility, candidate, ranking, and feed-session services; Spanner; Kafka plus schema registry; ScyllaDB; object storage/CDN; Redis; feature/model store; offline warehouse and training pipeline; dead-letter/replay tooling; metrics, tracing, audit logs, and capacity/SLO automation.",
