      "outputText": "Use a hybrid fanout architecture: every post is written once to a canonical post/visibility store and published to a durable event log. For ordinary creators, a background fanout worker appends post IDs to followers’ bounded inbox shards; for high-follower creators, posts remain in a creator outbox and are pulled at read time. The Feed API fetches a fixed-size candidate set from the viewer inbox, followed-creator outboxes, and a recommendation candidate service, then ranks and returns a cursor based on a stable snapshot timestamp. Streaming delivery plus priority fanout makes a new post eligible within 30 seconds without attempting millions of synchronous writes.\n\nDeletion and privacy changes update the canonical visibility record synchronously and are acknowledged only after a strongly consistent revocation fence is committed. Feed inboxes, outboxes, ranking caches, and recommendation indexes may retain stale post IDs, but every candidate—and any media URL issued for it—is revalidated against the canonical post state, audience policy, and current relationship state before response. Thus cleanup can be asynchronous while any feed request beginning after acknowledgement cannot return content the viewer is no longer authorized to see.\n\n- **Canonical authorities:** post body/status and deletion tombstones; privacy/audience policy; follow/block relationship graph; user authorization state; recommendation-model inputs; durable post-and-policy event log.\n- **Ownership keys:** `post_id` for post and visibility state; `creator_id` for creator outboxes; `viewer_id` for inbox shards, feed cursors, and personalization features; `(viewer_id, creator_id)` for relationship state; `post_id + visibility_version` for cache validation.\n- **Bounded request paths:** read at most a configured number of inbox/outbox/recommendation candidates; rank a fixed top-K; perform batched visibility checks; issue short-lived authorized media tokens; use cursor pagination rather than offset scans.\n- **Bounded background paths:** partitioned event consumers; adaptive fanout only below a capacity-tuned follower threshold; rate-limited backfills and recommendation indexing; asynchronous stale-ID/tombstone compaction; dead-letter and replay support.\n- **Permanent components:** strongly consistent post/visibility service; relationship service; durable event log; fanout workers; creator-outbox and viewer-inbox stores; feed candidate/ranking service; visibility enforcement service; recommendation pipeline; cache layer that is never an authorization authority; observability, replay, and audit systems.",
