      "outputText": "Use a **hybrid fanout architecture**: push ordinary creators’ post IDs into followers’ bounded candidate inboxes, and pull recent posts from high-fanout creators when assembling feeds. Choose the fanout threshold from measured delivery cost and enforce a per-post work budget. Store canonical posts, visibility policies, follows, and blocks in a strongly consistent distributed SQL database. A publishing transaction writes the post, its creator’s recent-post index, and an outbox event; an event log drives inbox delivery and recommendation indexing. These indexes contain candidates, not permission grants. Make posts eligible within 30 seconds through monitored processing deadlines and contiguous source watermarks; a request whose sources cannot meet that freshness bound must use a bounded authoritative fallback or fail explicitly. Eligibility means a post can enter candidate selection, not that ranking guarantees an impression.\n\nFor each page, fetch bounded candidate sets from the viewer’s inbox, followed high-fanout creators, and a recommendation index; deduplicate, score with a versioned ranker, then hydrate authorized results. Deletion and privacy changes commit to canonical authority before acknowledgment. **Every feed response must pass a final, batched, strongly consistent authorization check** covering post deletion, current visibility, and relevant follow/block relationships; never authorize from candidate caches or stale replicas, and fail closed if authority is unavailable. Content and media delivery must enforce the same policy through an authorization gateway, including cached objects; publicly reusable asset URLs cannot provide this guarantee. Run the same logical architecture at launch with small deployments, then scale partitions and workers independently. Ten million daily users alone does not determine capacity: peak requests, posting rates, follow distributions, retention, and ranking cost remain sizing inputs.\n\n- **Canonical authorities:** Distributed SQL owns post existence, content version, deletion state, creator privacy, and relationship state. Object storage owns media bytes but grants no access itself. Inbox, recommendation, feature, and response caches are disposable projections. Acknowledged policy mutations must be visible to authorization reads on every later request, across regions.\n- **Ownership keys:** Posts and creator recency indexes use `creator_id`, with time/hash subpartitions for prolific creators; relationships use `(viewer_id, creator_id)`. Candidate inboxes use `viewer_id`; reverse follower indexes use `(creator_id, follower_bucket)` to distribute celebrity workloads. Events carry entity IDs and monotonic versions; consumers reject obsolete updates and retain deletion tombstones through the replay window.\n- **Bounded request path:** Set explicit limits for source calls, creators consulted, candidates scored, authorization batches, refill attempts, and total deadline. Oversample to absorb filtering, then return an underfilled page if necessary. Use an opaque cursor referencing a short-lived ranked candidate snapshot, ranker version, and position; reauthorize every page. New posts enter refreshed sessions.\n- **Bounded background paths:** Deliver fanout in fixed-size, checkpointed batches with idempotent writes, retries, backpressure, and per-creator quotas. Route creators exceeding the fanout budget to pull-based retrieval. Track publish-to-eligibility latency and source completeness; asynchronously purge revoked content, while synchronous authorization supplies correctness.\n- **Permanent components:** Feed API and ranker; canonical database; transactional outbox and event log; projection workers; candidate and recommendation stores; authorization service and media gateway; object storage; observability, replay, and reconciliation. Deployment size changes with growth; these responsibilities remain.",
