      "outputText": "Use a hybrid feed architecture: durably store posts, follows, and visibility policy; publish changes through a transactional outbox; and maintain asynchronous candidate indexes. Fan out post IDs to bounded per-user inboxes for ordinary creators, but pull recent posts from high-fanout creators at read time. Merge those candidates with recommendations, deduplicate, rank, and paginate using a signed cursor containing a ranking-session ID and stable ordering boundary. Cache candidate IDs and ranking features, never authorization decisions. Start with a sharded-capable relational database, durable queue, worker pool, and feed service; partition and scale these components as traffic grows. Ten million daily users alone does not determine capacity: peak requests, follow distributions, posting rates, and geographic requirements remain sizing inputs.\n\nTreat freshness and authorization as separate guarantees. Publish new posts synchronously into a recent-post index before acknowledging creation; use this index as a bounded fallback when asynchronous inbox or recommendation indexing lags, so eligibility does not depend on queue delivery within 30 seconds. Eligibility does not guarantee placement on the first page. Every feed request must bulk-authorize its selected IDs against authoritative, strongly consistent post and relationship policy before returning content, including cached candidates and subsequent pages. Acknowledge deletion or privacy changes only after their authoritative transaction commits; requests beginning afterward must observe that commit or fail closed. Enforce the same policy on content and media access: public caches or long-lived bearer URLs cannot provide this revocation guarantee. Previously downloaded content cannot be recalled.\n\n- **Canonical authorities:** Post store owns content, lifecycle, and visibility; relationship store owns follows, blocks, and private-account grants. The authorization service evaluates these authorities. Inbox entries, recommendation indexes, features, and caches are disposable projections.\n- **Ownership keys:** Posts and recent-post indexes partition by `creator_id`, with `post_id` identifying each post; inboxes and ranking sessions by `viewer_id`; relationship records by their policy owner and counterparty. Queue events carry entity IDs and monotonic versions so retries cannot resurrect deleted or outdated state.\n- **Bounded request path:** Enforce candidate, shard-read, scoring, and authorization budgets. Maintain a per-viewer directory of relevant active creator-index partitions, rather than querying every followed creator. Fetch fixed-size batches, perform limited refill after filtering, and return a shorter page when the budget is exhausted. Reauthorize every page; cursors never grant access.\n- **Bounded background paths:** Fanout jobs process checkpointed follower batches with idempotent writes, bounded concurrency, and per-creator quotas. Switch creators exceeding a measured fanout budget to pull delivery. Update active-creator directories independently of per-post fanout; bound followed accounts per viewer and validate that the fallback can cover this supported limit within its freshness budget. Monitor publication-to-eligibility latency, queue lag, and fallback capacity.\n- **Permanent components:** Authoritative stores and authorization enforcement; transactional outbox and durable event log; recent-post index; inbox and recommendation projections; ranking/pagination service; authenticated media delivery; retry/reconciliation workers; observability and admission control. Deployment count and shard count grow over time without changing these responsibilities.",
