      "outputText": "Use a **hybrid feed with read-time authorization**: asynchronously push post references from ordinary creators into followers’ candidate inboxes, and pull recent posts from high-fanout creators when assembling a page. Combine these with precomputed recommendation candidates, deduplicate, rank, and return a cursor over a short-lived ranked session. Keep inboxes and sessions as references, never embedded post bodies. Commit posts and transactional outbox events together; partitioned workers update creator timelines and candidate indexes, targeting eligibility within 30 seconds. Because queues alone cannot guarantee that deadline, also query a bounded, write-through recent-post index for followed creators; recommendations use an incrementally updated discovery index. Eligibility means a post can enter candidate selection, not that it must appear on a page.\n\nBefore returning content, batch-check every selected reference against a strongly consistent authorization service holding canonical deletion state, creator privacy, and viewer access. Deletion and privacy mutations are acknowledged only after this authority commits; every feed request beginning after that acknowledgment must observe the new policy, including requests using old cursors or cached candidates. Fail closed if authorization cannot be established. Hydrate only authorized posts, and protect media with an access-checking gateway rather than durable public URLs. Start with a replicated relational database, transactional outbox, durable queue, workers, and a disposable cache; retain the same ownership model while partitioning storage and expanding worker and serving capacity. Ten million daily users alone does not determine capacity: peak request rate, follow distribution, posting rate, candidate counts, and ranking cost remain sizing inputs.\n\n- **Canonical authorities:** Post store owns content and lifecycle; authorization store owns deletion tombstones, visibility, and access relationships. An acknowledgment requires authorization state to be committed even if content cleanup is asynchronous. Feed inboxes, search indexes, recommendations, caches, and ranked sessions are derived state.\n- **Ownership keys:** Posts and creator timelines partition by `creator_id`, with globally unique `post_id`; inboxes, recommendation candidates, and sessions by `viewer_id`; access relationships by `(creator_id, viewer_id)`. Events carry aggregate versions so retries are idempotent and stale updates cannot undo deletions.\n- **Bounded request path:** Cap follow count as a product limit; fetch candidates through a bounded number of index shards, with fixed candidate and page budgets. Merge inbox, high-fanout timeline, recent-index, and recommendation results; rank, authorize, and hydrate. Allow at most one bounded refill after filtering, then return a shorter page. Reauthorize on every page; cursors bind viewer, session, and expiry.\n- **Bounded background paths:** Cap push fanout per post; creators exceeding the threshold use pull delivery. Split fanout into resumable follower batches with backpressure, deduplication, and retry limits. Bound candidate retention and recommendation refresh work. Monitor commit-to-index latency against the 30-second requirement; index availability is required to meet it. Cleanup never gates privacy correctness.\n- **Permanent components:** Canonical post/access stores, authorization service, transactional outbox and durable event transport, creator/recent/discovery indexes, candidate workers, feed assembler and ranker, media access gateway, and latency/correctness monitoring. Caches and session snapshots remain replaceable accelerators.",
